      "size": 137,
      "rules": [
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204708",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204708",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000001",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204708",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000001",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204708r1265167_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204708r1265167",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000001-AS-000001",
          "rule_title": "The application server must limit the number of concurrent sessions to an organization-defined number for all accounts and/or account types.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to limit the number of concurrent sessions for all accounts and/or account types to the organization-defined number.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server product documentation and configuration to determine if the number of concurrent sessions can be limited to the organization-defined number of sessions for all accounts and/or account types.\n\nIf a feature to limit the number of concurrent sessions is not available, is not set, or is set to unlimited, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Application management includes the ability to control the number of sessions that utilize an application by all accounts and/or account types. Limiting the number of allowed sessions is helpful in limiting risks related to Denial of Service attacks.\n\nApplication servers host and expose business logic and application processes.\n\nThe application server must possess the capability to limit the maximum number of concurrent sessions in a manner that affects the entire application server or on an individual application basis.\n\nAlthough there is some latitude concerning the settings themselves, the settings should follow DoW-recommended values, but the settings should be configurable to allow for future DoW direction.\n\nWhile the DoW will specify recommended values, the values can be adjusted to accommodate the operational requirement of a given system.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46335",
            "V-35070"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000054"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "46f50a7e-7bde-5e90-8302-f76594d83f07",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The actual release delegates sessions to UDS Keycloak, not bundled Keycloak. Core adds an enforcing user-session-limits authenticator, but its realm default is explicitly0/unlimited. The official check expressly treats unlimited as a finding, so this is a provider configuration gap, not API code work. The session limiter is implemented by Core; only Gallatin policy value and applied operator configuration remain. Human review on 2026-11-10 authorized applying the earlier non-platform decision to matching counterparts. Keep Needs work and share V-222387 verification/remediation where it covers this component. Preserve the exact component scope and applicability rather than treating a shared control as automatic compliance.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Share V-222387 concurrent-session policy work: one concurrent session per user is proposed, not yet approved or applied. Confirm the organization-defined limits and account types for this application-server scope, verify current effective limits and whether the same shared IdP control covers them, then configure missing approved limits. Preserve justified service-account distinctions and verify limit enforcement; do not apply a human-browser maximum blindly to all service connections.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-coreidentity] re-appserver-coreidentity\nCore 1.14 default enables OTP and X509, sets concurrent per-user limit0(unlimited), leaves inactivity workflow opt-in, includes explicit truststorepaths and optional alert enablement.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-init-only] mpn-identity-init-only\nrealmInitEnv and realmAuthFlows are initial realm import inputs. Existing realm is persisted in DB; redeploying Secret does not change it. Core documents equivalent Keycloak AdminConsole settings. Use controlled non-destructive AdminAPI/console update and persist the desired bundle override; do not advise tearing down production identity.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-keycloak-login-policies.mdx\n\n[mpn-identity-realm] mpn-identity-realm\nKnowndefaults: idle600sec,maxlifespan36000sec; sessionlimit0; permanentlockout threshold3; default passwords length15/classes/history5/expiry60. CRL cachechecking disabled, OCSP checking enabled/failclosed; no eightcharacter-distance/24h minchange interval/selecteddenylist. Explicit source gaps are config/tailoring work, not Unknown.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[mpn-core-known-identity-settings] mpn-core-known-identity-settings\nKnown Core settings: realmInitEnv.TERMS_AND_CONDITIONS_ENABLED and themeCustomizations.termsAndConditions.text.inline/configmap; realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER (default0 unlimited); realmInitEnv.X509_CRL_CHECKING_ENABLED/RELATIVE_PATH/ABORT_IF_NON_UPDATED; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled(defaultfalse), logging.enabled/defaulttrue. These are Gallatin configuration choices.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "The actual release delegates sessions to UDS Keycloak, not bundled Keycloak. Core adds an enforcing user-session-limits authenticator, but its realm default is explicitly0/unlimited. The official check expressly treats unlimited as a finding, so this is a provider configuration gap, not API code work. The session limiter is implemented by Core; only Gallatin policy value and applied operator configuration remain. Human review on 2026-11-10 authorized applying the earlier non-platform decision to matching counterparts. Keep Needs work and share V-222387 verification/remediation where it covers this component. Preserve the exact component scope and applicability rather than treating a shared control as automatic compliance.\n\nNext action: Share V-222387 concurrent-session policy work: one concurrent session per user is proposed, not yet approved or applied. Confirm the organization-defined limits and account types for this application-server scope, verify current effective limits and whether the same shared IdP control covers them, then configure missing approved limits. Preserve justified service-account distinctions and verify limit enforcement; do not apply a human-browser maximum blindly to all service connections."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204709",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204709",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000014",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204709",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000014",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204709r960759_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204709r960759",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000014-AS-000009",
          "rule_title": "The application server must use encryption strength in accordance with the categorization of the management data during remote access management sessions.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to use encryption strength in accordance with the categorization of the management data during remote access management sessions.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Check the application server configuration to ensure all management interfaces use encryption in accordance with the management data.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to encrypt remote access management sessions in accordance with the categorization of the management data, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Remote management access is accomplished by leveraging common communication protocols and establishing a remote connection to the application server via a network for the purposes of managing the application server. If cryptography is not used, then the session data traversing the remote connection could be intercepted and compromised. \n\nTypes of management interfaces utilized by an application server include web-based HTTPS interfaces as well as command line-based management interfaces.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46376",
            "V-35089"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000068"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "eb4dd759-8613-5ee0-9082-d3646c45dd17",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS gateway enforces HTTPS/TLS1.3-or-1.2 and root STRICT mTLS protects the enrolled workload hops; Horizon opts into ambient mesh. This supports source inference of transit protection, with approved-crypto module claims assessed separately.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[core-gateway] core-gateway\nGateway redirects HTTP to HTTPS and requires TLS 1.3 by default, optionally TLS 1.2; credentialName references Kubernetes TLS secrets.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/charts/uds-istio-config/templates/gateway.yaml\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS gateway enforces HTTPS/TLS1.3-or-1.2 and root STRICT mTLS protects the enrolled workload hops; Horizon opts into ambient mesh. This supports source inference of transit protection, with approved-crypto module claims assessed separately.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204710",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204710",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000015",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204710",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000015",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204710r960762_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204710r960762",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000015-AS-000010",
          "rule_title": "The application server must implement cryptography mechanisms to protect the integrity of the remote access session.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to implement cryptography mechanisms to protect the integrity of the remote access session.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and configuration to ensure the application server is configured to use cryptography to protect the integrity of remote access sessions.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to implement cryptography mechanisms to protect the integrity of remote access sessions, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Encryption is critical for protection of remote access sessions. If encryption is not being used for integrity, malicious users may gain the ability to modify the application server configuration. The use of cryptography for ensuring integrity of remote access sessions mitigates that risk.\n\nApplication servers utilize a web management interface and scripted commands when allowing remote access. Web access requires the use of TLS and scripted access requires using ssh or some other form of approved cryptography. Application servers must have a capability to enable a secure remote admin capability.\n\nFIPS 140-2 approved TLS versions must be enabled and non-FIPS-approved SSL versions must be disabled.\n\nNIST SP 800-52 specifies the preferred configurations for government systems.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46377",
            "V-35090"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001453"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "1f9697d5-3174-5f8d-baca-57333e11fcad",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS gateway enforces HTTPS/TLS1.3-or-1.2 and root STRICT mTLS protects the enrolled workload hops; Horizon opts into ambient mesh. This supports source inference of transit protection, with approved-crypto module claims assessed separately.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[core-gateway] core-gateway\nGateway redirects HTTP to HTTPS and requires TLS 1.3 by default, optionally TLS 1.2; credentialName references Kubernetes TLS secrets.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/charts/uds-istio-config/templates/gateway.yaml\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS gateway enforces HTTPS/TLS1.3-or-1.2 and root STRICT mTLS protects the enrolled workload hops; Horizon opts into ambient mesh. This supports source inference of transit protection, with approved-crypto module claims assessed separately.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204711",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204711",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000016",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204711",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000016",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204711r960765_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204711r960765",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000016-AS-000013",
          "rule_title": "The application server must ensure remote sessions for accessing security functions and security-relevant information are logged.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to log an event for each instance when the administrator accesses the system remotely.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server product documentation to determine if the application server logs remote administrative sessions.\n\nIf the application server does not log remote sessions for the admin user, then this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Logging must be utilized in order to track system activity, assist in diagnosing system issues, and provide evidence needed for forensic investigations post security incident.\n\nRemote access by administrators requires that the admin activity be logged.\n\nApplication servers provide a web and command line-based remote management capability for managing the application server. Application servers must ensure that all actions related to administrative functionality such as application server configuration are logged.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71683",
            "V-57411"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000067"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "43d8bc3f-796b-5d77-b320-11c69e19ee49",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The delivered release uses UDS identity. Its realm enables user/admin events, and the JSON listener emits original event type, actor/session/source details and UTC time to stdout, which the existing Vector/Loki path collects. API request/process events and platform lifecycle records provide their respective access/startup/shutdown sources. There is no bundled-Keycloak event-store forwarding dependency in this package.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-idlistener] re-appserver-idlistener\nActual UDS identity listener handles both UserEvent and AdminEvent, serializes original event fields plus event type and UTC timestamp, and prints JSON to stdout. Vector can collect the actual provider records directly; no bundled identity database forwarder is needed.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[re-appserver-logging] re-appserver-logging\nVector collects pod/node records with namespace/app/container/host identifiers, acknowledged diskbuffers and Loki sinks, exports internal metrics and is monitored. It collects process/container identity independent of Horizonrequest actors.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-logger] re-appserver-logger\nPino records stable serviceidentity, trace/requestIDs, requestmethod/URL and outcome, with secretredaction. Its commonbindings do not attach authenticated userId; not every per-domain failure can be assigned to a user from that record alone.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/observability/logging.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The delivered release uses UDS identity. Its realm enables user/admin events, and the JSON listener emits original event type, actor/session/source details and UTC time to stdout, which the existing Vector/Loki path collects. API request/process events and platform lifecycle records provide their respective access/startup/shutdown sources. There is no bundled-Keycloak event-store forwarding dependency in this package.\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204712",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204712",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000033",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204712",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000033",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204712r1137578_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204712r1137578",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000033-AS-000024",
          "rule_title": "The application server must enforce approved authorizations for logical access to information and system resources in accordance with applicable access control policies.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to enforce access control policies for logical access to the system in accordance with applicable policy.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server product documentation and configuration to determine if the system enforces authorization requirements for logical access to the system in accordance with applicable policy.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to utilize access controls or follow access control policies, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Strong access controls are critical to securing the application server. Access control policies (e.g., identity-based policies, role-based policies, attribute-based policies) and access enforcement mechanisms (e.g., access control lists, access control matrices, cryptography) must be employed by the application server to control access between users (or processes acting on behalf of users) and objects (e.g., applications, files, records, processes, application domains) in the application server.\n\nWithout stringent logical access and authorization controls, an adversary may have the ability, with very little effort, to compromise the application server and associated supporting infrastructure.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-47025",
            "V-35738"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000213"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "69e75520-f791-5841-9118-9825d50ecb58",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Current global authentication plus per-route permission enforcement consults the authorization model and fails closed if user/target/authorization resolution fails. Schema/route coverage checks constrain omitted metadata; preserve negative role/tenant tests for the release.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[authz] Application access control\nAPI guard validates identity and sets tenant/user context; permission guard invokes a permission enforcer when route metadata is present. This is source support, not complete route/tenant coverage.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[app-enforcer] app-enforcer\nPermission enforcement resolves user/target and fails closed on missing authorization or unexpected resolution errors. Individual route metadata remains part of coverage.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/core/authorization/permission-enforcer.service.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Current global authentication plus per-route permission enforcement consults the authorization model and fails closed if user/target/authorization resolution fails. Schema/route coverage checks constrain omitted metadata; preserve negative role/tenant tests for the release.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204713",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204713",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000068",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204713",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000068",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204713r960843_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204713r960843",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000068-AS-000035",
          "rule_title": "The application server management interface must display the Standard Mandatory DoD Notice and Consent Banner before granting access to the system.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server management interface so it displays the Standard Mandatory DoD Notice and Consent Banner prior to allowing access.\n\nThe banner must read:\n\n\"You are accessing a U.S. Government (USG) Information System (IS) that is provided for USG-authorized use only.\nBy using this IS (which includes any device attached to this IS), you consent to the following conditions:\n-The USG routinely intercepts and monitors communications on this IS for purposes including, but not limited to, penetration testing, COMSEC monitoring, network operations and defense, personnel misconduct (PM), law enforcement (LE), and counterintelligence (CI) investigations.\n-At any time, the USG may inspect and seize data stored on this IS.\n-Communications using, or data stored on, this IS are not private, are subject to routine monitoring, interception, and search, and may be disclosed or used for any USG-authorized purpose.\n-This IS includes security measures (e.g., authentication and access controls) to protect USG interests--not for your personal benefit or privacy.\n-Notwithstanding the above, using this IS does not constitute consent to PM, LE or CI investigative searching or monitoring of the content of privileged communications, or work product, related to personal representation or services by attorneys, psychotherapists, or clergy, and their assistants. Such communications and work product are private and confidential. See User Agreement for details.\"",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server management interface configuration to verify the application server is configured to display the Standard Mandatory DoD Notice and Consent Banner before granting access.\n\nThe banner must read:\n\n\"You are accessing a U.S. Government (USG) Information System (IS) that is provided for USG-authorized use only.\nBy using this IS (which includes any device attached to this IS), you consent to the following conditions:\n-The USG routinely intercepts and monitors communications on this IS for purposes including, but not limited to, penetration testing, COMSEC monitoring, network operations and defense, personnel misconduct (PM), law enforcement (LE), and counterintelligence (CI) investigations.\n-At any time, the USG may inspect and seize data stored on this IS.\n-Communications using, or data stored on, this IS are not private, are subject to routine monitoring, interception, and search, and may be disclosed or used for any USG-authorized purpose.\n-This IS includes security measures (e.g., authentication and access controls) to protect USG interests--not for your personal benefit or privacy.\n-Notwithstanding the above, using this IS does not constitute consent to PM, LE or CI investigative searching or monitoring of the content of privileged communications, or work product, related to personal representation or services by attorneys, psychotherapists, or clergy, and their assistants. Such communications and work product are private and confidential. See User Agreement for details.\"\n\nIf the application server management interface does not display the banner or displays an unapproved banner, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Application servers are required to display the Standard Mandatory DoD Notice and Consent Banner before granting access to the system management interface, providing privacy and security notices consistent with applicable federal laws, Executive Orders, directives, policies, regulations, standards, and guidance that states that: \n\n(i) users are accessing a U.S. Government information system; \n(ii) system usage may be monitored, recorded, and subject to audit; \n(iii) unauthorized use of the system is prohibited and subject to criminal and civil penalties; and \n(iv) the use of the system indicates consent to monitoring and recording.\n\nSystem use notification messages can be implemented in the form of warning banners displayed when individuals log on to the information system. \n\nSystem use notification is intended only for information system access including an interactive logon interface with a human user, and is not required when an interactive interface does not exist. \n\nUse this banner for desktops, laptops, and other devices accommodating banners of 1300 characters. The banner shall be implemented as a click-through banner at logon (to the extent permitted by the operating system), meaning it prevents further activity on the information system unless and until the user executes a positive action to manifest agreement by clicking on a box indicating \"OK\".\n\n\"You are accessing a U.S. Government (USG) Information System (IS) that is provided for USG-authorized use only.\nBy using this IS (which includes any device attached to this IS), you consent to the following conditions:\n-The USG routinely intercepts and monitors communications on this IS for purposes including, but not limited to, penetration testing, COMSEC monitoring, network operations and defense, personnel misconduct (PM), law enforcement (LE), and counterintelligence (CI) investigations.\n-At any time, the USG may inspect and seize data stored on this IS.\n-Communications using, or data stored on, this IS are not private, are subject to routine monitoring, interception, and search, and may be disclosed or used for any USG-authorized purpose.\n-This IS includes security measures (e.g., authentication and access controls) to protect USG interests--not for your personal benefit or privacy.\n-Notwithstanding the above, using this IS does not constitute consent to PM, LE or CI investigative searching or monitoring of the content of privileged communications, or work product, related to personal representation or services by attorneys, psychotherapists, or clergy, and their assistants. Such communications and work product are private and confidential. See User Agreement for details.\"",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46383",
            "V-35096"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000048"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "a113384c-1199-5559-8b12-18ad5b7ac972",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS owns authentication and already supplies the complete mandatory USGnotice plus acknowledgement UI. However its TERMS_AND_CONDITIONS required action is disabled by default and the release profile supplies no override. No custom API/banner implementation is needed; enable the existing provider action. Core ships the USG notice and acknowledgement UI; its terms action defaults off. Gallatin owns enabling the existing control. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the same decision to similar notice/consent banner requirements. Share the existing V-222434/V-222435 configuration and verification actions, but verify the exact component and management entry points; Horizon login alone does not establish every platform entry path.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Verify current UDS notice/consent settings and inventory applicable interactive entry points for this exact component or management interface. Reuse V-222434/V-222435 configuration: persist the approved Core terms setting and update the existing realm through a controlled change if needed. Verify the required banner text and display before access. Test each applicable entry path, retain configuration/results and credit existing coverage. Do not create a duplicate Horizon banner or assume one login test covers all interfaces.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-coreidentity] re-appserver-coreidentity\nCore 1.14 default enables OTP and X509, sets concurrent per-user limit0(unlimited), leaves inactivity workflow opt-in, includes explicit truststorepaths and optional alert enablement.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-terms] re-appserver-terms\nUDS theme includes mandatory USG notice and explicit acknowledgement UI, but this template is reached only when the terms required action is enabled.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/theme/login/terms.ftl\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-banner] mpn-identity-banner\nShipped theme already contains USG InformationSystem notice/consent text and acknowledgementUI. Enable realmInitEnv.TERMS_AND_CONDITIONS_ENABLED=true; review mission applicability rather than build another banner.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/theme/login/terms.ftl\n\n[mpn-identity-init-only] mpn-identity-init-only\nrealmInitEnv and realmAuthFlows are initial realm import inputs. Existing realm is persisted in DB; redeploying Secret does not change it. Core documents equivalent Keycloak AdminConsole settings. Use controlled non-destructive AdminAPI/console update and persist the desired bundle override; do not advise tearing down production identity.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-keycloak-login-policies.mdx\n\n[mpn-core-known-identity-settings] mpn-core-known-identity-settings\nKnown Core settings: realmInitEnv.TERMS_AND_CONDITIONS_ENABLED and themeCustomizations.termsAndConditions.text.inline/configmap; realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER (default0 unlimited); realmInitEnv.X509_CRL_CHECKING_ENABLED/RELATIVE_PATH/ABORT_IF_NON_UPDATED; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled(defaultfalse), logging.enabled/defaulttrue. These are Gallatin configuration choices.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "UDS owns authentication and already supplies the complete mandatory USGnotice plus acknowledgement UI. However its TERMS_AND_CONDITIONS required action is disabled by default and the release profile supplies no override. No custom API/banner implementation is needed; enable the existing provider action. Core ships the USG notice and acknowledgement UI; its terms action defaults off. Gallatin owns enabling the existing control. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the same decision to similar notice/consent banner requirements. Share the existing V-222434/V-222435 configuration and verification actions, but verify the exact component and management entry points; Horizon login alone does not establish every platform entry path.\n\nNext action: Verify current UDS notice/consent settings and inventory applicable interactive entry points for this exact component or management interface. Reuse V-222434/V-222435 configuration: persist the approved Core terms setting and update the existing realm through a controlled change if needed. Verify the required banner text and display before access. Test each applicable entry path, retain configuration/results and credit existing coverage. Do not create a duplicate Horizon banner or assume one login test covers all interfaces."
        },
