        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204715",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204715",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000080",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204715",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000080",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204715r960864_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204715r960864",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000080-AS-000045",
          "rule_title": "The application server must protect against an individual (or process acting on behalf of an individual) falsely denying having performed organization-defined actions to be covered by non-repudiation.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to protect against an individual's (or process acting on behalf of an individual) falsely denying having performed organization-defined actions to be covered by non-repudiation. \n",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server product documentation and server configuration to determine if the system does protect against an individual's (or process acting on behalf of an individual) falsely denying having performed organization-defined actions to be covered by non-repudiation.\n\nIf the application does not meet this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Non-repudiation of actions taken is required in order to maintain application integrity. Examples of particular actions taken by individuals include creating information, sending a message, approving information (e.g., indicating concurrence or signing a contract), and receiving a message. \n\nNon-repudiation protects individuals against later claims by an author of not having authored a particular document, a sender of not having transmitted a message, a receiver of not having received a message, or a signatory of not having signed a document. \n\nTypical application server actions requiring non-repudiation will be related to application deployment among developers/users and administrative actions taken by admin personnel.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46422",
            "V-35135"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000166"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "92ff18c2-5981-58cd-b30c-11168246e2cb",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The required outcome is supplied jointly: UDS provides individual authenticated identity; protected server/process logs are centralized separately; append-only permission audit records bind privileged role changes to actor, subject, tenant, action and time. A database-backed audit record is valid evidence; everyAPI response does not need a separate nonrepudiation subsystem. The approved organization event set still bounds final acceptance.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-auth] re-appserver-auth\nEvery protected request validates OIDC claims and single tenantgroup, resolves stable sub->localuser/tenant, and sets trusted userId/tenantId CLS context. Pending/no-role users are denied.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[re-appserver-roleaudit] re-appserver-roleaudit\nRole grant/revocation writes an actor+subject+tenant+action+role audit record, transactionally coupled with mutation. Failed preconditions throw before insert; failed transaction paths do not retain successful audit rows.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/users/user-roles.service.ts\n\n[re-appserver-auditfields] re-appserver-auditfields\nAppendonly permission audit table records actor_user_id, subject_user_id, tenant_id, action, role and timestamp. This is valid audit evidence even though it is a database record rather than Pino JSON.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl\n\n[re-appserver-logging] re-appserver-logging\nVector collects pod/node records with namespace/app/container/host identifiers, acknowledged diskbuffers and Loki sinks, exports internal metrics and is monitored. It collects process/container identity independent of Horizonrequest actors.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-logaccess] re-appserver-logaccess\nGrafana local login disabled; UDSgroups map Admin/Auditor to Admin/Viewer and unmatched groups are rejected. Runtime log configuration is Kubernetes-managed outside application users.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The required outcome is supplied jointly: UDS provides individual authenticated identity; protected server/process logs are centralized separately; append-only permission audit records bind privileged role changes to actor, subject, tenant, action and time. A database-backed audit record is valid evidence; everyAPI response does not need a separate nonrepudiation subsystem. The approved organization event set still bounds final acceptance.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204716",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204716",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000086",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204716",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000086",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204716r1265168_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204716r1265168",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000086-AS-000048",
          "rule_title": "For application servers providing log record aggregation, the application server must compile log records from organization-defined information system components into a system-wide log trail that is time-correlated with an organization-defined level of tolerance for the relationship between time stamps of individual records in the log trail.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server or an external logging tool supporting the application server to compile log records from multiple components within the server into a system-wide log trail that is time-correlated with an organization-defined level of tolerance for the relationship between time stamps of individual records in the log trail.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server log feature configuration to determine if the application server or an external logging tool in conjunction with the application server does compile log records from multiple components within the server into a system-wide log trail that is time-correlated with an organization-defined level of tolerance for the relationship between time stamps of individual records in the log trail.\n\nIf the application server does not meet this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Log generation and log records can be generated from various components within the application server. The list of logged events is the set of events for which logs are to be generated. This set of events is typically a subset of the list of all events for which the system is capable of generating log records (e.g., logable events, time stamps, source and destination addresses, user/process identifiers, event descriptions, success/fail indications, filenames involved, and access control or flow control rules invoked).\n\nThe events occurring must be time-correlated in order to conduct accurate forensic analysis. In addition, the correlation must meet certain tolerance criteria. For instance, DoW may define that the time stamps of different logged events must not differ by any amount greater than ten seconds. It is also acceptable for the application server to utilize an external logging tool that provides this capability.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35139",
            "SV-46426"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000174"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "a6e591a7-ad82-51c2-a028-fc9131805933",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Application log aggregation is explicitly delegated to UDS Vector/Loki, which labels and combines records across components; host-derived/UTC timestamps provide a concrete correlation mechanism. Define any required tighter clock tolerance as a host acceptance parameter.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-id-audit] core-id-audit\nUDS event listener prints user/admin events to stdout with event fields and UTC timestamps. Vector can collect these without a separately configured app forwarding agent.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Application log aggregation is explicitly delegated to UDS Vector/Loki, which labels and combines records across components; host-derived/UTC timestamps provide a concrete correlation mechanism. Define any required tighter clock tolerance as a host acceptance parameter.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204717",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204717",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000089",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204717",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000089",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204717r960879_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204717r960879",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000089-AS-000050",
          "rule_title": "The application server must generate log records for access and authentication events.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to generate log records for system startup and shutdown, system access, and system authentication events.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and the deployed system configuration to determine if, at a minimum, system startup and shutdown, system access, and system authentication events are logged.\n\nIf the logs do not include the minimum logable events, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Log records can be generated from various components within the application server.  From an application server perspective, certain specific application server functionalities may be logged as well.  The application server must allow the definition of what events are to be logged.  As conditions change, the number and types of events to be logged may change, and the application server must be able to facilitate these changes.\n\nThe minimum list of logged events should be those pertaining to system startup and shutdown, system access, and system authentication events.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35141",
            "SV-46428"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000169"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "5d085566-118b-50be-a92c-b07a54d95541",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The delivered release uses UDS identity. Its realm enables user/admin events, and the JSON listener emits original event type, actor/session/source details and UTC time to stdout, which the existing Vector/Loki path collects. API request/process events and platform lifecycle records provide their respective access/startup/shutdown sources. There is no bundled-Keycloak event-store forwarding dependency in this package.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-idlistener] re-appserver-idlistener\nActual UDS identity listener handles both UserEvent and AdminEvent, serializes original event fields plus event type and UTC timestamp, and prints JSON to stdout. Vector can collect the actual provider records directly; no bundled identity database forwarder is needed.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[re-appserver-logging] re-appserver-logging\nVector collects pod/node records with namespace/app/container/host identifiers, acknowledged diskbuffers and Loki sinks, exports internal metrics and is monitored. It collects process/container identity independent of Horizonrequest actors.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-logger] re-appserver-logger\nPino records stable serviceidentity, trace/requestIDs, requestmethod/URL and outcome, with secretredaction. Its commonbindings do not attach authenticated userId; not every per-domain failure can be assigned to a user from that record alone.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/observability/logging.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The delivered release uses UDS identity. Its realm enables user/admin events, and the JSON listener emits original event type, actor/session/source details and UTC time to stdout, which the existing Vector/Loki path collects. API request/process events and platform lifecycle records provide their respective access/startup/shutdown sources. There is no bundled-Keycloak event-store forwarding dependency in this package.\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204718",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204718",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000090",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204718",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000090",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204718r960882_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204718r960882",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000090-AS-000051",
          "rule_title": "The application server must allow only the ISSM (or individuals or roles appointed by the ISSM) to select which logable events are to be logged.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to only allow the ISSM (or individuals or roles appointed by the ISSM) to change logable events.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server product documentation and configuration to determine if the system only allows the ISSM (or individuals or roles appointed by the ISSM) to change logable events.\n\nIf the system is not configured to perform this function, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Log records can be generated from various components within the application server, (e.g., httpd, beans, etc.) From an application perspective, certain specific application functionalities may be logged, as well.\n\nThe list of logged events is the set of events for which logs are to be generated. This set of events is typically a subset of the list of all events for which the system is capable of generating log records (e.g., logable events, time stamps, source and destination addresses, user/process identifiers, event descriptions, success/fail indications, filenames involved, and access control or flow control rules invoked).\n\n\nApplication servers utilize role-based access controls in order to specify the individuals who are allowed to configure application component logable events. The application server must be configured to select which personnel are assigned the role of selecting which logable events are to be logged.\n\nThe personnel or roles that can select logable events are only the ISSM (or individuals or roles appointed by the ISSM).",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35142",
            "SV-46429"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000171"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "67647ddd-02bc-56df-ad75-64bcf4d11157",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Logging selection is privileged Kubernetes/UDS configuration rather than an ordinary application user setting. Grafana rejects unmatched users and separates Admin/Auditor roles; application containers cannot modify host collector configuration. This supplies the ISSM-designated admin role mechanism; named role assignment belongs in the inheritance record, not API code.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-logaccess] re-appserver-logaccess\nGrafana local login disabled; UDSgroups map Admin/Auditor to Admin/Viewer and unmatched groups are rejected. Runtime log configuration is Kubernetes-managed outside application users.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-logging] re-appserver-logging\nVector collects pod/node records with namespace/app/container/host identifiers, acknowledged diskbuffers and Loki sinks, exports internal metrics and is monitored. It collects process/container identity independent of Horizonrequest actors.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-podsecurity] re-appserver-podsecurity\nAdmission restricts privileges/non-root/escalation/capabilities with explicitexemptions. This supplies secureapplication server operation and a preventionmechanism, but does not itself integrate Falcointrusionevents into accesscontrol.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Logging selection is privileged Kubernetes/UDS configuration rather than an ordinary application user setting. Grafana rejects unmatched users and separates Admin/Auditor roles; application containers cannot modify host collector configuration. This supplies the ISSM-designated admin role mechanism; named role assignment belongs in the inheritance record, not API code.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204719",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204719",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000091",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204719",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000091",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204719r960885_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204719r960885",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000091-AS-000052",
          "rule_title": "The application server must generate log records when successful/unsuccessful attempts to access subject privileges occur.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to generate log records when privileges are successfully/unsuccessfully accessed.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and the system configuration to determine if the application server generates log records when successful/unsuccessful attempts are made to access privileges.\n\nIf log records are not generated, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Accessing a subject's privileges can be used to elevate a lower-privileged subject's privileges temporarily in order to cause harm to the application server or to gain privileges to operate temporarily for a designed purpose.  When these actions take place, the event needs to be logged.\n\nApplication servers either provide a local user store, or they integrate with enterprise user stores like LDAP.  When the application server provides the user store and enforces authentication, the application server must generate a log record when modification of privileges is successfully or unsuccessfully performed.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35143",
            "SV-46430"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000172"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "206a7c12-5aff-54c0-b5c7-170de6860aff",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Audit coverage is partially implemented. Successful grant/revoke operations already have append-only actor-attributed permission audit records and IdP admin actions have separate UDS logging. The remaining concrete application-domain gap is unsuccessful/denied privilegeaccess or mutation: the guard returns403 without recording actor, while transaction/precondition failures can occur before audit insert or roll it back. Generic HTTPstatus records do not recover that missing actor. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the partially implemented recommendation to similar audit-coverage requirements. Confirm required controls, credit existing coverage and implement only the missing required event coverage; this is not a final compliance finding.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Add narrowly-scoped actor-attributed denied/failed privilege events and forward them through the existing logging path; reuse existing successful audit. As part of the shared V-222431 audit-control review, confirm the required event catalog for this exact requirement, map existing coverage, implement missing desired controls consistent with the STIG minimum, and retain verification evidence. Preserve scoped applicability decisions for nonexistent operations.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-roleaudit] re-appserver-roleaudit\nRole grant/revocation writes an actor+subject+tenant+action+role audit record, transactionally coupled with mutation. Failed preconditions throw before insert; failed transaction paths do not retain successful audit rows.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/users/user-roles.service.ts\n\n[re-appserver-auditfields] re-appserver-auditfields\nAppendonly permission audit table records actor_user_id, subject_user_id, tenant_id, action, role and timestamp. This is valid audit evidence even though it is a database record rather than Pino JSON.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl\n\n[re-appserver-denials] re-appserver-denials\nDeniedpermissions return403; unexpected resolutionfailures log permission/error but do not attach actor. Source distinguishes missing denied-domain audit from existing successful role change audit.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/authorization/permission-enforcer.service.ts\n\n[re-appserver-logger] re-appserver-logger\nPino records stable serviceidentity, trace/requestIDs, requestmethod/URL and outcome, with secretredaction. Its commonbindings do not attach authenticated userId; not every per-domain failure can be assigned to a user from that record alone.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/observability/logging.ts",
          "finding_details": "Audit coverage is partially implemented. Successful grant/revoke operations already have append-only actor-attributed permission audit records and IdP admin actions have separate UDS logging. The remaining concrete application-domain gap is unsuccessful/denied privilegeaccess or mutation: the guard returns403 without recording actor, while transaction/precondition failures can occur before audit insert or roll it back. Generic HTTPstatus records do not recover that missing actor. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the partially implemented recommendation to similar audit-coverage requirements. Confirm required controls, credit existing coverage and implement only the missing required event coverage; this is not a final compliance finding.\n\nNext action: Add narrowly-scoped actor-attributed denied/failed privilege events and forward them through the existing logging path; reuse existing successful audit. As part of the shared V-222431 audit-control review, confirm the required event catalog for this exact requirement, map existing coverage, implement missing desired controls consistent with the STIG minimum, and retain verification evidence. Preserve scoped applicability decisions for nonexistent operations."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204720",
