        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204724",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204724",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000098",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204724",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000098",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204724r960900_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204724r960900",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000098-AS-000061",
          "rule_title": "The application server must produce log records containing sufficient information to establish the sources of the events.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to generate the source of each logable event.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and deployment configuration to determine if the application server is configured to generate sufficient information to resolve the source, e.g., source IP, of the log event.\n\nRequest a user access the application server and generate logable events, and then review the logs to determine if the source of the event can be established.\n\nIf the source of the event cannot be determined, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Application server logging capability is critical for accurate forensic analysis.  Without sufficient and accurate information, a correct replay of the events cannot be determined.\n\nAscertaining the correct source, e.g., source IP, of the events is important during forensic analysis.  Correctly determining the source will add information to the overall reconstruction of the logable event.  By determining the source of the event correctly, analysis of the enterprise can be undertaken to determine if the event compromised other assets within the enterprise.\n\nWithout sufficient information establishing the source of the logged event, investigation into the cause of event is severely hindered.  Log record content that may be necessary to satisfy the requirement of this control includes, but is not limited to, time stamps, source and destination IP addresses, user/process identifiers, event descriptions, application-specific events, success/fail indications, file names involved, access control, or flow control rules invoked.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35170",
            "SV-46457"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000133"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "73160679-9efc-582c-b563-255f535a38cd",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: nginx/Istio emit access records to stdout, current API structured logging records event/method/path/source/status with timestamps, and Vector continuously aggregates pod/node records in Loki. This supports the specified timing/location/outcome/collection mechanics, not complete security-event or actor coverage.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-web] app-web\nnginx serves a static SPA at /usr/share/nginx/html on port 3000, with try_files fallback, stdout/stderr logs and no configured proxy_pass, CGI or WebDAV locations.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/web/nginx.conf\n\n[core-accesslog] core-accesslog\nIstio access logs go to stdout; path normalization merges slashes, proxies are held until ready, and forwarded certificate details are sanitized.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/values/base-istiod.yaml\n\n[logging] API logging\nCurrent HTTP logger includes service, request/trace IDs, event, remote address and response status, with secret redaction. Its common bindings do not add authenticated actor identity; other records/correlation must be checked.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/core/observability/logging.ts\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. nginx/Istio emit access records to stdout, current API structured logging records event/method/path/source/status with timestamps, and Vector continuously aggregates pod/node records in Loki. This supports the specified timing/location/outcome/collection mechanics, not complete security-event or actor coverage.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204725",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204725",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000099",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204725",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000099",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204725r960903_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204725r960903",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000099-AS-000062",
          "rule_title": "The application server must produce log records that contain sufficient information to establish the outcome of events.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server logging system to log the event outcome.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation and the log files on the application server to determine if the logs contain information that establishes the outcome of event data.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to meet this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Information system logging capability is critical for accurate forensic analysis. Log record content that may be necessary to satisfy the requirement of this control includes, but is not limited to, time stamps, source and destination IP addresses, user/process identifiers, event descriptions, application-specific events, success/fail indications, filenames involved, access control or flow control rules invoked.\n\nSuccess and failure indicators ascertain the outcome of a particular application server event or function. As such, they also provide a means to measure the impact of an event and help authorized personnel to determine the appropriate response.  Event outcome may also include event-specific results (e.g., the security state of the information system after the event occurred).",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35176",
            "SV-46463"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000134"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "098272f1-81f2-59c3-8eca-95f50c54d14f",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: nginx/Istio emit access records to stdout, current API structured logging records event/method/path/source/status with timestamps, and Vector continuously aggregates pod/node records in Loki. This supports the specified timing/location/outcome/collection mechanics, not complete security-event or actor coverage.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-web] app-web\nnginx serves a static SPA at /usr/share/nginx/html on port 3000, with try_files fallback, stdout/stderr logs and no configured proxy_pass, CGI or WebDAV locations.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/web/nginx.conf\n\n[core-accesslog] core-accesslog\nIstio access logs go to stdout; path normalization merges slashes, proxies are held until ready, and forwarded certificate details are sanitized.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/values/base-istiod.yaml\n\n[logging] API logging\nCurrent HTTP logger includes service, request/trace IDs, event, remote address and response status, with secret redaction. Its common bindings do not add authenticated actor identity; other records/correlation must be checked.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/core/observability/logging.ts\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. nginx/Istio emit access records to stdout, current API structured logging records event/method/path/source/status with timestamps, and Vector continuously aggregates pod/node records in Loki. This supports the specified timing/location/outcome/collection mechanics, not complete security-event or actor coverage.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204726",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204726",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000100",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204726",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000100",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204726r960906_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204726r960906",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000100-AS-000063",
          "rule_title": "The application server must generate log records containing information that establishes the identity of any individual or process associated with the event.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server logging system to log the identity of the user or process related to the events.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation and the log files on the application server to determine if the logs contain information that establishes the identity of the user or process associated with log event data.\n\nIf the application server does not produce logs that establish the identity of the user or process associated with log event data, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Information system logging capability is critical for accurate forensic analysis. Log record content that may be necessary to satisfy the requirement of this control includes: time stamps, source and destination addresses, user/process identifiers, event descriptions, success/fail indications, filenames involved, and access control or flow control rules invoked. \n\nApplication servers have differing levels of logging capabilities that can be specified by setting a verbosity level. The application server must, at a minimum, be capable of establishing the identity of any user or process that is associated with any particular event.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35182",
            "SV-46469"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001487"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "87e75349-8e25-5d21-901b-d703c22d3338",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS Vector labels process/container/service identity, UDS identity events record authenticatedactors, and applicationprivilegechanges record explicit actor_user_id. The literal requirement accepts identity of the individual OR process associated with the event; requiring userId on everygenericprocess log would overstate this control. Domain-specific failed privilege events remain separately flagged.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-logging] re-appserver-logging\nVector collects pod/node records with namespace/app/container/host identifiers, acknowledged diskbuffers and Loki sinks, exports internal metrics and is monitored. It collects process/container identity independent of Horizonrequest actors.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-logger] re-appserver-logger\nPino records stable serviceidentity, trace/requestIDs, requestmethod/URL and outcome, with secretredaction. Its commonbindings do not attach authenticated userId; not every per-domain failure can be assigned to a user from that record alone.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/observability/logging.ts\n\n[re-appserver-auditfields] re-appserver-auditfields\nAppendonly permission audit table records actor_user_id, subject_user_id, tenant_id, action, role and timestamp. This is valid audit evidence even though it is a database record rather than Pino JSON.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS Vector labels process/container/service identity, UDS identity events record authenticatedactors, and applicationprivilegechanges record explicit actor_user_id. The literal requirement accepts identity of the individual OR process associated with the event; requiring userId on everygenericprocess log would overstate this control. Domain-specific failed privilege events remain separately flagged.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204727",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204727",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000101",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204727",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000101",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204727r960909_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204727r960909",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000101-AS-000072",
          "rule_title": "The application server must generate log records containing the full-text recording of privileged commands or the individual identities of group account users.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to generate the full-text recording of privileged commands or the individual identities of group users, or both.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and deployment configuration to determine if the application server is configured to generate full-text recording of privileged commands or the individual identities of group users at a minimum.\n\nHave a user execute a privileged command and review the log data to validate that the full-text or identity of the individual is being logged.\n\nIf the application server is not meeting this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Privileged commands are commands that change the configuration or data of the application server.  Since this type of command changes the application server configuration and could possibly change the security posture of the application server, these commands need to be logged to show the full-text of the command executed.  Without the full-text, reconstruction of harmful events or forensic analysis is not possible.\n\nOrganizations can consider limiting the additional log information to only that information explicitly needed for specific log requirements.  At a minimum, the organization must log either full-text recording of privileged commands or the individual identities of group users, or both.  The organization must maintain log trails in sufficient detail to reconstruct events to determine the cause and impact of compromise.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57417",
            "SV-71689"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000135"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "a80f2268-4a45-5d5b-9a86-77a216ebe53c",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Privileged role grant/revocation already records individual actor, targetsubject, role/action and time in the append-only audit store; UDS IdP admin records and platformnode/containerlogs cover their own management boundary. This satisfies the record generation/individual-identity mechanism without requiring a duplicate application serverconsole recorder. Failedprivilege eventcoverage is separately flagged under204719/824/825.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-roleaudit] re-appserver-roleaudit\nRole grant/revocation writes an actor+subject+tenant+action+role audit record, transactionally coupled with mutation. Failed preconditions throw before insert; failed transaction paths do not retain successful audit rows.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/users/user-roles.service.ts\n\n[re-appserver-auditfields] re-appserver-auditfields\nAppendonly permission audit table records actor_user_id, subject_user_id, tenant_id, action, role and timestamp. This is valid audit evidence even though it is a database record rather than Pino JSON.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-logging] re-appserver-logging\nVector collects pod/node records with namespace/app/container/host identifiers, acknowledged diskbuffers and Loki sinks, exports internal metrics and is monitored. It collects process/container identity independent of Horizonrequest actors.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Privileged role grant/revocation already records individual actor, targetsubject, role/action and time in the append-only audit store; UDS IdP admin records and platformnode/containerlogs cover their own management boundary. This satisfies the record generation/individual-identity mechanism without requiring a duplicate application serverconsole recorder. Failedprivilege eventcoverage is separately flagged under204719/824/825.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204728",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204728",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000108",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204728",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000108",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204728r960912_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204728r960912",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000108-AS-000067",
          "rule_title": "The application server must alert the SA and ISSO, at a minimum, in the event of a log processing failure.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server log feature to alert the SA and ISSO in the event of a log processing failure.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server log configuration.  Verify the application server sends alerts to the SA and ISSO in the event of a log processing failure.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to meet this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Logs are essential to monitor the health of the system, investigate changes that occurred to the system, or investigate a security incident.  When log processing fails, the events during the failure can be lost.  To minimize the timeframe of the log failure, an alert needs to be sent to the SA and ISSO at a minimum.\n\nLog processing failures include, but are not limited to, failures in the application server log capturing mechanisms or log storage capacity being reached or exceeded. In some instances, it is preferred to send alarms to individuals rather than to an entire group. Application servers must be able to trigger an alarm and send an alert to, at a minimum, the SA and ISSO in the event there is an application server log processing failure.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35186",
            "SV-46473"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000139"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "7d0e4076-95e1-599d-b821-0a15fbdb3c72",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. The dedicated UDS logging/monitoring service is the correct implementationboundary and supplies Vectorinternal metrics, storage/Prometheus alerts, Falco and optional identity rules. Baseline configuration nevertheless does not establish all required processingfailure/tampering events, the 75 percent audit capacity threshold or SA/ISSO receiver. This is sharedmonitoring configuration/routing, not a new API alert engine. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Identify the audit/log processing failures covered by this exact rule, including capture, hardware/software, collector/backend and storage failures as applicable. Configure the required alerts and named recipients using the existing central monitoring. Test coverage and delivery; verify real-time latency where the rule requires it. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-logging] re-appserver-logging\nVector collects pod/node records with namespace/app/container/host identifiers, acknowledged diskbuffers and Loki sinks, exports internal metrics and is monitored. It collects process/container identity independent of Horizonrequest actors.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-alerts] re-appserver-alerts\nCore enables storage/workload/Prometheus defaultalerts and Alertmanager. This baseline does not configure a dedicated 75 percent audit storage threshold or actual SA/ISSO receiver.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/prometheus-stack/values/kube-prometheus-stack-values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-identityalerts] re-appserver-identityalerts\nIdentity realm/user/admin alert definitions exist behind detailedObservability.alerts.enabled; they do not automatically enable alerts or define operational receivers.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/keycloak-logging-alerting-rules.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-falco] re-appserver-falco\nFalco/Sidekick detects runtime behavior and sends events toLoki, but Alertmanager routing is commented and no direct automated permissionrevocation/responsetrigger is configured.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-core-falco-notify] mpn-core-falco-notify\nFalco sends structured events to Loki by default. falcosidekick.config.alertmanager.hostport/endpoint are documented but commented; Gallatin can enable the route to monitoring Alertmanager and configure receivers itself.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. The dedicated UDS logging/monitoring service is the correct implementationboundary and supplies Vectorinternal metrics, storage/Prometheus alerts, Falco and optional identity rules. Baseline configuration nevertheless does not establish all required processingfailure/tampering events, the 75 percent audit capacity threshold or SA/ISSO receiver. This is sharedmonitoring configuration/routing, not a new API alert engine. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nNext action: Identify the audit/log processing failures covered by this exact rule, including capture, hardware/software, collector/backend and storage failures as applicable. Configure the required alerts and named recipients using the existing central monitoring. Test coverage and delivery; verify real-time latency where the rule requires it. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204731",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204731",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000116",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204731",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000116",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204731r960927_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204731r960927",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000116-AS-000076",
          "rule_title": "The application server must use internal system clocks to generate time stamps for log records.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to use internal system clocks to generate time stamps for log records.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server configuration files to determine if the internal system clock is used for time stamps. If this is not feasible, an alternative workaround is to take an action that generates an entry in the logs and then immediately query the operating system for the current time. A reasonable match between the two times will suffice as evidence that the system is using the internal clock for timestamps.\n\nIf the application server does not use the internal system clock to generate time stamps, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Without the use of an approved and synchronized time source configured on the systems, events cannot be accurately correlated and analyzed to determine what is transpiring within the application server.\n\nIf an event has been triggered on the network, and the application server is not configured with the correct time, the event may be seen as insignificant, when in reality the events are related and may have a larger impact across the network. Synchronization of system clocks is needed in order to correctly correlate the timing of events that occur across multiple systems. Determining the correct time a particular event occurred on a system, via time stamps, is critical when conducting forensic analysis and investigating system events. \n\nApplication servers must utilize the internal system clock when generating time stamps and log records.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46490",
            "V-35203"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000159"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "b91406b9-59d4-517f-829a-13e72ce58dda",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: nginx/Istio emit access records to stdout, current API structured logging records event/method/path/source/status with timestamps, and Vector continuously aggregates pod/node records in Loki. This supports the specified timing/location/outcome/collection mechanics, not complete security-event or actor coverage.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-web] app-web\nnginx serves a static SPA at /usr/share/nginx/html on port 3000, with try_files fallback, stdout/stderr logs and no configured proxy_pass, CGI or WebDAV locations.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/web/nginx.conf\n\n[core-accesslog] core-accesslog\nIstio access logs go to stdout; path normalization merges slashes, proxies are held until ready, and forwarded certificate details are sanitized.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/values/base-istiod.yaml\n\n[logging] API logging\nCurrent HTTP logger includes service, request/trace IDs, event, remote address and response status, with secret redaction. Its common bindings do not add authenticated actor identity; other records/correlation must be checked.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/core/observability/logging.ts\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. nginx/Istio emit access records to stdout, current API structured logging records event/method/path/source/status with timestamps, and Vector continuously aggregates pod/node records in Loki. This supports the specified timing/location/outcome/collection mechanics, not complete security-event or actor coverage.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204732",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204732",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000118",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204732",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000118",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204732r960930_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204732r960930",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000118-AS-000078",
          "rule_title": "The application server must protect log information from any type of unauthorized read access.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to protect log information from unauthorized read access.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the configuration settings to determine if the application server log features protect log information from unauthorized access.\n\nReview file system settings to verify the application server sets secure file permissions on log files.\n\nIf the application server does not protect log information from unauthorized read access, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If log data were to become compromised, then competent forensic analysis and discovery of the true source of potentially malicious system activity is difficult, if not impossible, to achieve. In addition, access to log records provides information an attacker could potentially use to his or her advantage.\n\nApplication servers contain admin interfaces that allow reading and manipulation of log records. Therefore, these interfaces should not allow unfettered access to those records. Application servers also write log data to log files which are stored on the OS, so appropriate file permissions must also be used to restrict access.\n\nLog information includes all information (e.g., log records, log settings, transaction logs, and log reports) needed to successfully log information system activity. Application servers must protect log information from unauthorized read access.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46492",
            "V-35205"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000162"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "68ce069b-fa0a-5712-99f8-1b70327b825d",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Pod logs are shipped to an isolated Loki service and viewed through SSO-group-restricted Grafana. Ordinary tenant workloads do not have declared access to modify the logging tools/store; bucket IAM/admin access is an inherited residual.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-loki] core-loki\nLoki uses ambient mesh; ingress is limited to Grafana, Vector, FalcoSidekick and monitoring service identities; object-store egress is declared separately.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/loki/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Pod logs are shipped to an isolated Loki service and viewed through SSO-group-restricted Grafana. Ordinary tenant workloads do not have declared access to modify the logging tools/store; bucket IAM/admin access is an inherited residual.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
