        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204735",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204735",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000121",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204735",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000121",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204735r960939_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204735r960939",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000121-AS-000081",
          "rule_title": "The application server must protect log tools from unauthorized access.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server or OS to protect log tools from unauthorized access.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and server configuration to determine if the application server protects log tools from unauthorized access.\n\nRequest a system administrator attempt to access log tools while logged into the server in a role that does not have the requisite privileges.\n\nIf the application server does not protect log tools from unauthorized access, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Protecting log data also includes identifying and protecting the tools used to view and manipulate log data. \n\nDepending upon the log format and application, system and application log tools may provide the only means to manipulate and manage application and system log data. \n\nIt is, therefore, imperative that access to log tools be controlled and protected from unauthorized access. \n\nApplication servers provide a web- and/or a command line-based management functionality for managing the application server log capabilities. In addition, subsets of log tool components may be stored on the file system as jar or xml configuration files. The application server must ensure that in addition to protecting any web-based log tools, any file system-based tools are protected as well.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46500",
            "V-35213"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001493"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "345124ed-b70d-5b13-a16b-853301e50e0e",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Pod logs are shipped to an isolated Loki service and viewed through SSO-group-restricted Grafana. Ordinary tenant workloads do not have declared access to modify the logging tools/store; bucket IAM/admin access is an inherited residual.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-loki] core-loki\nLoki uses ambient mesh; ingress is limited to Grafana, Vector, FalcoSidekick and monitoring service identities; object-store egress is declared separately.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/loki/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Pod logs are shipped to an isolated Loki service and viewed through SSO-group-restricted Grafana. Ordinary tenant workloads do not have declared access to modify the logging tools/store; bucket IAM/admin access is an inherited residual.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204736",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204736",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000122",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204736",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000122",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204736r960942_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204736r960942",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000122-AS-000082",
          "rule_title": "The application server must protect log tools from unauthorized modification.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server or the OS to protect log tools from unauthorized modification.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and server configuration to determine if the application server protects log tools from unauthorized modification. Request a system administrator attempt to modify log tools while logged into the server in a role that does not have the requisite privileges.\n\nLocate binary copies of log tool executables that are located on the file system and attempt to modify using unprivileged credentials.\n\nIf the application server does not protect log tools from unauthorized modification, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Protecting log data also includes identifying and protecting the tools used to view and manipulate log data. \n\nDepending upon the log format and application, system and application log tools may provide the only means to manipulate and manage application and system log data. \n\nIt is, therefore, imperative that access to log tools be controlled and protected from unauthorized modification. If an attacker were to modify log tools, he could also manipulate logs to hide evidence of malicious activity. \n\nApplication servers provide a web- and/or a command line-based management functionality for managing the application server log capabilities. In addition, subsets of log tool components may be stored on the file system as jar or xml configuration files. The application server must ensure that in addition to protecting any web-based log tools, any file system-based tools are protected as well.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46501",
            "V-35214"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001494"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "e6095e8e-ef24-521b-b8fd-d2ec42e4f638",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Pod logs are shipped to an isolated Loki service and viewed through SSO-group-restricted Grafana. Ordinary tenant workloads do not have declared access to modify the logging tools/store; bucket IAM/admin access is an inherited residual.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-loki] core-loki\nLoki uses ambient mesh; ingress is limited to Grafana, Vector, FalcoSidekick and monitoring service identities; object-store egress is declared separately.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/loki/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Pod logs are shipped to an isolated Loki service and viewed through SSO-group-restricted Grafana. Ordinary tenant workloads do not have declared access to modify the logging tools/store; bucket IAM/admin access is an inherited residual.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
