          "uuid": "56bdc3d8-7c1d-5d97-8a59-ee238c048430",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Vector sends records continuously from application/node local storage to a separate Loki/object-store component, more frequently than weekly. This is log offloading, not a claim that application PVC backups are configured.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-loki] core-loki\nLoki uses ambient mesh; ingress is limited to Grafana, Vector, FalcoSidekick and monitoring service identities; object-store egress is declared separately.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/loki/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Vector sends records continuously from application/node local storage to a separate Loki/object-store component, more frequently than weekly. This is log offloading, not a claim that application PVC backups are configured.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204739",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204739",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000126",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204739",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000126",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204739r960951_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204739r960951",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000126-AS-000085",
          "rule_title": "The application server must use cryptographic mechanisms to protect the integrity of log information.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to hash and sign logs using cryptographic means. \n\nAlternatively, configure the application server or OS to send logs to a centralized log server that meets the hashing and signing requirement.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and configuration to determine if the application server can be configured to protect the integrity of log data using cryptographic hashes and digital signatures. Configure the application server to hash and sign log data. This is typically done the moment when log files cease to be written to and are rolled over for storage or offloading. \n\nAlternatively, if the application server is not able to hash and sign log data, the task can be delegated by configuring the application server or underlying OS to send logs to a centralized log management system or SIEM that can meet the requirement. \n\nIf the application server is not configured to hash and sign logs, or is not configured to utilize the aforementioned OS and centralized log management resources to meet the requirement, this is a finding. ",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Protecting the integrity of log records helps to ensure log files are not tampered with. Cryptographic mechanisms are the industry-established standard used to protect the integrity of log data. An example of cryptographic mechanisms is the computation and application of a cryptographic hash and using asymmetric cryptography with digital signatures.  Application Servers often write log data to files on the file system.  These files typically roll over on a periodic basis. Once the logs are rolled over, hashing and signing the logs assures the logs are not tampered with and helps to assure log integrity.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35217",
            "SV-46504"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001350"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "84f64c68-8968-5be4-93a0-b629fdfca904",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS already centralizes logs and protects transport/access, but the saved evidence does not establish cryptographic hashing/signing of retained log records. The official check permits delegation to centralized logging or a SIEM that meets that requirement. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work with verification first: confirm central logging ownership and existing retained-log integrity coverage, or an explicitly accepted equivalent, before assigning changes. TLS and access restrictions alone do not establish the retained-record protection, and no duplicate per-application signer is inherently required.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, identify the actual central log-management/SIEM owner and retained-record lifecycle. Inspect configured hashing/signing, verification and key-management evidence, or an explicitly accepted equivalent mapped to the exact check. Credit supported existing centralized protection; configure only confirmed gaps at the appropriate central boundary. Retain configuration, integrity-verification and any accepted-equivalence records; do not treat generic immutability or transport TLS as automatically satisfying hashing/signing.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-images-logging] re-images-logging\nCore centralizes logs and supplies TLS/network/access controls. No log record/batch cryptographic hash-and-sign configuration was found in the shipped Loki/Vector settings; transport integrity alone does not implement the exact retained-log signing alternative asked by V204739.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/loki/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-images-core-identity] re-images-core-identity\nCore1.14 always starts Keycloak --features=fips --fips-mode=strict. identity-config0.32 stages bc-fips/bctls/bcutil jars via keycloak-crypto-fips1402 and syncs these providers; supported hardened flavors exist. Actual contract selects this shared identity, so web/API session and authentication crypto belongs here, not missing bundled Keycloak settings. Actual Horizon contract does not select the Core distribution flavor.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/statefulset.yaml",
          "finding_details": "UDS already centralizes logs and protects transport/access, but the saved evidence does not establish cryptographic hashing/signing of retained log records. The official check permits delegation to centralized logging or a SIEM that meets that requirement. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work with verification first: confirm central logging ownership and existing retained-log integrity coverage, or an explicitly accepted equivalent, before assigning changes. TLS and access restrictions alone do not establish the retained-record protection, and no duplicate per-application signer is inherently required.\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, identify the actual central log-management/SIEM owner and retained-record lifecycle. Inspect configured hashing/signing, verification and key-management evidence, or an explicitly accepted equivalent mapped to the exact check. Credit supported existing centralized protection; configure only confirmed gaps at the appropriate central boundary. Retain configuration, integrity-verification and any accepted-equivalence records; do not treat generic immutability or transport TLS as automatically satisfying hashing/signing."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204740",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204740",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000131",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204740",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000131",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204740r1265169_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204740r1265169",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000131-AS-000002",
          "rule_title": "The application server must prevent the installation of patches, service packs, or application components without verification the software component has been digitally signed using a certificate recognized and approved by the organization.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to prevent the installation of patches, service packs, or application components without verification the software component has been digitally signed using a certificate recognized and approved by the organization.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review system documentation to determine if the application server prevents the installation of patches, service packs, or application components without verification the software component has been digitally signed using a certificate recognized and approved by the organization.\n\nIf the application server does not meet this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Changes to any software components can have significant effects on the overall security of the application. Verifying software components have been digitally signed using a certificate recognized and approved by the organization ensures the software has not been tampered with and that it has been provided by a trusted vendor. \n\nAccordingly, patches, service packs, or application components must be signed with a certificate recognized and approved by the organization. \n\nVerifying the authenticity of the software prior to installation validates the integrity of the patch or upgrade received from a vendor. This ensures the software has not been tampered with and that it has been provided by a trusted vendor. Self-signed certificates are disallowed by this requirement. The application should not have to verify the software again. This requirement does not mandate DoW certificates for this purpose; however, the certificate used to verify the software must be from an approved CA.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57495",
            "SV-71771"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-003992"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "2caedb88-a572-5d11-8c24-2388fe0f0aee",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Whole-product approved delivery verifies real code-signing certificate identity and DSSE signatures, binds image digests inside exact packages, requires CAT vouch and publishes a digest-fixed bundle. PostgreSQL and application components are installed through that controlled immutable package path. This supplies a source-backed signed-package equivalent; lack of a second generic Pepr image-signature plugin is not by itself an application remediation finding. Acceptance of package-boundary verification versus every arbitrary cluster admission remains explicit tailoring, not a claimed universal admission policy.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the approved package-level signer/trust mapping and restrict deployment to this verified immutable release path; assess arbitrary cluster-admin installation separately.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-images-release] re-images-release\nSuccessful Nov5 trusted release2026.45.4-uds.1 binds Horizonb84b9af/build19710/run42109769948, package d528b7f, Core1.14.0, actual identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. Twelve delivered image platform digests are explicit; no bundled Keycloak is shipped. Published package digest sha256:486f55939f94ab081d2a12ecade5439512f8f7c4b3119c40b027c711d386adc9. Read build-info/platform-contract/publication metadata artifact13464284834, not just example chart defaults.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-images-verify] re-images-verify\nReal signer verification uses pinned DU Fulcio/Sigstore TSA anchors, expected subject/SAN/issuer, P256/SHA256 DSSE signature, code-signing EKU and RFC3161 timestamp. release.py check_evidence calls verify_production for all required phases and binds exact archives/build-info/packed image digests; CAT vouch gates publication. Actual delivery run succeeded these gates.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/ci/verify_attestation.py\n\n[re-images-publish] re-images-publish\nThe main-only protected release workflow selects qualified successful main source, validates image inventory/source digests against packed OCI manifests, binds archive SHA256SUMS, requires cryptographically verified Witness evidence and CAT vouch before registry publication; digest-qualified registry bundle fixes installation content.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/ci/release.py",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Whole-product approved delivery verifies real code-signing certificate identity and DSSE signatures, binds image digests inside exact packages, requires CAT vouch and publishes a digest-fixed bundle. PostgreSQL and application components are installed through that controlled immutable package path. This supplies a source-backed signed-package equivalent; lack of a second generic Pepr image-signature plugin is not by itself an application remediation finding. Acceptance of package-boundary verification versus every arbitrary cluster admission remains explicit tailoring, not a claimed universal admission policy.\n\nNext action: Retain the approved package-level signer/trust mapping and restrict deployment to this verified immutable release path; assess arbitrary cluster-admin installation separately."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204741",
