          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "7b72c965-c3ae-5028-9153-0e8b447a694b",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs N/A justification\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Horizoncontainers expose no native local interactive managementlogin/account authenticator. LocalCLI/serveradministration is Kubernetes/node operator access outside the application server process; its MFA must be assessed on the MPN management boundary. Adding an in-container password/MFAconsole would create an unnecessary accesspath.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Record no native local management interface and map local operator access to MPN host/Kubernetes control checklist.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-podsecurity] re-appserver-podsecurity\nAdmission restricts privileges/non-root/escalation/capabilities with explicitexemptions. This supplies secureapplication server operation and a preventionmechanism, but does not itself integrate Falcointrusionevents into accesscontrol.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[re-appserver-identitygateway] re-appserver-identitygateway\nUDS routes privateKeycloak paths away from public tenantgateway, exposes admin access separately, strips user-supplied clientcertificate header and injects downstreamcert for delegated X509 handling.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Needs N/A justification. Horizoncontainers expose no native local interactive managementlogin/account authenticator. LocalCLI/serveradministration is Kubernetes/node operator access outside the application server process; its MFA must be assessed on the MPN management boundary. Adding an in-container password/MFAconsole would create an unnecessary accesspath.\n\nNext action: Record no native local management interface and map local operator access to MPN host/Kubernetes control checklist."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204748",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204748",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000153",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204748",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000153",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204748r981680_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204748r981680",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000153-AS-000104",
          "rule_title": "The application server must authenticate users individually prior to using a group authenticator.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to authenticate users individually prior to allowing any group-based authentication.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and configuration to determine if the application server individually authenticates users prior to authenticating via a role or group.\n\nReview application server logs to verify user accesses requiring authentication can be traced back to an individual account.\n\nIf the application server does not authenticate users on an individual basis, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "To ensure individual accountability and prevent unauthorized access, application server users (and any processes acting on behalf of application server users) must be individually identified and authenticated. \n\nA group authenticator is a generic account used by multiple individuals. Use of a group authenticator alone does not uniquely identify individual users. \n\nApplication servers must ensure individual users are authenticated prior to authenticating via role or group authentication. This is to ensure that there is nonrepudiation for actions taken.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35302",
            "SV-46589"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004045"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "91fd3f34-94c5-5c39-80d1-76ce3f4dacaf",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Protected access requires the configured UDS issuer's individually authenticated subject before tenant/group roles are evaluated. AuthGuard validates signature/issuer/audience/expiry, maps the stable subject to a user and one tenant, and only then establishes trusted context for authorization. A shared group does not replace individual authentication.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-auth] re-appserver-auth\nEvery protected request validates OIDC claims and single tenantgroup, resolves stable sub->localuser/tenant, and sets trusted userId/tenantId CLS context. Pending/no-role users are denied.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[re-appserver-authorization] re-appserver-authorization\nAuthorization derives user, objecttype/relation/objectId and contextual tuples, passes the pinned model to OpenFGA and uses a per-request cache keyed by user+object+relation+tuples.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/authorization/authorization.service.ts\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Protected access requires the configured UDS issuer's individually authenticated subject before tenant/group roles are evaluated. AuthGuard validates signature/issuer/audience/expiry, maps the stable subject to a user and one tenant, and only then establishes trusted context for authorization. A shared group does not replace individual authentication.\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204749",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204749",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000156",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204749",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000156",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204749r960993_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204749r960993",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000156-AS-000106",
          "rule_title": "The application server must provide security extensions to extend the SOAP protocol and provide secure authentication when accessing sensitive data.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to utilize secure authentication when SOAP web services are used to access sensitive data. \n",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation to ensure the application server provides extensions to the SOAP protocol that provide secure authentication. These protocols include, but are not limited to, WS_Security suite.  Review policy and data owner protection requirements in order to identify sensitive data.\n\nIf secure authentication protocols are not utilized to protect data identified by data owner as requiring protection, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Application servers may provide a web services capability that could be leveraged to allow remote access to sensitive application data. A web service, which is a repeatable process used to make data available to remote clients, should not be confused with a web server. \n\nMany web services utilize SOAP, which in turn utilizes XML and HTTP as a transport. Natively, SOAP does not provide security protections. As such, the application server must provide security extensions to enhance SOAP capabilities to ensure that secure authentication mechanisms are employed to protect sensitive data. The WS_Security suite is a widely used and acceptable SOAP security extension.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35304",
            "SV-46591"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001941"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "8f777edb-15fd-51b0-89de-7515b57578b9",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs N/A justification\nEvidence state: \nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The identified API uses HTTP/OIDC rather than a demonstrated SOAP security endpoint. SOAP/WS-Security applicability must be confirmed for every delivered service.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Inventory actual exposed protocols/connectors for all services and obtain acceptance of a supported non-SOAP rationale.\n\nEvidence:\n[authz] Application access control\nAPI guard validates identity and sets tenant/user context; permission guard invokes a permission enforcer when route metadata is present. This is source support, not complete route/tenant coverage.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Needs N/A justification. The identified API uses HTTP/OIDC rather than a demonstrated SOAP security endpoint. SOAP/WS-Security applicability must be confirmed for every delivered service.\n\nNext action: Inventory actual exposed protocols/connectors for all services and obtain acceptance of a supported non-SOAP rationale."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204750",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204750",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000163",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204750",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000163",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204750r981681_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204750r981681",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000163-AS-000111",
          "rule_title": "The application server must disable identifiers (individuals, groups, roles, and devices) after 35 days of inactivity.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to disable identifiers (individuals, groups, roles, and devices) after 35 days of inactivity.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and configuration to ensure the application server disables identifiers (individuals, groups, roles, and devices) after 35 days of inactivity.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to disable identifiers (individuals, groups, roles, and devices) after 35 days of inactivity, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Inactive identifiers pose a risk to systems and applications. Attackers that are able to exploit an inactive identifier can potentially obtain and maintain undetected access to the application. Owners of inactive accounts will not notice if unauthorized access to their user account has been obtained. \n\nApplications need to track periods of inactivity and disable application identifiers after 35 days of inactivity. \n\nManagement of user identifiers is not applicable to shared information system accounts (e.g., guest and anonymous accounts). It is commonly the case that a user account is the name of an information system account associated with an individual.\n\nTo avoid having to build complex user management capabilities directly into their application, wise developers leverage the underlying OS or other user account management infrastructure (AD, LDAP) that is already in place within the organization and meets organizational user account management requirements.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46596",
            "V-35309"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-003627"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "0b763b3d-2b83-5390-8c52-f960f3847c59",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual UDS identity has an inactivity-disable workflow, but Core leaves ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset and workflowdisabled by default; administrativeexceptions are explicit. This can be solved by selecting existing provider policy or accepted inheriteddirectorypolicy, not by duplicating lifecycle in API. Inactivity implementation already exists in Core and is Gallatin-operated; the source-known disabled default needs Gallatin configuration, not documentation from an external provider. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the same 35-day inactivity decision to similar requirements. Share V-222411 configuration and verification work; confirm current configuration first and preserve only applicable authorized exceptions. The user-account workflow alone does not establish coverage for every group, role or device identifier in broader rules.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Verify current identity/directory inactivity controls and ownership, then configure the existing 35-day workflow for in-scope human accounts if not already enabled, coordinated with V-222411. Persist the Core override and update the existing realm through a controlled change where needed; verify effective disablement. For this broader identifier rule, separately map individuals, groups, roles and devices to appropriate lifecycle controls or justified scope decisions; do not assume disabling human accounts covers all identifier types. Confirm permitted emergency/infrequently-used account exceptions against the exact rule and retain configuration and test evidence. Credit an existing compliant directory service only where supported by the rule and deployment evidence.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-coreidentity] re-appserver-coreidentity\nCore 1.14 default enables OTP and X509, sets concurrent per-user limit0(unlimited), leaves inactivity workflow opt-in, includes explicit truststorepaths and optional alert enablement.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-inactivity] mpn-identity-inactivity\nSet realmInitEnv.ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS=\"35\" before firstimport. Existingrealm: uds Workflows >disable-inactive-users enabled=true, disable-user stepafter35d; realm-admin excluded by shipped workflow. Source establishes exact operator action without asking DU to prove it.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-account-inactivity-disable.mdx\n\n[mpn-identity-init-only] mpn-identity-init-only\nrealmInitEnv and realmAuthFlows are initial realm import inputs. Existing realm is persisted in DB; redeploying Secret does not change it. Core documents equivalent Keycloak AdminConsole settings. Use controlled non-destructive AdminAPI/console update and persist the desired bundle override; do not advise tearing down production identity.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-keycloak-login-policies.mdx\n\n[mpn-core-inactivity-setting] mpn-core-inactivity-setting\nGallatin can set keycloak realmInitEnv.ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS=35 to enable initial realm workflow. Source warns realm import is initial-only; existing realm requires directly enabling/updating disable-inactive-users workflow with after35d. Admin accounts are excepted and never-used accounts require handling.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-account-inactivity-disable.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Actual UDS identity has an inactivity-disable workflow, but Core leaves ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset and workflowdisabled by default; administrativeexceptions are explicit. This can be solved by selecting existing provider policy or accepted inheriteddirectorypolicy, not by duplicating lifecycle in API. Inactivity implementation already exists in Core and is Gallatin-operated; the source-known disabled default needs Gallatin configuration, not documentation from an external provider. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the same 35-day inactivity decision to similar requirements. Share V-222411 configuration and verification work; confirm current configuration first and preserve only applicable authorized exceptions. The user-account workflow alone does not establish coverage for every group, role or device identifier in broader rules.\n\nNext action: Verify current identity/directory inactivity controls and ownership, then configure the existing 35-day workflow for in-scope human accounts if not already enabled, coordinated with V-222411. Persist the Core override and update the existing realm through a controlled change where needed; verify effective disablement. For this broader identifier rule, separately map individuals, groups, roles and devices to appropriate lifecycle controls or justified scope decisions; do not assume disabling human accounts covers all identifier types. Confirm permitted emergency/infrequently-used account exceptions against the exact rule and retain configuration and test evidence. Credit an existing compliant directory service only where supported by the rule and deployment evidence."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204751",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204751",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000171",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204751",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000171",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204751r981682_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204751r981682",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000171-AS-000119",
          "rule_title": "The application server must for password-based authentication, store passwords using an approved salted key derivation function, preferably using a keyed hash.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to only store encrypted representations of passwords.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation and configuration to determine if the application server enforces the requirement to only store encrypted representations of passwords.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to meet this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Applications must enforce password encryption when storing passwords. Passwords need to be protected at all times and encryption is the standard method for protecting passwords. If passwords are not encrypted, they can be plainly read and easily compromised. \n\nApplication servers provide either a local user store or they integrate with enterprise user stores like LDAP. When the application server is responsible for creating or storing passwords, the application server must enforce the storage of encrypted representations of passwords.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46604",
            "V-35317"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004062"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "c0ff0205-aa20-5b26-8c04-258f8ed8e486",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The actual delegated UDS realm uses PBKDF2-SHA256 password hashing with configured strength/history/expiry constraints; Horizon does not store end-user plaintext passwords. Password storage is assessed at the selected identity provider, not the unused bundled realm. Validated module operation remains a separate crypto check.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-coreidentity] re-appserver-coreidentity\nCore 1.14 default enables OTP and X509, sets concurrent per-user limit0(unlimited), leaves inactivity workflow opt-in, includes explicit truststorepaths and optional alert enablement.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The actual delegated UDS realm uses PBKDF2-SHA256 password hashing with configured strength/history/expiry constraints; Horizon does not store end-user plaintext passwords. Password storage is assessed at the selected identity provider, not the unused bundled realm. Validated module operation remains a separate crypto check.\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204752",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204752",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000172",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204752",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000172",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204752r1207648_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204752r1207648",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000172-AS-000120",
          "rule_title": "The application server must transmit only encrypted representations of passwords.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to transmit only encrypted representations of passwords.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation and configuration to determine if the application server enforces the requirement to encrypt passwords when they are transmitted.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to meet this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Passwords need to be protected at all times, and encryption is the standard method for protecting passwords during transmission.  If passwords are not encrypted, they can be plainly read (i.e., clear text) and easily compromised.\n\nApplication servers have the capability to utilize either certificates (tokens) or user IDs and passwords in order to authenticate. When the application server transmits or receives passwords, the passwords must be encrypted.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46605",
            "V-35318"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000197"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "7311ebd7-11b4-582c-8411-5129e7363fc7",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS gateway enforces HTTPS/TLS1.3-or-1.2 and root STRICT mTLS protects the enrolled workload hops; Horizon opts into ambient mesh. This supports source inference of transit protection, with approved-crypto module claims assessed separately.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[core-gateway] core-gateway\nGateway redirects HTTP to HTTPS and requires TLS 1.3 by default, optionally TLS 1.2; credentialName references Kubernetes TLS secrets.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/charts/uds-istio-config/templates/gateway.yaml\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS gateway enforces HTTPS/TLS1.3-or-1.2 and root STRICT mTLS protects the enrolled workload hops; Horizon opts into ambient mesh. This supports source inference of transit protection, with approved-crypto module claims assessed separately.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204753",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204753",
