          "group_id": "V-204753",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000172",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204753r1207649_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204753r1207649",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000172-AS-000121",
          "rule_title": "The application server must utilize encryption when using LDAP for authentication.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to encrypt LDAP traffic.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation and configuration to determine if the application server enforces the requirement to encrypt LDAP traffic.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to meet this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Passwords need to be protected at all times, and encryption is the standard method for protecting passwords during transmission. \n\nApplication servers have the capability to utilize LDAP directories for authentication. If LDAP connections are not protected during transmission, sensitive authentication credentials can be stolen. When the application server utilizes LDAP, the LDAP traffic must be encrypted.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35319",
            "SV-46606"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000197"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "de33f12e-69c0-58d3-9b85-30ed40e5c7b2",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs N/A justification\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The actual release uses UDS OIDC. Its shipped identity realm defines no LDAP federation/provider and Horizon communicates with the issuer/JWKS through HTTPS; this conditional LDAP encryption rule is inapplicable to that selected path. This replaces the stale bundled-Keycloak rationale, rather than adding unused LDAP TLS code.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Record no LDAP authentication path in the selected identity boundary; reassess any later directory connector.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-auth] re-appserver-auth\nEvery protected request validates OIDC claims and single tenantgroup, resolves stable sub->localuser/tenant, and sets trusted userId/tenantId CLS context. Pending/no-role users are denied.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Needs N/A justification. The actual release uses UDS OIDC. Its shipped identity realm defines no LDAP federation/provider and Horizon communicates with the issuer/JWKS through HTTPS; this conditional LDAP encryption rule is inapplicable to that selected path. This replaces the stale bundled-Keycloak rationale, rather than adding unused LDAP TLS code.\n\nNext action: Record no LDAP authentication path in the selected identity boundary; reassess any later directory connector."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204754",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204754",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000175",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204754",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000175",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204754r961038_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204754r961038",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000175-AS-000124",
          "rule_title": "The application server must perform RFC 5280-compliant certification path validation.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to validate certificates in accordance with RFC 5280.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and deployed configuration to determine whether the application server provides PKI functionality that validates certification paths in accordance with RFC 5280.\n\nIf PKI is not being used, this is NA.\n\nIf the application server is using PKI, but it does not perform this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "A certificate's certification path is the path from the end entity certificate to a trusted root certification authority (CA).  Certification path validation is necessary for a relying party to make an informed decision regarding acceptance of an end entity certificate.  Certification path validation includes checks such as certificate issuer trust, time validity and revocation status for each certificate in the certification path.  Revocation status information for CA and subject certificates in a certification path is commonly provided via certificate revocation lists (CRLs) or online certificate status protocol (OCSP) responses.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46609",
            "V-35322"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000185"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "a37b3fcc-3f0a-5055-9e0e-f835575bea3c",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The actual profile uses PKI through UDS X509 identity and mesh rather than omitting PKI entirely. The delegated provider uses SHA-verified DoD trust chains, certificate timestamp/status checks, OCSP fail-closed and user-certificate mapping. Credit certification-path validation at that boundary instead of requiring the API to implement an RFC5280 parser.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain delegated certificate-validation implementation evidence; the local CRL cache default is tracked separately.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-trust] re-appserver-trust\nImage trust builder defaults officialDoD approved externalPKI trust chains ZIP, requires SHA256 verification, converts .cer files, excludes configured certificate categories, and includes non-expired certificates. This is actual source provisioning, not a generic claim that trust is unknowable.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/truststore/ca-to-jks.sh\n\n[re-appserver-identitygateway] re-appserver-identitygateway\nUDS routes privateKeycloak paths away from public tenantgateway, exposes admin access separately, strips user-supplied clientcertificate header and injects downstreamcert for delegated X509 handling.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The actual profile uses PKI through UDS X509 identity and mesh rather than omitting PKI entirely. The delegated provider uses SHA-verified DoD trust chains, certificate timestamp/status checks, OCSP fail-closed and user-certificate mapping. Credit certification-path validation at that boundary instead of requiring the API to implement an RFC5280 parser.\n\nNext action: Retain delegated certificate-validation implementation evidence; the local CRL cache default is tracked separately."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204755",
          "group_tree": [
