          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000176",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204755r961041_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204755r961041",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000176-AS-000125",
          "rule_title": "Only authenticated system administrators or the designated PKI Sponsor for the application server must have access to the web servers private key.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to enforce authorized access to the corresponding private key.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server configuration and documentation to ensure the application server enforces authorized access to the corresponding private key.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to enforce authorized access to the corresponding private key, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The cornerstone of the PKI is the private key used to encrypt or digitally sign information. \n\nIf the private key is stolen, this will lead to the compromise of the authentication and non-repudiation gained through PKI because the attacker can use the private key to digitally sign documents and can pretend to be the authorized user. \n\nBoth the holders of a digital certificate and the issuing authority must protect the computers, storage devices, or whatever they use to keep the private keys. Java-based application servers utilize the Java keystore, which provides storage for cryptographic keys and certificates. The keystore is usually maintained in a file stored on the file system.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46611",
            "V-35324"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000186"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "cb367ffb-3d88-56e4-9035-d37013ce177a",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Gateway/mesh private keys are supplied through restricted Kubernetes Secret/identity paths, not baked into the static application image; runtime containers are non-root/read-only and platform access is separated. If an HSM is organizationally required, that additional safeguard needs an explicit requirement.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-gateway] core-gateway\nGateway redirects HTTP to HTTPS and requires TLS 1.3 by default, optionally TLS 1.2; credentialName references Kubernetes TLS secrets.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/charts/uds-istio-config/templates/gateway.yaml\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[app-pods] app-pods\nCandidate service chart applies non-root/read-only/no-escalation contexts, Secret references, probes and resource settings. Rendered app pods corroborate those settings; some init containers lack explicit resources.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-service/templates/deployment.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Gateway/mesh private keys are supplied through restricted Kubernetes Secret/identity paths, not baked into the static application image; runtime containers are non-root/read-only and platform access is separated. If an HSM is organizationally required, that additional safeguard needs an explicit requirement.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204756",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204756",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000177",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204756",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000177",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204756r1265175_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204756r1265175",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000177-AS-000126",
          "rule_title": "The application server must map the authenticated identity to the individual user or group account for PKI-based authentication.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to utilize the DoW Enterprise PKI infrastructure.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation to ensure the application server provides a PKI integration capability that meets DoW PKI infrastructure requirements.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to meet this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The cornerstone of PKI is the private key used to encrypt or digitally sign information. The key by itself is a cryptographic value that does not contain specific user information, but the key can be mapped to a user. Without mapping the certificate used to authenticate to the user account, the ability to determine the identity of the individual user or group will not be available for forensic analysis.\n\nApplication servers must provide the capability to utilize and meet requirements of the DoW Enterprise PKI infrastructure for application authentication.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46612",
            "V-35325"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000187"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "3d561b1d-8e5c-584e-8c72-82cf7cc36141",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual delivered identity is UDS rather than bundled Keycloak. UDSaccepts X509 credentials at SSO/adminboundary, strips forgedcertificate headers, maps UPN/customcertificate attributes to individualusers, validates status viaOCSP and provisions SHA-verified DoD-approved externalPKItrust chains. This supplies PIV/CAC/partnerfederation mechanisms through delegatedidentity; the APIappropriately consumes its signed OIDC identity instead of parsing smartcards itself. Final approvedagency/profile scope is captured in the inheritance/trust record.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-coreidentity] re-appserver-coreidentity\nCore 1.14 default enables OTP and X509, sets concurrent per-user limit0(unlimited), leaves inactivity workflow opt-in, includes explicit truststorepaths and optional alert enablement.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-flowrender] re-appserver-flowrender\nChart maps OTP_ENABLED=true to OTP_FLOW_ENABLED=REQUIRED and MFA_FLOW_ENABLED=REQUIRED, and maps X509 auth to enabled alternate flow. Raw realm interpolation fallbacks alone are not the final rendered flow.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/secret-kc-realm.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-identitygateway] re-appserver-identitygateway\nUDS routes privateKeycloak paths away from public tenantgateway, exposes admin access separately, strips user-supplied clientcertificate header and injects downstreamcert for delegated X509 handling.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-trust] re-appserver-trust\nImage trust builder defaults officialDoD approved externalPKI trust chains ZIP, requires SHA256 verification, converts .cer files, excludes configured certificate categories, and includes non-expired certificates. This is actual source provisioning, not a generic claim that trust is unknowable.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/truststore/ca-to-jks.sh\n\n[re-appserver-trustsync] re-appserver-trustsync\nIdentityconfig sync copies generated certs into Keycloak conf/truststores and installs realm/plugin/theme configuration. FIPS libraries are copied when configured.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/sync.sh\n\n[re-appserver-auth] re-appserver-auth\nEvery protected request validates OIDC claims and single tenantgroup, resolves stable sub->localuser/tenant, and sets trusted userId/tenantId CLS context. Pending/no-role users are denied.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Actual delivered identity is UDS rather than bundled Keycloak. UDSaccepts X509 credentials at SSO/adminboundary, strips forgedcertificate headers, maps UPN/customcertificate attributes to individualusers, validates status viaOCSP and provisions SHA-verified DoD-approved externalPKItrust chains. This supplies PIV/CAC/partnerfederation mechanisms through delegatedidentity; the APIappropriately consumes its signed OIDC identity instead of parsing smartcards itself. Final approvedagency/profile scope is captured in the inheritance/trust record.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204757",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204757",
