          ],
          "group_id": "V-204757",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000178",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204757r961047_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204757r961047",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000178-AS-000127",
          "rule_title": "The application server must obscure feedback of authentication information during the authentication process to protect the information from possible exploitation/use by unauthorized individuals.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to obscure feedback of authentication information during the authentication process to protect the information from possible exploitation/use by unauthorized individuals.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and configuration to determine if any interfaces which are provided for authentication purposes display the user's password when it is typed into the data entry field.\n\nIf authentication information is not obfuscated when entered, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "To prevent the compromise of authentication information during the authentication process, the application server authentication screens must obfuscate input so an unauthorized user cannot view a password, PIN, or any other authenticator value as it is being typed.\n\nThis can occur when a user is authenticating to the application server through the web management interface or command line interface. The application server must obfuscate all passwords, PINs, or other authenticator information when typed. User ID is not required to be obfuscated.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35328",
            "SV-46615"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000206"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "ab70dc78-e2a1-5751-bfac-a9137aca900f",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Authentication is delegated to UDS, whose actual login theme uses a password-type input; Horizon does not render or echo an end-user password. Credential fields in server logs are redacted. Masking is implemented where credential entry occurs rather than repeated in the API.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-loginmask] re-appserver-loginmask\nThe delegated UDS login theme renders the credential input with type=password and autocomplete=off. Masking is implemented at the actual interactive authentication boundary.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/theme/login/login.ftl\n\n[re-appserver-logger] re-appserver-logger\nPino records stable serviceidentity, trace/requestIDs, requestmethod/URL and outcome, with secretredaction. Its commonbindings do not attach authenticated userId; not every per-domain failure can be assigned to a user from that record alone.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/observability/logging.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Authentication is delegated to UDS, whose actual login theme uses a password-type input; Horizon does not render or echo an end-user password. Credential fields in server logs are redacted. Masking is implemented where credential entry occurs rather than repeated in the API.\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204758",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204758",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000179",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204758",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000179",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204758r1265176_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204758r1265176",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000179-AS-000129",
          "rule_title": "The application server must utilize FIPS 140-2 approved encryption modules when authenticating users and processes.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to use a FIPS-2 approved TLS version to authenticate users and to disable all non-FIPS-approved SSL versions.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and deployed configuration to determine which version of TLS is being used.\n\nIf the application server is not using TLS when authenticating users or non-FIPS-approved SSL versions are enabled, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Encryption is only as good as the encryption modules utilized. Unapproved cryptographic module algorithms cannot be verified and cannot be relied upon to provide confidentiality or integrity, and DoW data may be compromised due to weak algorithms. The use of TLS provides confidentiality of data in transit between the application server and client.\n\nTLS must be enabled and non-FIPS-approved SSL versions must be disabled. NIST SP 800-52 specifies the preferred configurations for government systems.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46616",
            "V-35329"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000803"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "ffb8ea3b-b768-5f84-a127-574db73e3cc9",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Authentication cryptographic coverage is partially established by saved UDS FIPS-provider and Horizon OIDC/WebCrypto evidence. A separate NATS authentication-signing path uses pure-JavaScript cryptography that does not automatically inherit base-image module validation. However, the official check focuses on TLS during authentication and disabled nonapproved SSL versions, so the NATS concern must be mapped to the exact requirement before prescribing changes. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work with verification first: confirm actual authentication transport/module coverage and the NATS cryptographic boundary, then address only confirmed gaps or document an explicitly accepted scope determination.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, inspect assessed authentication paths and effective TLS/module configuration, including UDS user login, OIDC verification and NATS process/client/callout authentication. Verify TLS use and disabled nonapproved SSL versions against the official check; separately trace NATS signature operations and module provenance and determine their applicability to the requirement. Credit existing validated controls, retain scope/transport/module evidence, and recommend changes only for supported residuals or obtain explicit accepted tailoring. Do not infer validation of vendored JavaScript crypto from the image label.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-images-release] re-images-release\nSuccessful Nov5 trusted release2026.45.4-uds.1 binds Horizonb84b9af/build19710/run42109769948, package d528b7f, Core1.14.0, actual identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. Twelve delivered image platform digests are explicit; no bundled Keycloak is shipped. Published package digest sha256:486f55939f94ab081d2a12ecade5439512f8f7c4b3119c40b027c711d386adc9. Read build-info/platform-contract/publication metadata artifact13464284834, not just example chart defaults.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-images-core-identity] re-images-core-identity\nCore1.14 always starts Keycloak --features=fips --fips-mode=strict. identity-config0.32 stages bc-fips/bctls/bcutil jars via keycloak-crypto-fips1402 and syncs these providers; supported hardened flavors exist. Actual contract selects this shared identity, so web/API session and authentication crypto belongs here, not missing bundled Keycloak settings. Actual Horizon contract does not select the Core distribution flavor.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/statefulset.yaml\n\n[re-images-identity-libs] re-images-identity-libs\nIdentity-config stages required BouncyCastle FIPS provider jars from its version-matched Keycloak FIPS dependency; sync.sh copies these into Keycloak provider directory when FIPS_ENABLED.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/Dockerfile\n\n[re-images-keycloak-fips] re-images-keycloak-fips\nUpstream documents that --features=fips --fips-mode=strict uses BouncyCastle FIPS approved mode; session generation/authentication crypto can be delegated to the actual UDS identity service.\nhttps://www.keycloak.org/server/fips\n\n[re-images-api-sbom] re-images-api-sbom\nActual built API SBOM contains nodejs24.22.1-r2, NIST-CMVP-5132, openssl-provider-fips-3.4.0 version3.4.0-r5, openssl-config-fipshardened4.1.0-r0. NIST-CMVP-5523-optin is also present but is not the default provider.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/actions/runs/42109769948/artifacts/13453332302\n\n[re-images-node-mode] re-images-node-mode\nVendor explicitly says node-fips Crypto APIs are configured into approved mode at image build time through openssl-config-fipshardened, without application code changes.\nhttps://images.chainguard.dev/directory/image/node-fips/overview\n\n[re-images-nist5132] re-images-nist5132\nNIST lists Chainguard FIPS Provider for OpenSSL, FIPS140-3 certificate5132, Active, sunset2031-01-13, approved-mode caveat. Exact API/web/Postgres SBOM markers map to this certificate; no certificate number guessed from generic FIPS naming.\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-module-validation-program/certificate/5132\n\n[re-images-nats-purejs] re-images-nats-purejs\nActual delivered API image0e20f5f... was exported read-only. Runtime package@nats-io/nkeys2.0.3 lib/nacl.js imports tweetnacl1.0.3; kp.js sign() calls nacl.sign.detached. Actual tweetnacl nacl-fast.js contains pureJavaScript crypto_sign/crypto_hash (SHA512) rather than delegating signatures to NodeOpenSSL; Nodecrypto is used only for randombytes. File hashes: nkeys kp.js95678dc5fee2f88470cdb1dd8131f9275a359198e794b31563bc2bcb2ea267e2; nacl.js85bbc7f2b9c47265dc3e50c390f8a84104e7f2f37ef772ffd03af9a659492834; tweetnacl-fast6bcd37a3b20dce913f82d4b23e4e2b661058b4b953df8a3f8c45d56ac4f72447. Source uses these keys to sign NATS user/account authenticationJWTs and nonce authenticators. FIPS OpenSSL image does not validate this pureJS implementation.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/nats-auth/nats-auth.jwt.service.ts\n\n[re-images-jose-webcrypto] re-images-jose-webcrypto\nActual delivered jose6.2.13 implementation dist/webapi/lib/jws_verify.js calls crypto.subtle.verify; OIDC JWT verification therefore uses NodeWebCrypto in the validated FIPS image. The separate NATS JWT signature path uses @nats-io/nkeys/tweetnacl and must not be conflated with this OIDC implementation.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.oidc-jwt.service.ts",
          "finding_details": "Authentication cryptographic coverage is partially established by saved UDS FIPS-provider and Horizon OIDC/WebCrypto evidence. A separate NATS authentication-signing path uses pure-JavaScript cryptography that does not automatically inherit base-image module validation. However, the official check focuses on TLS during authentication and disabled nonapproved SSL versions, so the NATS concern must be mapped to the exact requirement before prescribing changes. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work with verification first: confirm actual authentication transport/module coverage and the NATS cryptographic boundary, then address only confirmed gaps or document an explicitly accepted scope determination.\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, inspect assessed authentication paths and effective TLS/module configuration, including UDS user login, OIDC verification and NATS process/client/callout authentication. Verify TLS use and disabled nonapproved SSL versions against the official check; separately trace NATS signature operations and module provenance and determine their applicability to the requirement. Credit existing validated controls, retain scope/transport/module evidence, and recommend changes only for supported residuals or obtain explicit accepted tailoring. Do not infer validation of vendored JavaScript crypto from the image label."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204759",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204759",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000181",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204759",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000181",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204759r961056_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204759r961056",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000181-AS-000255",
          "rule_title": "The application server must provide a log reduction capability that supports on-demand reporting requirements.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to provide and utilize log reduction with on-demand reporting or configure the application server to send its logs to a centralized log log system that provides log reduction and on-demand reporting functions.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server product documentation and server configuration to determine if the application server is configured to provide log reduction with on-demand reporting.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to provide log reduction with on-demand reporting, or is not configured to send its logs to a centralized log system, this is a finding.\n",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The ability to generate on-demand reports, including after the log data has been subjected to log reduction, greatly facilitates the organization's ability to generate incident reports as needed to better handle larger-scale or more complex security incidents.\n\nLog reduction is a process that manipulates collected log information and organizes such information in a summary format that is more meaningful to analysts. The report generation capability provided by the application must support on-demand (i.e., customizable, ad-hoc, and as needed) reports.\n\nInstead of the application server providing the log reduction function; it is also accepted practice to configure the application server to send its logs to a centralized log system that can be used to provide the log reduction with reporting capability. Security Incident Event Management (SIEM) systems are an example of such a solution.\n\nTo fully understand and investigate an incident within the components of the application server, the application server, must be configured to provide log reduction and on-demand reporting or be configured to send its logs to a centralized log system.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57527",
            "SV-71803"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001876"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "7177abd5-fe70-5510-b83d-88943dcf1b26",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS Vector defines persistent disk buffers with acknowledgements and continuously offloads to Loki; Grafana supports on-demand query/reduction. This is concrete source evidence for buffering/offload capability, not a request for an unspecified future logging system.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS Vector defines persistent disk buffers with acknowledgements and continuously offloads to Loki; Grafana supports on-demand query/reduction. This is concrete source evidence for buffering/offload capability, not a request for an unspecified future logging system.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204760",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204760",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000206",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204760",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000206",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204760r961083_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204760r961083",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000206-AS-000145",
          "rule_title": "The application server must identify prohibited mobile code.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to identify prohibited mobile code.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server configuration to determine if the application server is configured to identify prohibited mobile code.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to identify prohibited mobile code, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Mobile code is defined as software modules obtained from remote systems, transferred across a network, and then downloaded and executed on a local system without explicit installation or execution by the recipient.\n\nMobile code technologies include: Java, JavaScript, ActiveX, PDF, Postscript, Shockwave movies, Flash animations, and VBScript. Usage restrictions and implementation guidance apply to both the selection and use of mobile code installed on organizational servers and mobile code downloaded and executed on individual workstations.\n\nApplication servers must meet policy requirements regarding the deployment and/or use of mobile code. This includes digitally signing applets in order to provide a means for the client to establish application authenticity and prohibit unauthorized code from being used.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57547",
            "SV-71823"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001166"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "7c803cb4-4b5f-5878-a0b5-5eb3e05f0382",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs N/A justification\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The assessed server executes packaged Node/Python service images, not downloaded browser mobile code, Javaapplets or a user-installable script/plugin runtime. FrontendJavaScript is compiled/deployed and served to the browser; document uploads are parsed as data by dedicated workers. Apply mobile code approval to the browser/releaseboundary rather than assume this server needs a mobile codeclassification engine.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Document the absence of server-executed mobile code/plugininterfaces and the separate browsercode inventory.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-webmobile] re-appserver-webmobile\nMultistage immutable frontend image copies compiled assets; clientJavaScript is served to browser, not installed/executed by an application server plugin/mobile code interface.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/web/Dockerfile\n\n[re-appserver-podsecurity] re-appserver-podsecurity\nAdmission restricts privileges/non-root/escalation/capabilities with explicitexemptions. This supplies secureapplication server operation and a preventionmechanism, but does not itself integrate Falcointrusionevents into accesscontrol.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Needs N/A justification. The assessed server executes packaged Node/Python service images, not downloaded browser mobile code, Javaapplets or a user-installable script/plugin runtime. FrontendJavaScript is compiled/deployed and served to the browser; document uploads are parsed as data by dedicated workers. Apply mobile code approval to the browser/releaseboundary rather than assume this server needs a mobile codeclassification engine.\n\nNext action: Document the absence of server-executed mobile code/plugininterfaces and the separate browsercode inventory."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204761",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204761",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000211",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204761",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000211",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204761r1137579_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204761r1137579",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000211-AS-000146",
          "rule_title": "The application server must separate hosted application functionality from application server management functionality.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server so that admin management functionality and hosted applications are separated.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and configuration to verify that the application server separates admin functionality from hosted application functionality.\n\nIf the application server does not separate application server admin functionality from hosted application functionality, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The application server consists of the management interface and hosted applications. By separating the management interface from hosted applications, the user must authenticate as a privileged user to the management interface before being presented with management functionality. This prevents nonprivileged users from having visibility to functions not available to the user. By limiting visibility, a compromised nonprivileged account does not offer information to the attacker to functionality and information needed to further the attack on the application server.\n\nApplication server management functionality includes functions necessary to administer the application server and requires privileged access via one of the accounts assigned to a management role.  The hosted application and hosted application functionality consists of the assets needed for the application to function, such as the business logic, databases, user authentication, etc.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.\n\nThe separation of application server administration functionality from hosted application functionality is either physical or logical and is accomplished by using different computers, different central processing units, different instances of the operating system, network addresses, network ports, or combinations of these methods, as appropriate.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35376",
            "SV-46663"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001082"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "b7abf242-112d-5a01-8d5f-2f4bed61e052",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Hosted Horizon functionality runs in its own workload namespace, while UDS exposes Keycloak administration through a separate admin gateway and redirects private/admin identity paths away from the public SSO entry. Kubernetes-managed deployment/logging administration is separate from application roles. This provides management-plane separation.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-identitygateway] re-appserver-identitygateway\nUDS routes privateKeycloak paths away from public tenantgateway, exposes admin access separately, strips user-supplied clientcertificate header and injects downstreamcert for delegated X509 handling.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-podsecurity] re-appserver-podsecurity\nAdmission restricts privileges/non-root/escalation/capabilities with explicitexemptions. This supplies secureapplication server operation and a preventionmechanism, but does not itself integrate Falcointrusionevents into accesscontrol.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[re-appserver-logaccess] re-appserver-logaccess\nGrafana local login disabled; UDSgroups map Admin/Auditor to Admin/Viewer and unmatched groups are rejected. Runtime log configuration is Kubernetes-managed outside application users.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Hosted Horizon functionality runs in its own workload namespace, while UDS exposes Keycloak administration through a separate admin gateway and redirects private/admin identity paths away from the public SSO entry. Kubernetes-managed deployment/logging administration is separate from application roles. This provides management-plane separation.\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204762",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204762",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000219",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204762",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000219",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204762r1043178_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204762r1043178",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000219-AS-000147",
          "rule_title": "The application server must be configured to mutually authenticate connecting proxies, application servers or gateways. ",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to mutually authenticate proxy servers, other application servers and application gateways as specified.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation, system security plan and application data protection requirements. \n\nIf the connected web proxy is exposed to an untrusted network or if data protection requirements specified in the system security plan mandate the need to establish the identity of the connecting application server, proxy or application gateway and the application server is not configured to mutually authenticate the application server, proxy server or gateway, this is a finding. ",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Application architecture may sometimes require a configuration where an application server is placed behind a web proxy, an application gateway or communicates directly with another application server. In those instances, the application server hosting the service/application is considered the server. The application server, proxy or application gateway consuming the hosted service is considered a client. Authentication is accomplished via the use of certificates and protocols such as TLS mutual authentication. Authentication must be performed when the proxy is exposed to an untrusted network or when data protection requirements specified in the system security plan mandate the need to establish the identity of the connecting application server, proxy or application gateway.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35381",
            "SV-46668"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001184"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "37b76aaa-a658-5dec-ae56-66c9b860cd6c",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS root STRICT mTLS mutually authenticates enrolled proxy/workload hops; Horizon enrolls the namespace. This is platform mutual authentication even though application URLs are plain HTTP.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS root STRICT mTLS mutually authenticates enrolled proxy/workload hops; Horizon enrolls the namespace. This is platform mutual authentication even though application URLs are plain HTTP.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204763",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204763",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000220",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204763",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000220",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204763r1043179_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204763r1043179",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000220-AS-000148",
          "rule_title": "The application server must invalidate session identifiers upon user logout or other session termination.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to terminate administrative sessions upon logout or any other organization- or policy-defined session termination events.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server configuration and organizational policy to determine if the system is configured to terminate administrator sessions upon administrator logout or any other organization- or policy-defined session termination events, such as idle time limit exceeded.\n\nIf the configuration is not set to terminate administrator sessions per defined events, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If communications sessions remain open for extended periods of time even when unused, there is the potential for an adversary to hijack the session and use it to gain access to the device or networks to which it is attached. Terminating sessions after a logout event or after a certain period of inactivity is a method for mitigating the risk of this vulnerability. When a user management session becomes idle, or when a user logs out of the management interface, the application server must terminate the session.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35415",
            "SV-46702"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001185"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "51aa2b2b-fe0c-500e-812a-602412ba2a90",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Interactive sessions are owned by UDS Keycloak: Horizon logout wipes localOIDC state and invokes IdP signout; UDSalso enforces idle/max session lifetimes. This meets the official check's configured administrative-session termination mechanism without adding an independent API sessionstore. OfflineJWTs are short-lived bearer credentials rather than a separate interactive login; immediate credentialrevocation, if required by organizational policy, remains an explicit residual and must not be silently claimed.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Record delegated interactive-session termination and the accepted tokenlifetime/revocation policy; handle any stricter immediate-revocation requirement under its separate control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-logout] re-appserver-logout\nExplicitlogout captures IdP signoutcontext, wipes localOIDC/sessionstate then invokes signoutRedirect. This is a delegated interactive logout implementation, not an absence of logout.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/web/src/auth/auth-store.ts\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-jwt] re-appserver-jwt\nProtectedAPI verifies JWTsignature/issuer/audience/expiry offline. There is no per-request session revocation check; immediate bearer credential invalidation is a residual beyond ending the IdP/browser interactive session.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.oidc-jwt.service.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Interactive sessions are owned by UDS Keycloak: Horizon logout wipes localOIDC state and invokes IdP signout; UDSalso enforces idle/max session lifetimes. This meets the official check's configured administrative-session termination mechanism without adding an independent API sessionstore. OfflineJWTs are short-lived bearer credentials rather than a separate interactive login; immediate credentialrevocation, if required by organizational policy, remains an explicit residual and must not be silently claimed.\n\nNext action: Record delegated interactive-session termination and the accepted tokenlifetime/revocation policy; handle any stricter immediate-revocation requirement under its separate control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204764",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204764",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000223",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204764",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000223",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204764r1043180_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204764r1043180",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000223-AS-000150",
          "rule_title": "The application server must generate a unique session identifier for each session.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to generate a unique session identifier for each session.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server session management configuration settings in either the application server management console, application server initialization or application server configuration files to determine if the application server is configured to generate a unique session identifier for each session.\n\nIf the application server is  not configured to generate a unique session identifier for each session, this is a finding.\n",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Unique session IDs are the opposite of sequentially generated session IDs, which can be easily guessed by an attacker. Unique session identifiers help to reduce predictability of session identifiers. Unique session IDs address man-in-the-middle attacks, including session hijacking or insertion of false information into a session. If the attacker is unable to identify or guess the session information related to pending application traffic, they will have more difficulty in hijacking the session or otherwise manipulating valid sessions.\n\nApplication servers must generate a unique session identifier for each application session so as to prevent session hijacking.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57549",
            "SV-71825"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001664"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "19c91589-d5a3-583f-99cf-20460c1de41c",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The selected UDS Keycloak provider generates session/token identifiers. Horizon uses the standard OIDC flow and accepts only signed tokens with the expected issuer, audience and expiry; caller-chosen unauthenticated strings do not establish a session. FIPS randomness is assessed on the provider under the dedicated crypto rule.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-auth] re-appserver-auth\nEvery protected request validates OIDC claims and single tenantgroup, resolves stable sub->localuser/tenant, and sets trusted userId/tenantId CLS context. Pending/no-role users are denied.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[re-appserver-jwt] re-appserver-jwt\nProtectedAPI verifies JWTsignature/issuer/audience/expiry offline. There is no per-request session revocation check; immediate bearer credential invalidation is a residual beyond ending the IdP/browser interactive session.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.oidc-jwt.service.ts\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-logout] re-appserver-logout\nExplicitlogout captures IdP signoutcontext, wipes localOIDC/sessionstate then invokes signoutRedirect. This is a delegated interactive logout implementation, not an absence of logout.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/web/src/auth/auth-store.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The selected UDS Keycloak provider generates session/token identifiers. Horizon uses the standard OIDC flow and accepts only signed tokens with the expected issuer, audience and expiry; caller-chosen unauthenticated strings do not establish a session. FIPS randomness is assessed on the provider under the dedicated crypto rule.\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended."
        },
