          "group_id": "V-204765",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000223",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204765r1043180_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204765r1043180",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000223-AS-000151",
          "rule_title": "The application server must recognize only system-generated session identifiers.",
          "fix_text": "Design the application server to recognize only system-generated session identifiers.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server configuration to determine if the application server recognizes only system-generated session identifiers.\n\nIf the application server does not recognize only system-generated session identifiers, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "This requirement focuses on communications protection at the application session, versus network packet level. The intent of this control is to establish grounds for confidence at each end of a communications session in the ongoing identity of the other party and in the validity of the information being transmitted.\n\nUnique session IDs are the opposite of sequentially generated session IDs which can be easily guessed by an attacker. Unique session identifiers help to reduce predictability of said identifiers.\n\nUnique session IDs address man-in-the-middle attacks, including session hijacking or insertion of false information into a session. If the attacker is unable to identify or guess the session information related to pending application traffic, they will have more difficulty in hijacking the session or otherwise manipulating valid sessions.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35421",
            "SV-46708"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001664"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "3be363d4-72c8-54c2-ba15-4126536226b2",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The selected UDS Keycloak provider generates session/token identifiers. Horizon uses the standard OIDC flow and accepts only signed tokens with the expected issuer, audience and expiry; caller-chosen unauthenticated strings do not establish a session. FIPS randomness is assessed on the provider under the dedicated crypto rule.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-auth] re-appserver-auth\nEvery protected request validates OIDC claims and single tenantgroup, resolves stable sub->localuser/tenant, and sets trusted userId/tenantId CLS context. Pending/no-role users are denied.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[re-appserver-jwt] re-appserver-jwt\nProtectedAPI verifies JWTsignature/issuer/audience/expiry offline. There is no per-request session revocation check; immediate bearer credential invalidation is a residual beyond ending the IdP/browser interactive session.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.oidc-jwt.service.ts\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-logout] re-appserver-logout\nExplicitlogout captures IdP signoutcontext, wipes localOIDC/sessionstate then invokes signoutRedirect. This is a delegated interactive logout implementation, not an absence of logout.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/web/src/auth/auth-store.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The selected UDS Keycloak provider generates session/token identifiers. Horizon uses the standard OIDC flow and accepts only signed tokens with the expected issuer, audience and expiry; caller-chosen unauthenticated strings do not establish a session. FIPS randomness is assessed on the provider under the dedicated crypto rule.\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204766",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204766",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000224",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204766",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000224",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204766r1043181_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204766r1043181",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000224-AS-000152",
          "rule_title": "The application server must generate a unique session identifier using a FIPS 140-2 approved random number generator.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to generate unique session identifiers and to use a FIPS 140-2 random number generator to generate the randomness of the session identifiers.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server configuration and documentation to determine if the application server uses a FIPS 140-2 approved random number generator to create unique session identifiers.\n\nHave a user log onto the application server to determine if the session IDs generated are random and unique.\n\nIf the application server does not generate unique session identifiers and does not use a FIPS 140-2 random number generator to create the randomness of the session ID, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The application server will use session IDs to communicate between modules or applications within the application server and between the application server and users.  The session ID allows the application to track the communications along with credentials that may have been used to authenticate users or modules.\n\nUnique session IDs are the opposite of sequentially generated session IDs which can be easily guessed by an attacker.  Unique session identifiers help to reduce predictability of said identifiers.\n\nUnique session IDs address man-in-the-middle attacks, including session hijacking or insertion of false information into a session.  If the attacker is unable to identify or guess the session information related to pending application traffic, they will have more difficulty in hijacking the session or otherwise manipulating valid sessions.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46709",
            "V-35422"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001188"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "fc2686e2-020c-5efd-9db4-38e25d7cc5f2",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual published platform contract selects UDS shared identity, not bundled Keycloak. Its Core1.14 chart unconditionally enables FIPS strict mode, and identity-config supplies required BC-FIPS provider libraries; upstream confirms strict mode uses approved-mode crypto. Auth/session creation belongs to that provider; API token verification also runs in the actual CMVP5132 node-fips runtime. No duplicate API/nginx session generator or authentication crypto implementation is needed. The pure-JavaScript NATS signature exception is recorded separately under V204758 rather than incorrectly failing delegated UDS login/session generation.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the exact delivered-release mapping and cited evidence with the checklist; no additional application change recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-images-release] re-images-release\nSuccessful Nov5 trusted release2026.45.4-uds.1 binds Horizonb84b9af/build19710/run42109769948, package d528b7f, Core1.14.0, actual identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. Twelve delivered image platform digests are explicit; no bundled Keycloak is shipped. Published package digest sha256:486f55939f94ab081d2a12ecade5439512f8f7c4b3119c40b027c711d386adc9. Read build-info/platform-contract/publication metadata artifact13464284834, not just example chart defaults.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-images-core-identity] re-images-core-identity\nCore1.14 always starts Keycloak --features=fips --fips-mode=strict. identity-config0.32 stages bc-fips/bctls/bcutil jars via keycloak-crypto-fips1402 and syncs these providers; supported hardened flavors exist. Actual contract selects this shared identity, so web/API session and authentication crypto belongs here, not missing bundled Keycloak settings. Actual Horizon contract does not select the Core distribution flavor.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/statefulset.yaml\n\n[re-images-identity-libs] re-images-identity-libs\nIdentity-config stages required BouncyCastle FIPS provider jars from its version-matched Keycloak FIPS dependency; sync.sh copies these into Keycloak provider directory when FIPS_ENABLED.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/Dockerfile\n\n[re-images-keycloak-fips] re-images-keycloak-fips\nUpstream documents that --features=fips --fips-mode=strict uses BouncyCastle FIPS approved mode; session generation/authentication crypto can be delegated to the actual UDS identity service.\nhttps://www.keycloak.org/server/fips\n\n[re-images-api-sbom] re-images-api-sbom\nActual built API SBOM contains nodejs24.22.1-r2, NIST-CMVP-5132, openssl-provider-fips-3.4.0 version3.4.0-r5, openssl-config-fipshardened4.1.0-r0. NIST-CMVP-5523-optin is also present but is not the default provider.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/actions/runs/42109769948/artifacts/13453332302\n\n[re-images-node-mode] re-images-node-mode\nVendor explicitly says node-fips Crypto APIs are configured into approved mode at image build time through openssl-config-fipshardened, without application code changes.\nhttps://images.chainguard.dev/directory/image/node-fips/overview\n\n[re-images-nist5132] re-images-nist5132\nNIST lists Chainguard FIPS Provider for OpenSSL, FIPS140-3 certificate5132, Active, sunset2031-01-13, approved-mode caveat. Exact API/web/Postgres SBOM markers map to this certificate; no certificate number guessed from generic FIPS naming.\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-module-validation-program/certificate/5132",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Actual published platform contract selects UDS shared identity, not bundled Keycloak. Its Core1.14 chart unconditionally enables FIPS strict mode, and identity-config supplies required BC-FIPS provider libraries; upstream confirms strict mode uses approved-mode crypto. Auth/session creation belongs to that provider; API token verification also runs in the actual CMVP5132 node-fips runtime. No duplicate API/nginx session generator or authentication crypto implementation is needed. The pure-JavaScript NATS signature exception is recorded separately under V204758 rather than incorrectly failing delegated UDS login/session generation.\n\nNext action: Retain the exact delivered-release mapping and cited evidence with the checklist; no additional application change recommended."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204767",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204767",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000225",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204767",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000225",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204767r961122_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204767r961122",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000225-AS-000153",
          "rule_title": "The application server must be configured to perform complete application deployments.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to detect errors that occur during application deployment and to prevent deployment if errors are encountered.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server configuration and documentation to ensure the system is configured to perform complete application deployments.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to ensure complete application deployments or provides no rollback functionality, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Failure to a known secure state helps prevent a loss of confidentiality, integrity, or availability in the event of a failure of the information system or a component of the system.\n\nWhen an application is deployed to the application server, if the deployment process does not complete properly and without errors, there is the potential that some application files may not be deployed or may be corrupted and an application error may occur during runtime.\n\nThe application server must be able to perform complete application deployments. A partial deployment can leave the server in an inconsistent state. Application servers may provide a transaction rollback function to address this issue.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46710",
            "V-35423"
