          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "d026b9e1-f3cc-5319-bab1-461bf3df56cd",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The build/release path verifies source image digests and produces archive hashes/provenance; image-based deployments provide a complete reproducible baseline and allow returning to a previous package. Tested database rollback is a separate operational requirement.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[images] Image release process\nBuilder verifies source digests and produces archive hashes; sample values with empty digests do not establish that a built release is unpinned. Assessed release/SBOM/scans still needed.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/build-bundle.py\n\n[app-namespace] app-namespace\nAll declared workload charts target horizon-uds; package supplies dedicated namespace, distinct workload charts and generated deployment credentials.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The build/release path verifies source image digests and produces archive hashes/provenance; image-based deployments provide a complete reproducible baseline and allow returning to a previous package. Tested database rollback is a separate operational requirement.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204768",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204768",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000225",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204768",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000225",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204768r961122_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204768r961122",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000225-AS-000154",
          "rule_title": "The application server must provide a clustering capability.",
          "fix_text": "This requirement is dependent upon system MAC and confidentiality.\n\nIf the system MAC and confidentiality levels do not specify redundancy requirements, this requirement is NA.\n\nConfigure the application server to provide application failover or participate in an application cluster which provides failover.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "This requirement is dependent upon system MAC and confidentiality.\n\nIf the system MAC and confidentiality levels do not specify redundancy requirements, this requirement is NA.\n\nReview the application server configuration and documentation to ensure the application server is configured to provide clustering functionality.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to provide clustering or some form of failover functionality, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "This requirement is dependent upon system MAC and confidentiality. If the system MAC and confidentiality levels do not specify redundancy requirements, this requirement is NA.\n\nFailure to a known secure state helps prevent a loss of confidentiality, integrity, or availability in the event of a failure of the information system or a component of the system. When application failure is encountered, preserving application state facilitates application restart and return to the operational mode of the organization with less disruption of mission/business processes.\n\nClustering of multiple application servers is a common approach to providing fail-safe application availability when system MAC and confidentiality levels require redundancy.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35424",
            "SV-46711"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001190"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "bb51709d-490b-5aca-a483-92aa16e3db48",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The application service chart implements Kubernetes replicas, HPA, health/readiness probes and service routing, so a clustering/failover capability is present. A default single replica is not absence of that capability. Any mission-required minimum redundancy must be selected in deployment values and assessed under the actual HA requirement.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Map chart clustering capability and any selected HA topology to the mission availability requirement.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-clustering] re-appserver-clustering\nThe generic service chart implements Kubernetes Deployment replicaCount, optional autoscaling/v2 HPA, readiness/liveness probes and Service routing. This is a clustering/failover capability; MAC I actual redundancy requires its selected deployment configuration.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-service/templates/hpa.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The application service chart implements Kubernetes replicas, HPA, health/readiness probes and service routing, so a clustering/failover capability is present. A default single replica is not absence of that capability. Any mission-required minimum redundancy must be selected in deployment values and assessed under the actual HA requirement.\n\nNext action: Map chart clustering capability and any selected HA topology to the mission availability requirement."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204769",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204769",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000225",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204769",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000225",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204769r961122_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204769r961122",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000225-AS-000166",
          "rule_title": "The application server must fail to a secure state if system initialization fails, shutdown fails, or aborts fail.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to fail to a secure state if system initialization fails, shutdown fails, or aborts fail.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation and configuration to determine if the application server fails to a secure state if system initialization fails, shutdown fails, or aborts fail.\n\nIf the application server cannot be configured to fail securely, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Fail-secure is a condition achieved by the application server in order to ensure that in the event of an operational failure, the system does not enter into an unsecure state where intended security properties no longer hold.  Preserving information system state information also facilitates system restart and return to the operational mode of the organization with less disruption of mission-essential processes.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
