          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57553",
            "SV-71829"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001190"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "ffd84f1c-0adb-5f1f-abed-ba1cc93306c2",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: API authorization enforcement fails closed on missing identity or authorization resolution failure; deployment probes/readiness and UDS admission/mesh isolation prevent granting access because initialization failed.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-enforcer] app-enforcer\nPermission enforcement resolves user/target and fails closed on missing authorization or unexpected resolution errors. Individual route metadata remains part of coverage.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/core/authorization/permission-enforcer.service.ts\n\n[authz] Application access control\nAPI guard validates identity and sets tenant/user context; permission guard invokes a permission enforcer when route metadata is present. This is source support, not complete route/tenant coverage.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[app-pods] app-pods\nCandidate service chart applies non-root/read-only/no-escalation contexts, Secret references, probes and resource settings. Rendered app pods corroborate those settings; some init containers lack explicit resources.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-service/templates/deployment.yaml\n\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. API authorization enforcement fails closed on missing identity or authorization resolution failure; deployment probes/readiness and UDS admission/mesh isolation prevent granting access because initialization failed.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204770",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204770",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000231",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204770",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000231",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204770r1265177_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204770r1265177",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000231-AS-000133",
          "rule_title": "The application server must protect the confidentiality and integrity of all information at rest.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to protect the confidentiality and integrity of all information at rest.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and configuration to ensure the application server is protecting the confidentiality and integrity of all information at rest.\n\nIf the confidentiality and integrity of all information at rest is not protected, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "When data is written to digital media such as hard drives, mobile computers, external/removable hard drives, personal digital assistants, flash/thumb drives, etc., there is risk of data loss and data compromise.\n\nFewer protection measures are needed for media containing information determined by the organization to be in the public domain, to be publicly releasable, or to have limited or no adverse impact if accessed by other than authorized personnel. In these situations, it is assumed the physical access controls where the media resides provide adequate protection.\n\nAs part of a defense-in-depth strategy, data owners and the DoW should consider routinely encrypting information at rest on selected secondary storage devices. The employment of cryptography is at the discretion of the information owner/steward. The selection of the cryptographic mechanisms used is based upon maintaining the confidentiality and integrity of the information.\n\nThe strength of mechanisms is commensurate with the classification and sensitivity of the information.\n\nThe application server must directly provide, or provide access to, cryptographic libraries and functionality that allow applications to encrypt data when it is stored.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71831",
            "V-57555"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001199"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "ab68f24d-d7d1-5bc6-94fb-7f18bf7c6a4f",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Human review on 2026-11-10 selected provisional Likely covered through inherited MPN/platform storage controls under the instruction to apply the same recommendation to similar storage-protection requirements. PR reviewer must confirm or reject platform ownership and the mapping for storage confidentiality and integrity as required by the exact rule. Actual platform protection and the authoritative responsibility boundary remain unverified; this is not an accepted compliance result or an N/A determination. Gallatin retains application data requirements, use of approved protected storage and any application/configuration residuals. This supersedes any earlier assumption that Gallatin must provision a new storage layer.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: MPN/platform storage owner (proposed; reviewer confirmation required); Gallatin for application requirements/configuration and residuals\n\nNext action: PR reviewer must confirm or reject MPN/platform ownership and inherited coverage for storage confidentiality and integrity as required by the exact rule. In the post-classification verification pass, obtain the responsibility agreement and evidence for the actual protection, map all data/components covered by this exact rule, and verify Horizon uses the protected boundary correctly. Identify any Gallatin application/configuration residual separately and revise the recommendation if inheritance or protection is unsupported. Do not prescribe new storage infrastructure before that verification.\n\nEvidence:\n[sol-storage-postgres-pvc] sol-storage-postgres-pvc\nPostgreSQL mounts PGDATA on its own PVC and renders persistence.storageClassName when nonempty. Correct chart key is storageClassName; source/values/postgres.yaml uses storageClass, so changing that unmatched field would not select an encrypted class.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/templates/statefulset.yaml\n\n[sol-storage-seaweed-chart] sol-storage-seaweed-chart\nCurrent chart runs weed server -s3 -s3.config=... without data-encryption flags; a mounted dataPVC supplies its persistence. Its PVC template currently has no storageClassName field, so add optional class binding or deliberately choose the cluster default.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/seaweedfs/templates/deployment.yaml\n\n[sol-storage-pg-official] sol-storage-pg-official\nPostgreSQL18 explicitly supports filesystem/block/full-disk encryption as a data-partition protection layer. Mounted filesystem access still returns plaintext; this does not defeat privileged server access or replace authorization/integrity controls.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/encryption-options.html\n\n[sol-storage-core-boundary] sol-storage-core-boundary\nCore expects an existing cluster and defaultStorageClass; it does not provision MPN host disk encryption. Gallatin owns this infrastructure choice and can bind the app/CorePVCs to a selected encrypted storage class.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/getting-started/production/prerequisites.mdx\n\n[sol-storage-seaweed-aead] sol-storage-seaweed-aead\nExact delivered SeaweedFS4.52 cipher code generates32-byte random keys, AES-GCM encryption/nonces and authenticated gcm.Open decryption. This is an existing object-storage mechanism, not new API cryptography.\nhttps://github.com/seaweedfs/seaweedfs/blob/4.52/weed/util/cipher.go\n\n[sol-storage-dm-auth-integrity] sol-storage-dm-auth-integrity\nLinux dm-integrity with dm-crypt supports authenticated disk encryption that rejects modified encrypted sectors with I/O error. Standalone CRC mode only detects accidental corruption; journal/mode choices affect consistency and performance. Distribution/FIPS-module support must be selected, not assumed.\nhttps://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/admin-guide/device-mapper/dm-integrity.html\n\n[sol-storage-dm-auth-modes] sol-storage-dm-auth-modes\nKernel dm-crypt documents authenticated modes such as gcm(aes) and authenc(hmac(sha256),xts(aes)) using integrity:aead metadata. Plain AES-XTS is not this authenticated mode. Existing host functionality can supply integrity without adding custom DB crypto, when supported in the selected approved platform.\nhttps://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/admin-guide/device-mapper/dm-crypt.html",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Human review on 2026-11-10 selected provisional Likely covered through inherited MPN/platform storage controls under the instruction to apply the same recommendation to similar storage-protection requirements. PR reviewer must confirm or reject platform ownership and the mapping for storage confidentiality and integrity as required by the exact rule. Actual platform protection and the authoritative responsibility boundary remain unverified; this is not an accepted compliance result or an N/A determination. Gallatin retains application data requirements, use of approved protected storage and any application/configuration residuals. This supersedes any earlier assumption that Gallatin must provision a new storage layer.\n\nNext action: PR reviewer must confirm or reject MPN/platform ownership and inherited coverage for storage confidentiality and integrity as required by the exact rule. In the post-classification verification pass, obtain the responsibility agreement and evidence for the actual protection, map all data/components covered by this exact rule, and verify Horizon uses the protected boundary correctly. Identify any Gallatin application/configuration residual separately and revise the recommendation if inheritance or protection is unsupported. Do not prescribe new storage infrastructure before that verification."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204771",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204771",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000231",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204771",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000231",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204771r961128_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204771r961128",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000231-AS-000156",
          "rule_title": "The application server must employ cryptographic mechanisms to ensure confidentiality and integrity of all information at rest when stored off-line.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to employ cryptographic mechanisms to ensure confidentiality and integrity of all application server data at rest when stored off-line.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server configuration to ensure the system is protecting the confidentiality and integrity of all application server data at rest when stored off-line.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to protect all application server data at rest when stored off-line, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "This control is intended to address the confidentiality and integrity of information at rest in non-mobile devices and covers user information and system information. Information at rest refers to the state of information when it is located on a secondary storage device (e.g., disk drive, tape drive) within an organizational information system.\n\nApplication servers generate information throughout the course of their use, most notably, log data.  If the data is not encrypted while at rest, the data used later for forensic investigation cannot be guaranteed to be unchanged and cannot be used for prosecution of an attacker.  To accomplish a credible investigation and prosecution, the data integrity and information confidentiality must be guaranteed.\n\nApplication servers must provide the capability to protect all data, especially log data, so as to ensure confidentiality and integrity.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46713",
            "V-35426"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001199"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "411459e3-edfd-5105-9234-38d44cf16a97",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Existing platform backup/recovery capabilities and corporate procedures provide a starting point, but the saved evidence does not establish complete coverage of this exact requirement for Horizon on MPN. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work with verification first for this and similar backup/recovery issues: locate existing MPN services and records, confirm ownership and actual data coverage, and complete only confirmed gaps. Resource-only backups do not establish application-data recovery, and inherited live-volume encryption does not by itself establish protection of exported/offline copies.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: MPN/platform backup operator and Gallatin application owner; responsibility boundary to verify\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, locate existing MPN/Gallatin backup services, procedures, ownership agreements and execution/restore records. Credit supported existing coverage and assign only confirmed residuals to the responsible operator. Confirm whether offline/exported application-server data copies exist and identify their actual cryptographic confidentiality and integrity protection and recovery-key custody. Verify protection remains effective outside live platform storage; implement only confirmed export/offline gaps rather than assume a new encryption wrapper is needed. Retain the applicable procedure, configuration and verification evidence, coordinated with V-222636.\n\nEvidence:\n[sol-storage-cms-aead] sol-storage-cms-aead\nOpenSSL CMS supports encryption/decryption, signing/verification and AES-GCM authenticated encryption. An approved pinned provider can supply encrypted authenticated backup envelopes without inventing a cryptographic format; exact validated module/mode selection is separate from algorithm availability.\nhttps://docs.openssl.org/3.5/man1/openssl-cms/\n\n[sol-storage-fips-mode] sol-storage-fips-mode\nOpenSSL FIPS use requires configuring the actual validated provider and approved operation selection; simply naming AES or an image FIPS does not establish an approved module boundary for every operation.\nhttps://docs.openssl.org/3.5/man7/fips_module/\n\n[sol-storage-offline-key-limits] sol-storage-offline-key-limits\nAzure KeyVault object backup is an encrypted blob that restores only into Azure within the documented subscription/geography boundary. It is not by itself a portable disconnected private-key escrow or an offline application-data backup.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/general/backup\n\n[sol-storage-azure-objects] sol-storage-azure-objects\nIf Azure storage is selected, service encryption protects data at rest transparently; client downloads receive plaintext, so providerSSE does not by itself preserve offline exported-backup encryption. Do not redirect the existing S3 client to native AzureBlob API without a supported adapter.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-service-encryption",
          "finding_details": "Existing platform backup/recovery capabilities and corporate procedures provide a starting point, but the saved evidence does not establish complete coverage of this exact requirement for Horizon on MPN. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work with verification first for this and similar backup/recovery issues: locate existing MPN services and records, confirm ownership and actual data coverage, and complete only confirmed gaps. Resource-only backups do not establish application-data recovery, and inherited live-volume encryption does not by itself establish protection of exported/offline copies.\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, locate existing MPN/Gallatin backup services, procedures, ownership agreements and execution/restore records. Credit supported existing coverage and assign only confirmed residuals to the responsible operator. Confirm whether offline/exported application-server data copies exist and identify their actual cryptographic confidentiality and integrity protection and recovery-key custody. Verify protection remains effective outside live platform storage; implement only confirmed export/offline gaps rather than assume a new encryption wrapper is needed. Retain the applicable procedure, configuration and verification evidence, coordinated with V-222636."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204772",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204772",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000251",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204772",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000251",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204772r961158_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204772r961158",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000251-AS-000165",
          "rule_title": "The application server must check the validity of all data inputs to the management interface, except those specifically identified by the organization.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to check the validity of data inputs into the management interface except those specifically identified by the organization.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server configuration to determine if the system checks the validity of information inputs to the management interface, except those specifically identified by the organization.\n\nIf the management interface data inputs are not validated, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Invalid user input occurs when a user inserts data or characters into an applications data entry field and the application is unprepared to process that data. This results in unanticipated application behavior potentially leading to an application or information system compromise. Invalid user input is one of the primary methods employed when attempting to compromise an application.\n\nApplication servers must ensure their management interfaces perform data input validation checks. Input validation consists of evaluating user input and ensuring that only allowed characters are utilized. An example is ensuring that the interfaces are not susceptible to SQL injection attacks.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-46723",
            "V-35436"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001310"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "723cca7a-298b-502f-b706-19a6e4a8ea8e",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Current API global OpenAPI/Zod validation constrains declared input fields/types and rejects invalid Content-Type/prototype poisoning. Static nginx delegates application input semantics to that API; multipart/binary handlers remain separately enumerated.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-validation] app-validation\nGlobal interceptor validates declared params/query/headers/JSON with Zod, enforces Content-Type, and fails routes missing a documented exemption/schema; binary/multipart paths are handled separately.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/common/openapi/openapi.validation.interceptor.ts\n\n[app-error] app-error\nUnknown/DB errors map to generic 500 problem details; HTTP transport and internal NATS dispatcher use the same mapper. Explicit application exceptions retain their supplied detail.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/common/errors/problem.mapper.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Current API global OpenAPI/Zod validation constrains declared input fields/types and rejects invalid Content-Type/prototype poisoning. Static nginx delegates application input semantics to that API; multipart/binary handlers remain separately enumerated.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204773",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204773",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000266",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204773",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000266",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204773r961167_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204773r961167",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000266-AS-000168",
          "rule_title": "The application server must identify potentially security-relevant error conditions.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to identify potentially security-relevant error conditions on the server.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server configuration to determine if the system identifies potentially security-relevant error conditions on the server.\n\nIf this function is not performed, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The structure and content of error messages need to be carefully considered by the organization and development team.  Any application providing too much information in error logs and in administrative messages to the screen risks compromising the data and security of the application and system.  The extent to which the application server is able to identify and handle error conditions is guided by organizational policy and operational requirements. Adequate logging levels and system performance capabilities need to be balanced with data protection requirements.\n\nThe structure and content of error messages needs to be carefully considered by the organization and development team.\n\nApplication servers must have the capability to log at various levels which can provide log entries for potential security-related error events.\n\nAn example is the capability for the application server to assign a criticality level to a failed logon attempt error message, a security-related error message being of a higher criticality.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71843",
            "V-57567"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001312"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "b6174295-1b77-5859-af43-90fe7f9a015a",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Global HTTP error filter and shared NATS problem mapper turn unexpected/DB errors into generic 500 problem documents while logging bounded diagnostics on the server. Explicit domain errors still need ordinary application review, but missing error handling is not a supported finding.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-error] app-error\nUnknown/DB errors map to generic 500 problem details; HTTP transport and internal NATS dispatcher use the same mapper. Explicit application exceptions retain their supplied detail.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/common/errors/problem.mapper.ts\n\n[logging] API logging\nCurrent HTTP logger includes service, request/trace IDs, event, remote address and response status, with secret redaction. Its common bindings do not add authenticated actor identity; other records/correlation must be checked.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/core/observability/logging.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Global HTTP error filter and shared NATS problem mapper turn unexpected/DB errors into generic 500 problem documents while logging bounded diagnostics on the server. Explicit domain errors still need ordinary application review, but missing error handling is not a supported finding.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204774",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204774",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000266",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204774",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000266",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204774r961167_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204774r961167",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000266-AS-000169",
          "rule_title": "The application server must only generate error messages that provide information necessary for corrective actions without revealing sensitive or potentially harmful information in error logs and administrative messages.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to not write sensitive information into the logs and administrative messages.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review system documentation and logs to determine if the application server writes sensitive information such as passwords or private keys into the logs and administrative messages.\n\nIf the application server writes sensitive or potentially harmful information into the logs and administrative messages, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Any application providing too much information in error logs and in administrative messages to the screen risks compromising the data and security of the application and system. The structure and content of error messages needs to be carefully considered by the organization and development team.\n\nThe application server must not log sensitive information such as passwords, private keys, or other sensitive data. This requirement pertains to logs that are generated by the application server and application server processes, not the applications that may reside on the application server. Those errors are out of the scope of these requirements.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
