        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204776",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204776",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000290",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204776",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000290",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204776r961206_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204776r961206",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000290-AS-000174",
          "rule_title": "The application server must use cryptographic mechanisms to protect the integrity of log tools.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server log tools to be cryptographically signed to protect the integrity of the tools.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server configuration to determine if the application server log tools have been cryptographically signed to protect the integrity of the tools.\n\nIf the application server log tools have not been cryptographically signed, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Protecting the integrity of the tools used for logging purposes is a critical step in ensuring the integrity of log data. Log data includes all information (e.g., log records, log settings, and log reports) needed to successfully log information system activity. \n\nIt is not uncommon for attackers to replace the log tools or inject code into the existing tools for the purpose of providing the capability to hide or erase system activity from the logs. \n\nTo address this risk, log tools must be cryptographically signed in order to provide the capability to identify when the log tools have been modified, manipulated or replaced. An example is a checksum hash of the file or files.\n\nApplication server log tools must use cryptographic mechanisms to protect the integrity of the tools or allow cryptographic protection mechanisms to be applied to their tools.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-35445",
            "SV-46732"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001496"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "ba7b10bd-99f1-5bb7-9736-b114c2a6b472",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Logging tools are immutable UDS platform components supplied through the platform package/release integrity boundary, not mutable application binaries installed by tenant users. The application package itself also has real verified signer/manifest evidence. Retain the UDS package-signing/inheritance evidence for Vector/Loki rather than demand that the API re-sign centrally managed log tools.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Attach the UDS logging-component package signature/inheritance mapping; retain tenant/runtime immutability controls.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-images-release] re-images-release\nSuccessful Nov5 trusted release2026.45.4-uds.1 binds Horizonb84b9af/build19710/run42109769948, package d528b7f, Core1.14.0, actual identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. Twelve delivered image platform digests are explicit; no bundled Keycloak is shipped. Published package digest sha256:486f55939f94ab081d2a12ecade5439512f8f7c4b3119c40b027c711d386adc9. Read build-info/platform-contract/publication metadata artifact13464284834, not just example chart defaults.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-images-verify] re-images-verify\nReal signer verification uses pinned DU Fulcio/Sigstore TSA anchors, expected subject/SAN/issuer, P256/SHA256 DSSE signature, code-signing EKU and RFC3161 timestamp. release.py check_evidence calls verify_production for all required phases and binds exact archives/build-info/packed image digests; CAT vouch gates publication. Actual delivery run succeeded these gates.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/ci/verify_attestation.py\n\n[re-images-publish] re-images-publish\nThe main-only protected release workflow selects qualified successful main source, validates image inventory/source digests against packed OCI manifests, binds archive SHA256SUMS, requires cryptographically verified Witness evidence and CAT vouch before registry publication; digest-qualified registry bundle fixes installation content.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/ci/release.py\n\n[re-images-logging] re-images-logging\nCore centralizes logs and supplies TLS/network/access controls. No log record/batch cryptographic hash-and-sign configuration was found in the shipped Loki/Vector settings; transport integrity alone does not implement the exact retained-log signing alternative asked by V204739.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/loki/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Logging tools are immutable UDS platform components supplied through the platform package/release integrity boundary, not mutable application binaries installed by tenant users. The application package itself also has real verified signer/manifest evidence. Retain the UDS package-signing/inheritance evidence for Vector/Loki rather than demand that the API re-sign centrally managed log tools.\n\nNext action: Attach the UDS logging-component package signature/inheritance mapping; retain tenant/runtime immutability controls."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204777",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204777",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000295",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204777",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000295",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204777r1043182_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204777r1043182",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000295-AS-000263",
          "rule_title": "The application server must automatically terminate a user session after organization-defined conditions or trigger events requiring a session disconnect.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to terminate user sessions on defined conditions or trigger events.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation and configuration settings to determine if the application server is configured to close user sessions after defined conditions or trigger events are met.\n\nIf the application server is not configured or cannot be configured to disconnect users after defined conditions and trigger events are met, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "An attacker can take advantage of user sessions that are left open, thus bypassing the user authentication process.\n\nTo thwart the vulnerability of open and unused user sessions, the application server must be configured to close the sessions when a configured condition or trigger event is met.\n\nSession termination terminates all processes associated with a user's logical session except those processes that are specifically created by the user (i.e., session owner) to continue after the session is terminated.\n\nConditions or trigger events requiring automatic session termination can include, for example, periods of user inactivity, targeted responses to certain types of incidents, and time-of-day restrictions on information system use.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57401",
            "SV-71673"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002361"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "a0b4acd2-f38e-5c84-994d-a16c3195b3cb",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The actual UDS realm configures finite access-token lifetime, 600-second SSO idle and 36,000-second maximum session lifetime. JWT validation rejects expired credentials and the OIDC client renews or clears unusable state. These implement termination, reauthentication and cached-credential expiry at the delegated provider/client boundary. Any stricter organization-specific immediate trigger/revocation policy remains an explicit residual rather than a claim that all such policies are encoded.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-jwt] re-appserver-jwt\nProtectedAPI verifies JWTsignature/issuer/audience/expiry offline. There is no per-request session revocation check; immediate bearer credential invalidation is a residual beyond ending the IdP/browser interactive session.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.oidc-jwt.service.ts\n\n[re-appserver-logout] re-appserver-logout\nExplicitlogout captures IdP signoutcontext, wipes localOIDC/sessionstate then invokes signoutRedirect. This is a delegated interactive logout implementation, not an absence of logout.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/web/src/auth/auth-store.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The actual UDS realm configures finite access-token lifetime, 600-second SSO idle and 36,000-second maximum session lifetime. JWT validation rejects expired credentials and the OIDC client renews or clears unusable state. These implement termination, reauthentication and cached-credential expiry at the delegated provider/client boundary. Any stricter organization-specific immediate trigger/revocation policy remains an explicit residual rather than a claim that all such policies are encoded.\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204778",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204778",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000296",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204778",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000296",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204778r961224_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204778r961224",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000296-AS-000201",
          "rule_title": "The application server management interface must provide a logout capability for user-initiated communication session.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server management interface to provide a logout capability for the users.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation and configuration settings to determine if the application server management interface provides a logout capability.\n\nIf the application server management interface does not provide a logout capability, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If a user cannot explicitly end an application server management interface session, the session may remain open and be exploited by an attacker; this is referred to as a zombie session.\n\nThe attacker will then have access to the application server management functions without going through the user authentication process.\n\nTo prevent this type of attack, the application server management interface must close user sessions when defined events are met and provide a logout function for users to explicitly close the session and free resources that were in use by the user.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57403",
            "SV-71675"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002363"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "f0baebce-6912-55b6-a822-83dba5e32d86",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Auth store wipes local state and calls OIDC signout; LoggedOutView displays an explicit logout message. This supports the UI/logout mechanism without claiming old access tokens are immediately revoked.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[logout] Browser logout\nCurrent auth store wipes local auth state then invokes OIDC signout. Local storage use alone is not proof that logout fails; replay/expiry/server-side invalidation need tests.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/web/src/auth/auth-store.ts\n\n[app-logged-out] app-logged-out\nDedicated logged-out view displays an explicit logout message and return-to-login action.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/web/src/views/LoggedOutView.vue",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Auth store wipes local state and calls OIDC signout; LoggedOutView displays an explicit logout message. This supports the UI/logout mechanism without claiming old access tokens are immediately revoked.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204779",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204779",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000297",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204779",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000297",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204779r961227_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204779r961227",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000297-AS-000188",
          "rule_title": "The application server management interface must display an explicit logout message to users indicating the reliable termination of authenticated communications sessions.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server management interface to display an explicit logout message to users.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation and configuration settings to determine if the application server management interface displays a logout message.\n\nIf the application server management interface does not display a logout message, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Providing a logout capability to the user allows the user to explicitly close a session and free those resources used during the session.\n\nIf a user cannot explicitly end an application session, the session may remain open and be exploited by an attacker; this is referred to as a zombie session.\n\nThe attacker will then have access to the application server management functions without going through the user authentication process.\n\nTo inform the user that the session has been reliably closed, a logout message must be displayed to the user.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
