ype/relation/objectId and contextual tuples, passes the pinned model to OpenFGA and uses a per-request cache keyed by user+object+relation+tuples.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/authorization/authorization.service.ts\n\n[re-appserver-tenantresolver] re-appserver-tenantresolver\nTenant-scoped authorization targets derive from authenticated tenantId CLS rather than caller-selected arbitrary tenancy.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/authorization/tenant-permission-context.resolver.ts\n\n[re-appserver-schemaattributes] re-appserver-schemaattributes\nBranch records automatically derive tenant_id from trusted app.tenant_id context; composite ancestry/tenantkeys and user audit fields preserve objectsecurity context. Application tenancy is an actual security attribute, not merely an arbitrary label.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/branches.pg.hcl",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Current source has concrete security attributes: authenticatedOIDC sub+tenant groups map to trusted userId/tenantId, request context carries them during processing, OpenFGAderives object/relation/contextual tuples and database objects automatically attach tenant_id with tenant-consistent keys/RLS. These are dynamic subject/object attribute associations in process/transit/storage. The generic rule does not require inventing a classifiedlabel taxonomy when the product's selected securitypolicy is tenancy/roleisolation.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
