            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "In order to be able to provide a forensic history of activity, the application server must ensure users who are granted a privileged role or those who utilize a separate distinct account when accessing privileged functions or data have their actions logged.\n\nIf privileged activity is not logged, no forensic logs can be used to establish accountability for privileged actions that occur on the system.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71669",
            "V-57397"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002234"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "7a05b551-7514-535d-bee2-6ede55d370ca",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Privileged role grant/revocation already records individual actor, targetsubject, role/action and time in the append-only audit store; UDS IdP admin records and platformnode/containerlogs cover their own management boundary. This satisfies the record generation/individual-identity mechanism without requiring a duplicate application serverconsole recorder. Failedprivilege eventcoverage is separately flagged under204719/824/825.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-roleaudit] re-appserver-roleaudit\nRole grant/revocation writes an actor+subject+tenant+action+role audit record, transactionally coupled with mutation. Failed preconditions throw before insert; failed transaction paths do not retain successful audit rows.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/users/user-roles.service.ts\n\n[re-appserver-auditfields] re-appserver-auditfields\nAppendonly permission audit table records actor_user_id, subject_user_id, tenant_id, action, role and timestamp. This is valid audit evidence even though it is a database record rather than Pino JSON.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-logging] re-appserver-logging\nVector collects pod/node records with namespace/app/container/host identifiers, acknowledged diskbuffers and Loki sinks, exports internal metrics and is monitored. It collects process/container identity independent of Horizonrequest actors.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Privileged role grant/revocation already records individual actor, targetsubject, role/action and time in the append-only audit store; UDS IdP admin records and platformnode/containerlogs cover their own management boundary. This satisfies the record generation/individual-identity mechanism without requiring a duplicate application serverconsole recorder. Failedprivilege eventcoverage is separately flagged under204719/824/825.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204788",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204788",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000357",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204788",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000357",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204788r961392_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204788r961392",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000357-AS-000038",
          "rule_title": "The application server must allocate log record storage capacity in accordance with organization-defined log record storage requirements.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to allocate storage for log data before offloading to a log data storage facility.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and configuration to determine if the application server creates log storage to buffer log data until offloading to a log data storage facility.\n\nIf the application server does not allocate storage for log data, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The proper management of log records not only dictates proper archiving processes and procedures be established, it also requires allocating enough storage space to maintain the logs online for a defined period of time.\n\nIf adequate online log storage capacity is not maintained, intrusion monitoring, security investigations, and forensic analysis can be negatively affected.\n\nIt is important to keep a defined amount of logs online and readily available for investigative purposes. The logs may be stored on the application server until they can be archived to a log system or, in some instances, a Storage Area Networks (SAN).  Regardless of the method used, log record storage capacity must be sufficient to store log data when the data cannot be offloaded to a log system or SAN.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71693",
            "V-57421"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001849"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "6f255325-84f2-56f4-94bb-610985f88833",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS Vector defines persistent disk buffers with acknowledgements and continuously offloads to Loki; Grafana supports on-demand query/reduction. This is concrete source evidence for buffering/offload capability, not a request for an unspecified future logging system.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS Vector defines persistent disk buffers with acknowledgements and continuously offloads to Loki; Grafana supports on-demand query/reduction. This is concrete source evidence for buffering/offload capability, not a request for an unspecified future logging system.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204789",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204789",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000358",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204789",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000358",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204789r961395_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204789r961395",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000358-AS-000064",
          "rule_title": "The application server must off-load log records onto a different system or media from the system being logged.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to off-load the logs to a remote log or management server.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the log records are being off-loaded to a separate system or transferred from the application server to a storage location other than the application server itself.\n\nThe system administrator of the device may demonstrate this capability using a log management application, system configuration, or other means.\n\nIf logs are not being off-loaded, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Information system logging capability is critical for accurate forensic analysis. Log record content that may be necessary to satisfy the requirement of this control includes, but is not limited to, time stamps, source and destination IP addresses, user/process identifiers, event descriptions, application-specific events, success/fail indications, filenames involved, access control or flow control rules invoked.\n\nOff-loading is a common process in information systems with limited log storage capacity.\n\nCentralized management of log records provides for efficiency in maintenance and management of records, as well as the backup and archiving of those records. Application servers and their related components are required to off-load log records onto a different system or media than the system being logged.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71695",
            "V-57423"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001851"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "9be684e5-dd29-51e0-8fb9-227cac196bac",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS Vector defines persistent disk buffers with acknowledgements and continuously offloads to Loki; Grafana supports on-demand query/reduction. This is concrete source evidence for buffering/offload capability, not a request for an unspecified future logging system.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS Vector defines persistent disk buffers with acknowledgements and continuously offloads to Loki; Grafana supports on-demand query/reduction. This is concrete source evidence for buffering/offload capability, not a request for an unspecified future logging system.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204790",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204790",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000359",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204790",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000359",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204790r961398_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204790r961398",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000359-AS-000065",
          "rule_title": "The application server must provide an immediate warning to the SA and ISSO, at a minimum, when allocated log record storage volume reaches 75% of maximum log record storage capacity.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to provide an alert to the SA and ISSO when allocated log record storage volume reaches 75% of maximum log record storage capacity.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the configuration settings to determine if the application server logging system provides a warning to the SA and ISSO when 75% of allocated log record storage volume is reached.\n\nIf designated alerts are not sent, or the application server is not configured to use a dedicated logging tool that meets this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "It is critical for the appropriate personnel to be aware if a system is at risk of failing to process logs as required.  Log processing failures include software/hardware errors, failures in the log capturing mechanisms, and log storage capacity being reached or exceeded.  Notification of the storage condition will allow administrators to take actions so that logs are not lost.  This requirement can be met by configuring the application server to utilize a dedicated logging tool that meets this requirement.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71699",
            "V-57427"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001855"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "7913da41-a6f1-5d86-a444-9f34ad643e0b",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. The dedicated UDS logging/monitoring service is the correct implementationboundary and supplies Vectorinternal metrics, storage/Prometheus alerts, Falco and optional identity rules. Baseline configuration nevertheless does not establish all required processingfailure/tampering events, the 75 percent audit capacity threshold or SA/ISSO receiver. This is sharedmonitoring configuration/routing, not a new API alert engine. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Identify the assessed audit/log repository and capacity metric; configure an immediate warning at 75 percent utilization to the recipients specified by this exact rule. Use a different threshold only where this rule expressly permits it and the required approval is retained. Test threshold triggering and delivery. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-logging] re-appserver-logging\nVector collects pod/node records with namespace/app/container/host identifiers, acknowledged diskbuffers and Loki sinks, exports internal metrics and is monitored. It collects process/container identity independent of Horizonrequest actors.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-alerts] re-appserver-alerts\nCore enables storage/workload/Prometheus defaultalerts and Alertmanager. This baseline does not configure a dedicated 75 percent audit storage threshold or actual SA/ISSO receiver.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/prometheus-stack/values/kube-prometheus-stack-values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-identityalerts] re-appserver-identityalerts\nIdentity realm/user/admin alert definitions exist behind detailedObservability.alerts.enabled; they do not automatically enable alerts or define operational receivers.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/keycloak-logging-alerting-rules.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-falco] re-appserver-falco\nFalco/Sidekick detects runtime behavior and sends events toLoki, but Alertmanager routing is commented and no direct automated permissionrevocation/responsetrigger is configured.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-core-falco-notify] mpn-core-falco-notify\nFalco sends structured events to Loki by default. falcosidekick.config.alertmanager.hostport/endpoint are documented but commented; Gallatin can enable the route to monitoring Alertmanager and configure receivers itself.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. The dedicated UDS logging/monitoring service is the correct implementationboundary and supplies Vectorinternal metrics, storage/Prometheus alerts, Falco and optional identity rules. Baseline configuration nevertheless does not establish all required processingfailure/tampering events, the 75 percent audit capacity threshold or SA/ISSO receiver. This is sharedmonitoring configuration/routing, not a new API alert engine. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nNext action: Identify the assessed audit/log repository and capacity metric; configure an immediate warning at 75 percent utilization to the recipients specified by this exact rule. Use a different threshold only where this rule expressly permits it and the required approval is retained. Test threshold triggering and delivery. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine."
        },
