          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Without logging the enforcement of access restrictions against changes to the application server configuration, it will be difficult to identify attempted attacks, and a log trail will not be available for forensic investigation for after-the-fact actions. Configuration changes may occur to any of the modules within the application server through the management interface, but logging of actions to the configuration of a module outside the application server is not logged.\n\nEnforcement actions are the methods or mechanisms used to prevent unauthorized changes to configuration settings. Enforcement action methods may be as simple as denying access to a file based on the application of file permissions (access restriction). Log items may consist of lists of actions blocked by access restrictions or changes identified after the fact.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57493",
            "SV-71769"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-003938"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "c4e67a14-ee57-5600-823d-ec9982f6b74b",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS admission decisions/identity events and node logs are collected by Vector; MPN owns Kubernetes API audit, which records administrative configuration access. This is inherited enforcement auditing, not domain event generation.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-id-audit] core-id-audit\nUDS event listener prints user/admin events to stdout with event fields and UTC timestamps. Vector can collect these without a separately configured app forwarding agent.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS admission decisions/identity events and node logs are collected by Vector; MPN owns Kubernetes API audit, which records administrative configuration access. This is inherited enforcement auditing, not domain event generation.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204798",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204798",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000389",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204798",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000389",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204798r1265178_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204798r1265178",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000389-AS-000253",
          "rule_title": "The application server must require users to reauthenticate when organization-defined circumstances or situations require reauthentication.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to require a user to reauthenticate when organization-defined circumstances or situations are met.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and configuration to determine if the application server requires a user to reauthenticate when organization-defined circumstances or situations are met.\n\nIf the application server does not require a user to reauthenticate when organization-defined circumstances or situations are met, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Without reauthentication, users may access resources or perform tasks for which they do not have authorization.\n\nWhen applications provide the capability to change security roles or escalate the functional capability of the application, it is critical the user reauthenticate.\n\nIn addition to the reauthentication requirements associated with session locks, the application server security model may require reauthentication of individuals in other situations, including (but not limited to) the following circumstances:\n\n(i) When authenticators change; \n(ii) When roles change; \n(iii) When security categories of information systems change; \n(iv) When the execution of privileged functions occurs; \n(v) After a fixed period of time; or \n(vi) Periodically.\n\nWithin the DoW, the minimum circumstances requiring reauthentication are privilege escalation and role changes.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57523",
            "SV-71799"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002038"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "2e18d9d5-4720-5e2d-a6f5-2ff56ccfe463",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The actual UDS realm configures finite access-token lifetime, 600-second SSO idle and 36,000-second maximum session lifetime. JWT validation rejects expired credentials and the OIDC client renews or clears unusable state. These implement termination, reauthentication and cached-credential expiry at the delegated provider/client boundary. Any stricter organization-specific immediate trigger/revocation policy remains an explicit residual rather than a claim that all such policies are encoded.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-jwt] re-appserver-jwt\nProtectedAPI verifies JWTsignature/issuer/audience/expiry offline. There is no per-request session revocation check; immediate bearer credential invalidation is a residual beyond ending the IdP/browser interactive session.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.oidc-jwt.service.ts\n\n[re-appserver-logout] re-appserver-logout\nExplicitlogout captures IdP signoutcontext, wipes localOIDC/sessionstate then invokes signoutRedirect. This is a delegated interactive logout implementation, not an absence of logout.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/web/src/auth/auth-store.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The actual UDS realm configures finite access-token lifetime, 600-second SSO idle and 36,000-second maximum session lifetime. JWT validation rejects expired credentials and the OIDC client renews or clears unusable state. These implement termination, reauthentication and cached-credential expiry at the delegated provider/client boundary. Any stricter organization-specific immediate trigger/revocation policy remains an explicit residual rather than a claim that all such policies are encoded.\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204800",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204800",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000391",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204800",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000391",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204800r1265179_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204800r1265179",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000391-AS-000239",
          "rule_title": "The application server must accept Personal Identity Verification (PIV) credentials to access the management interface.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to accept PIV credentials to access the management interface.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation and configuration to ensure the application server accepts PIV credentials to the management interface.\n\nIf PIV credentials are not accepted, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The use of PIV credentials facilitates standardization and reduces the risk of unauthorized access.\n\nPIV credentials are only used in an unclassified environment.\n\nDoW has mandated the use of the CAC to support identity management and personal authentication for systems covered under HSPD 12, as well as its use as a primary component of layered protection for national security systems.\n\nThe application server must support the use of PIV credentials to access the management interface and perform management functions.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57503",
            "SV-71779"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001953"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "0c0cf263-39c5-5abb-b847-7ae72dfafde4",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual delivered identity is UDS rather than bundled Keycloak. UDSaccepts X509 credentials at SSO/adminboundary, strips forgedcertificate headers, maps UPN/customcertificate attributes to individualusers, validates status viaOCSP and provisions SHA-verified DoD-approved externalPKItrust chains. This supplies PIV/CAC/partnerfederation mechanisms through delegatedidentity; the APIappropriately consumes its signed OIDC identity instead of parsing smartcards itself. Final approvedagency/profile scope is captured in the inheritance/trust record.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-coreidentity] re-appserver-coreidentity\nCore 1.14 default enables OTP and X509, sets concurrent per-user limit0(unlimited), leaves inactivity workflow opt-in, includes explicit truststorepaths and optional alert enablement.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-flowrender] re-appserver-flowrender\nChart maps OTP_ENABLED=true to OTP_FLOW_ENABLED=REQUIRED and MFA_FLOW_ENABLED=REQUIRED, and maps X509 auth to enabled alternate flow. Raw realm interpolation fallbacks alone are not the final rendered flow.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/secret-kc-realm.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-identitygateway] re-appserver-identitygateway\nUDS routes privateKeycloak paths away from public tenantgateway, exposes admin access separately, strips user-supplied clientcertificate header and injects downstreamcert for delegated X509 handling.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-trust] re-appserver-trust\nImage trust builder defaults officialDoD approved externalPKI trust chains ZIP, requires SHA256 verification, converts .cer files, excludes configured certificate categories, and includes non-expired certificates. This is actual source provisioning, not a generic claim that trust is unknowable.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/truststore/ca-to-jks.sh\n\n[re-appserver-trustsync] re-appserver-trustsync\nIdentityconfig sync copies generated certs into Keycloak conf/truststores and installs realm/plugin/theme configuration. FIPS libraries are copied when configured.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/sync.sh\n\n[re-appserver-auth] re-appserver-auth\nEvery protected request validates OIDC claims and single tenantgroup, resolves stable sub->localuser/tenant, and sets trusted userId/tenantId CLS context. Pending/no-role users are denied.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Actual delivered identity is UDS rather than bundled Keycloak. UDSaccepts X509 credentials at SSO/adminboundary, strips forgedcertificate headers, maps UPN/customcertificate attributes to individualusers, validates status viaOCSP and provisions SHA-verified DoD-approved externalPKItrust chains. This supplies PIV/CAC/partnerfederation mechanisms through delegatedidentity; the APIappropriately consumes its signed OIDC identity instead of parsing smartcards itself. Final approvedagency/profile scope is captured in the inheritance/trust record.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
        },
        {
