          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The actual UDS realm configures finite access-token lifetime, 600-second SSO idle and 36,000-second maximum session lifetime. JWT validation rejects expired credentials and the OIDC client renews or clears unusable state. These implement termination, reauthentication and cached-credential expiry at the delegated provider/client boundary. Any stricter organization-specific immediate trigger/revocation policy remains an explicit residual rather than a claim that all such policies are encoded.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-jwt] re-appserver-jwt\nProtectedAPI verifies JWTsignature/issuer/audience/expiry offline. There is no per-request session revocation check; immediate bearer credential invalidation is a residual beyond ending the IdP/browser interactive session.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.oidc-jwt.service.ts\n\n[re-appserver-logout] re-appserver-logout\nExplicitlogout captures IdP signoutcontext, wipes localOIDC/sessionstate then invokes signoutRedirect. This is a delegated interactive logout implementation, not an absence of logout.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/web/src/auth/auth-store.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The actual UDS realm configures finite access-token lifetime, 600-second SSO idle and 36,000-second maximum session lifetime. JWT validation rejects expired credentials and the OIDC client renews or clears unusable state. These implement termination, reauthentication and cached-credential expiry at the delegated provider/client boundary. Any stricter organization-specific immediate trigger/revocation policy remains an explicit residual rather than a claim that all such policies are encoded.\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204805",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204805",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000401",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204805",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000401",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204805r981694_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204805r981694",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000401-AS-000243",
          "rule_title": "The application server, for PKI-based authentication, must implement a local cache of revocation data to support path discovery and validation in case of the inability to access revocation information via the network.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to implement a local cache of revocation data to support path discovery and validation in case of the inability to access revocation information via the network.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation to ensure the application server provides a PKI integration capability that implements a local cache of revocation data to support path discovery and validation in case of the inability to access revocation information via the network.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to meet this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The cornerstone of the PKI is the private key used to encrypt or digitally sign information. The key by itself is a cryptographic value that does not contain specific user information.\n\nApplication servers must provide the capability to utilize and meet requirements of the DOD Enterprise PKI infrastructure for application authentication, but without configuring a local cache of revocation data, there is the potential to allow access to users who are no longer authorized (users with revoked certificates) when access through the network to the CA is not available.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57511",
            "SV-71787"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004068"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "66f0050e-2dd1-542e-877a-5e74851b5024",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDSX509path enables OCSPfailclosed, but its localCRL/cache check is false by default. This exact disconnectedrevocationcache requirement is not met merely by onlineOCSPsupport. It is a provider trustconfiguration task, not API code. The cache implementation and delivery recipe already exist in Core; the shipped checking flag is off and Gallatin owns mounting/enabling/refreshing it. Human review on 2026-11-10 authorized applying the earlier non-platform decision to matching counterparts. Keep Needs work and share V-222553 verification/remediation where it covers this component. Preserve the exact component scope and applicability rather than treating a shared control as automatic compliance.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Share V-222553 local CRL configuration, refresh/freshness controls and disconnected-validation tests. First verify current PKI-authentication paths and existing cache coverage for this component. Enable/update the existing shared IdP mechanism only where missing and retain effective configuration and valid/revoked/stale-data test evidence. Apply exceptions only where this exact rule supports them; do not copy an ASD-only exception to every component.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-trust] re-appserver-trust\nImage trust builder defaults officialDoD approved externalPKI trust chains ZIP, requires SHA256 verification, converts .cer files, excludes configured certificate categories, and includes non-expired certificates. This is actual source provisioning, not a generic claim that trust is unknowable.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/truststore/ca-to-jks.sh\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-crl] mpn-identity-crl\nCore includes an OCI CRL-volume packaging script and override example: mount current CRLs, set X509_CRL_CHECKING_ENABLED true and filepaths. Strictfreshness requires X509_CRL_ABORT_IF_NON_UPDATED=true; default examplefalse permits expiredCRLs. ImageVolume needs Kubernetes feature support; no customKeycloakcode/image required.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-x509-crl-airgap.mdx\n\n[mpn-identity-crl-script] mpn-identity-crl-script\nGallatin can package approved CRLs with supplied script and deliver updated OCI dataimage beforeCoreidentity component; mountreadOnly via chart extraVolumes/extraVolumeMounts.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/scripts/keycloak-crl-airgap/create-keycloak-crl-oci-volume-package.sh\n\n[mpn-identity-init-only] mpn-identity-init-only\nrealmInitEnv and realmAuthFlows are initial realm import inputs. Existing realm is persisted in DB; redeploying Secret does not change it. Core documents equivalent Keycloak AdminConsole settings. Use controlled non-destructive AdminAPI/console update and persist the desired bundle override; do not advise tearing down production identity.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-keycloak-login-policies.mdx",
          "finding_details": "UDSX509path enables OCSPfailclosed, but its localCRL/cache check is false by default. This exact disconnectedrevocationcache requirement is not met merely by onlineOCSPsupport. It is a provider trustconfiguration task, not API code. The cache implementation and delivery recipe already exist in Core; the shipped checking flag is off and Gallatin owns mounting/enabling/refreshing it. Human review on 2026-11-10 authorized applying the earlier non-platform decision to matching counterparts. Keep Needs work and share V-222553 verification/remediation where it covers this component. Preserve the exact component scope and applicability rather than treating a shared control as automatic compliance.\n\nNext action: Share V-222553 local CRL configuration, refresh/freshness controls and disconnected-validation tests. First verify current PKI-authentication paths and existing cache coverage for this component. Enable/update the existing shared IdP mechanism only where missing and retain effective configuration and valid/revoked/stale-data test evidence. Apply exceptions only where this exact rule supports them; do not copy an ASD-only exception to every component."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204806",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204806",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000402",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204806",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000402",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204806r961527_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204806r961527",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000402-AS-000247",
          "rule_title": "The application server must accept Personal Identity Verification (PIV) credentials from other federal agencies to access the management interface.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to accept PIV credentials from other federal agencies to access the management interface.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and configuration to determine if the application server accepts PIV credentials from other federal agencies to access the management interface.\n\nIf the application server does not accept other federal agency PIV credentials to access the management interface, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Personal Identity Verification (PIV) credentials are those credentials issued by federal agencies that conform to FIPS Publication 201 and supporting guidance documents. OMB Memorandum 11-11 requires federal agencies to continue implementing the requirements specified in HSPD-12 to enable agency-wide use of PIV credentials.  PIV credentials are only used in an unclassified environment.\n\nAccess may be denied to authorized users if federal agency PIV credentials are not accepted to access the management interface.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57515",
            "SV-71791"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002009"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "e917a545-5b39-52b8-85bd-0d3ce27d323c",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual delivered identity is UDS rather than bundled Keycloak. UDSaccepts X509 credentials at SSO/adminboundary, strips forgedcertificate headers, maps UPN/customcertificate attributes to individualusers, validates status viaOCSP and provisions SHA-verified DoD-approved externalPKItrust chains. This supplies PIV/CAC/partnerfederation mechanisms through delegatedidentity; the APIappropriately consumes its signed OIDC identity instead of parsing smartcards itself. Final approvedagency/profile scope is captured in the inheritance/trust record.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-coreidentity] re-appserver-coreidentity\nCore 1.14 default enables OTP and X509, sets concurrent per-user limit0(unlimited), leaves inactivity workflow opt-in, includes explicit truststorepaths and optional alert enablement.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-flowrender] re-appserver-flowrender\nChart maps OTP_ENABLED=true to OTP_FLOW_ENABLED=REQUIRED and MFA_FLOW_ENABLED=REQUIRED, and maps X509 auth to enabled alternate flow. Raw realm interpolation fallbacks alone are not the final rendered flow.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/secret-kc-realm.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-identitygateway] re-appserver-identitygateway\nUDS routes privateKeycloak paths away from public tenantgateway, exposes admin access separately, strips user-supplied clientcertificate header and injects downstreamcert for delegated X509 handling.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-trust] re-appserver-trust\nImage trust builder defaults officialDoD approved externalPKI trust chains ZIP, requires SHA256 verification, converts .cer files, excludes configured certificate categories, and includes non-expired certificates. This is actual source provisioning, not a generic claim that trust is unknowable.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/truststore/ca-to-jks.sh\n\n[re-appserver-trustsync] re-appserver-trustsync\nIdentityconfig sync copies generated certs into Keycloak conf/truststores and installs realm/plugin/theme configuration. FIPS libraries are copied when configured.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/sync.sh\n\n[re-appserver-auth] re-appserver-auth\nEvery protected request validates OIDC claims and single tenantgroup, resolves stable sub->localuser/tenant, and sets trusted userId/tenantId CLS context. Pending/no-role users are denied.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Actual delivered identity is UDS rather than bundled Keycloak. UDSaccepts X509 credentials at SSO/adminboundary, strips forgedcertificate headers, maps UPN/customcertificate attributes to individualusers, validates status viaOCSP and provisions SHA-verified DoD-approved externalPKItrust chains. This supplies PIV/CAC/partnerfederation mechanisms through delegatedidentity; the APIappropriately consumes its signed OIDC identity instead of parsing smartcards itself. Final approvedagency/profile scope is captured in the inheritance/trust record.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204807",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204807",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000403",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204807",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000403",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204807r1265182_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204807r1265182",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000403-AS-000248",
          "rule_title": "The application server must electronically verify Personal Identity Verification (PIV) credentials from other federal agencies to access the management interface.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the unclassified application server to electronically verify PIV credentials from other federal agencies before granting access to the management interface.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "The CAC is the standard DoW authentication token; the PIV is the standard authentication token used by federal/civilian agencies.\n \nIf access to the application server is limited to DoW personnel accessing the system via CAC; and PIV access is not warranted or allowed as per the system security plan, the PIV requirement is NA.\n\nReview the application server documentation and configuration to determine if the application server electronically verifies PIV credentials from other federal agencies to access the management interface.\n\nIf the application server does not electronically verify other federal agency PIV credentials to access the management interface, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Personal Identity Verification (PIV) credentials are those credentials issued by federal agencies that conform to FIPS Publication 201 and supporting guidance documents. OMB Memorandum 11-11 requires federal agencies to continue implementing the requirements specified in HSPD-12 to enable agency-wide use of PIV credentials. PIV credentials are only used in an unclassified environment.\n\nIf PIV credentials are not electronically verified before accessing the management interface, unauthorized users may gain access to the system and data the user has not been granted access to.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71793",
            "V-57517"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002010"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "a22a4839-a34a-5861-9ede-3ccf360e2184",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual delivered identity is UDS rather than bundled Keycloak. UDSaccepts X509 credentials at SSO/adminboundary, strips forgedcertificate headers, maps UPN/customcertificate attributes to individualusers, validates status viaOCSP and provisions SHA-verified DoD-approved externalPKItrust chains. This supplies PIV/CAC/partnerfederation mechanisms through delegatedidentity; the APIappropriately consumes its signed OIDC identity instead of parsing smartcards itself. Final approvedagency/profile scope is captured in the inheritance/trust record.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-coreidentity] re-appserver-coreidentity\nCore 1.14 default enables OTP and X509, sets concurrent per-user limit0(unlimited), leaves inactivity workflow opt-in, includes explicit truststorepaths and optional alert enablement.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-flowrender] re-appserver-flowrender\nChart maps OTP_ENABLED=true to OTP_FLOW_ENABLED=REQUIRED and MFA_FLOW_ENABLED=REQUIRED, and maps X509 auth to enabled alternate flow. Raw realm interpolation fallbacks alone are not the final rendered flow.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/secret-kc-realm.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-identitygateway] re-appserver-identitygateway\nUDS routes privateKeycloak paths away from public tenantgateway, exposes admin access separately, strips user-supplied clientcertificate header and injects downstreamcert for delegated X509 handling.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-trust] re-appserver-trust\nImage trust builder defaults officialDoD approved externalPKI trust chains ZIP, requires SHA256 verification, converts .cer files, excludes configured certificate categories, and includes non-expired certificates. This is actual source provisioning, not a generic claim that trust is unknowable.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/truststore/ca-to-jks.sh\n\n[re-appserver-trustsync] re-appserver-trustsync\nIdentityconfig sync copies generated certs into Keycloak conf/truststores and installs realm/plugin/theme configuration. FIPS libraries are copied when configured.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/sync.sh\n\n[re-appserver-auth] re-appserver-auth\nEvery protected request validates OIDC claims and single tenantgroup, resolves stable sub->localuser/tenant, and sets trusted userId/tenantId CLS context. Pending/no-role users are denied.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Actual delivered identity is UDS rather than bundled Keycloak. UDSaccepts X509 credentials at SSO/adminboundary, strips forgedcertificate headers, maps UPN/customcertificate attributes to individualusers, validates status viaOCSP and provisions SHA-verified DoD-approved externalPKItrust chains. This supplies PIV/CAC/partnerfederation mechanisms through delegatedidentity; the APIappropriately consumes its signed OIDC identity instead of parsing smartcards itself. Final approvedagency/profile scope is captured in the inheritance/trust record.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
