        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204811",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204811",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000427",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204811",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000427",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204811r1265185_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204811r1265185",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000427-AS-000264",
          "rule_title": "The application server must only allow the use of DoW PKI-established certificate authorities for verification of the establishment of protected sessions.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to allow the use of DoW PKI-established certificate authorities for verification of the establishment of protected sessions.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and configuration to determine if the application server only allows the use of DoW PKI-established certificate authorities.\n\nIf the application server allows other certificate authorities for verification, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Untrusted Certificate Authorities (CA) can issue certificates, but they may be issued by organizations or individuals that seek to compromise DoW systems or by organizations with insufficient security controls. If the CA used for verifying the certificate is not a DoW-approved CA, trust of this CA has not been established.\n\nThe DoW will only accept PKI certificates obtained from a DoW-approved internal or external certificate authority. Reliance on CAs for the establishment of secure sessions includes, for example, the use of SSL/TLS certificates. The application server must only allow the use of DoW PKI-established certificate authorities for verification.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71827",
            "V-57551"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002470"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "503a87f5-f42a-56f2-a76d-31da19d96226",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: This check restricts the CAs accepted when verifying protected sessions; it is not solely a question about the gateway leaf certificate class. UDS supplies approved DoD identity trust and configurable trust distribution. Complete the MPN profile with an approved root allow-list at the actual client/gateway verification boundaries, including API JWKS HTTPS. Replace default Node trust rather than only adding CAs. This is a defined Gallatin-owned configuration task; it requires no new TLS implementation inside each API and no DU-managed cluster. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work with verification first: inspect effective trust stores and actual protected-session verifier boundaries, confirm ownership and approved CA scope, then restrict only confirmed gaps and test unrelated-CA rejection. Configured UDS identity trust or additive trust-bundle distribution alone does not establish all applicable verifier restrictions.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE / Gallatin-operated MPN Core platform; mission authority only for mission applicability and CA issuance approval\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, inventory applicable protected-session verifiers, including shared identity/gateway and Horizon outbound signing-key HTTPS. Confirm approved CA scope and ownership, inspect actual effective trust stores and client settings, and test rejection of an unrelated CA. Credit existing restrictive configuration; replace or restrict trust sources only where required rather than merely adding approved roots. Retain public CA fingerprints/approval, configuration and test evidence; reuse supported UDS/runtime trust configuration.\n\nEvidence:\n[solution-topology-trust-doc] solution-topology-trust-doc\nCore distributes uds-trust-bundle ConfigMaps using CA_BUNDLE_CERTS,CA_BUNDLE_INCLUDE_DOD_CERTS,CA_BUNDLE_INCLUDE_PUBLIC_CERTS. Both include flags defaultfalse; settingDoDtrue/publicfalse selectsDoD trustmaterial. Application custommounts are explicit; additive CA configuration does not remove systemroots.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/networking/manage-trust-bundles.mdx\n\n[solution-topology-trust-source] solution-topology-trust-source\nShared identity image builds a checksum-pinnedDoD-approvedCA bundle, excluding expired/email/software certs. This is client-certificateidentity trust evidence, not proof of presented gatewayleafClass3/4.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/truststore/ca-to-jks.sh\n\n[solution-topology-app-jwks] solution-topology-app-jwks\nDelivered API uses createRemoteJWKSet(newURL(jwksUrl)); it does not supply a per-clientexplicitCA option. Configuring only Keycloakclienttrust is not sufficient to prove APIoutboundTLS restrictedCAroots.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.oidc-jwt.service.ts\n\n[solution-topology-node-ca] solution-topology-node-ca\nNode can replace its defaultCA source using --use-openssl-ca and SSL_CERT_FILE/SSL_CERT_DIR; NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS only extends built-intrust. Explicit clientTLS ca also replaces roots for thatclient. This is a source-defined enforcement path for APIoutboundverification.\nhttps://nodejs.org/api/cli.html#--use-bundled-ca---use-openssl-ca\n\n[solution-topology-istio-secret] solution-topology-istio-secret\nOfficial GatewayAPI supports serverTLS Secret keys tls.crt,tls.key and trustedclientCA/cacert plus CRL; credentialName selects namespace-local certificate material. TLSterminationcanstayatthegateway instead of duplicating in everyAPIcontainer.\nhttps://istio.io/latest/docs/reference/config/networking/gateway/\n\n[solution-topology-dod-ca] solution-topology-dod-ca\nDoD publishes approvedexternalPKI assurance profiles; approvalmustmaptoactual issuer/policy rather than assume any publicDVcertificate or FIPSimage is an approved servercertificate.\nhttps://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/pki-pke/pdf/unclass-dod_approved_external_pkis_master_document.pdf",
          "finding_details": "This check restricts the CAs accepted when verifying protected sessions; it is not solely a question about the gateway leaf certificate class. UDS supplies approved DoD identity trust and configurable trust distribution. Complete the MPN profile with an approved root allow-list at the actual client/gateway verification boundaries, including API JWKS HTTPS. Replace default Node trust rather than only adding CAs. This is a defined Gallatin-owned configuration task; it requires no new TLS implementation inside each API and no DU-managed cluster. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work with verification first: inspect effective trust stores and actual protected-session verifier boundaries, confirm ownership and approved CA scope, then restrict only confirmed gaps and test unrelated-CA rejection. Configured UDS identity trust or additive trust-bundle distribution alone does not establish all applicable verifier restrictions.\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, inventory applicable protected-session verifiers, including shared identity/gateway and Horizon outbound signing-key HTTPS. Confirm approved CA scope and ownership, inspect actual effective trust stores and client settings, and test rejection of an unrelated CA. Credit existing restrictive configuration; replace or restrict trust sources only where required rather than merely adding approved roots. Retain public CA fingerprints/approval, configuration and test evidence; reuse supported UDS/runtime trust configuration."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204812",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204812",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000428",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204812",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000428",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204812r1067567_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204812r1067567",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000428-AS-000265",
          "rule_title": "The application server must implement cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized modification of organization-defined information at rest on organization-defined information system components.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to implement cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized modification of organization-defined information at rest on organization-defined information system components.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation and configuration to determine if the application server implements cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized modification of organization-defined information at rest on organization-defined information system components.\n\nIf the application server does not implement cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized modification, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Information at rest refers to the state of information when it is located on a secondary storage device (e.g., disk drive, tape drive) within an application server. Alternative physical protection measures include protected distribution systems.\n\nTo prevent unauthorized disclosure or modification of the information, application servers must protect data at rest by using cryptographic mechanisms.\n\nSelection of a cryptographic mechanism is based on the need to protect the integrity of organizational information. The strength of the mechanism is commensurate with the security category and/or classification of the information. Organizations have the flexibility to either encrypt all information on storage devices (i.e., full disk encryption) or encrypt specific data structures (e.g., files, records, or fields).",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71833",
            "V-57557"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002475"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "7e464ad8-6dc4-549f-b863-067f0a567c3e",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Human review on 2026-11-10 selected provisional Likely covered through inherited MPN/platform storage controls under the instruction to apply the same recommendation to similar storage-protection requirements. PR reviewer must confirm or reject platform ownership and the mapping for cryptographic integrity. Actual platform protection and the authoritative responsibility boundary remain unverified; this is not an accepted compliance result or an N/A determination. Gallatin retains application data requirements, use of approved protected storage and any application/configuration residuals. This supersedes any earlier assumption that Gallatin must provision a new storage layer.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: MPN/platform storage owner (proposed; reviewer confirmation required); Gallatin for application requirements/configuration and residuals\n\nNext action: PR reviewer must confirm or reject MPN/platform ownership and inherited coverage for cryptographic integrity. In the post-classification verification pass, obtain the responsibility agreement and evidence for the actual protection, map all data/components covered by this exact rule, and verify Horizon uses the protected boundary correctly. Identify any Gallatin application/configuration residual separately and revise the recommendation if inheritance or protection is unsupported. Do not prescribe new storage infrastructure before that verification.\n\nEvidence:\n[sol-storage-seaweed-aead] sol-storage-seaweed-aead\nExact delivered SeaweedFS4.52 cipher code generates32-byte random keys, AES-GCM encryption/nonces and authenticated gcm.Open decryption. This is an existing object-storage mechanism, not new API cryptography.\nhttps://github.com/seaweedfs/seaweedfs/blob/4.52/weed/util/cipher.go\n\n[sol-storage-dm-auth-integrity] sol-storage-dm-auth-integrity\nLinux dm-integrity with dm-crypt supports authenticated disk encryption that rejects modified encrypted sectors with I/O error. Standalone CRC mode only detects accidental corruption; journal/mode choices affect consistency and performance. Distribution/FIPS-module support must be selected, not assumed.\nhttps://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/admin-guide/device-mapper/dm-integrity.html\n\n[sol-storage-pg-official] sol-storage-pg-official\nPostgreSQL18 explicitly supports filesystem/block/full-disk encryption as a data-partition protection layer. Mounted filesystem access still returns plaintext; this does not defeat privileged server access or replace authorization/integrity controls.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/encryption-options.html\n\n[sol-storage-dm-auth-modes] sol-storage-dm-auth-modes\nKernel dm-crypt documents authenticated modes such as gcm(aes) and authenc(hmac(sha256),xts(aes)) using integrity:aead metadata. Plain AES-XTS is not this authenticated mode. Existing host functionality can supply integrity without adding custom DB crypto, when supported in the selected approved platform.\nhttps://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/admin-guide/device-mapper/dm-crypt.html",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Human review on 2026-11-10 selected provisional Likely covered through inherited MPN/platform storage controls under the instruction to apply the same recommendation to similar storage-protection requirements. PR reviewer must confirm or reject platform ownership and the mapping for cryptographic integrity. Actual platform protection and the authoritative responsibility boundary remain unverified; this is not an accepted compliance result or an N/A determination. Gallatin retains application data requirements, use of approved protected storage and any application/configuration residuals. This supersedes any earlier assumption that Gallatin must provision a new storage layer.\n\nNext action: PR reviewer must confirm or reject MPN/platform ownership and inherited coverage for cryptographic integrity. In the post-classification verification pass, obtain the responsibility agreement and evidence for the actual protection, map all data/components covered by this exact rule, and verify Horizon uses the protected boundary correctly. Identify any Gallatin application/configuration residual separately and revise the recommendation if inheritance or protection is unsupported. Do not prescribe new storage infrastructure before that verification."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204813",
