          "group_id": "V-204813",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000429",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204813r1067568_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204813r1067568",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000429-AS-000157",
          "rule_title": "The application must implement cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized disclosure of organization-defined information at rest on organization-defined information system components.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to implement cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized disclosure of organization-defined information at rest on organization-defined information system components.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation and configuration to determine if the application server implements cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized disclosure of organization-defined information at rest on organization-defined information system components.\n\nIf the application server does not implement cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized disclosure, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Information at rest refers to the state of information when it is located on a secondary storage device (e.g., disk drive, tape drive) within an application server. Alternative physical protection measures include protected distribution systems.\n\nTo prevent unauthorized disclosure or modification of the information, application servers must protect data at rest by using cryptographic mechanisms.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57559",
            "SV-71835"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002476"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "1667b492-42fd-5538-acf4-feb3b1394505",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Human review on 2026-11-10 selected provisional Likely covered through inherited MPN/platform storage controls under the instruction to apply the same recommendation to similar storage-protection requirements. PR reviewer must confirm or reject platform ownership and the mapping for storage confidentiality and integrity as required by the exact rule. Actual platform protection and the authoritative responsibility boundary remain unverified; this is not an accepted compliance result or an N/A determination. Gallatin retains application data requirements, use of approved protected storage and any application/configuration residuals. This supersedes any earlier assumption that Gallatin must provision a new storage layer.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: MPN/platform storage owner (proposed; reviewer confirmation required); Gallatin for application requirements/configuration and residuals\n\nNext action: PR reviewer must confirm or reject MPN/platform ownership and inherited coverage for storage confidentiality and integrity as required by the exact rule. In the post-classification verification pass, obtain the responsibility agreement and evidence for the actual protection, map all data/components covered by this exact rule, and verify Horizon uses the protected boundary correctly. Identify any Gallatin application/configuration residual separately and revise the recommendation if inheritance or protection is unsupported. Do not prescribe new storage infrastructure before that verification.\n\nEvidence:\n[sol-storage-postgres-pvc] sol-storage-postgres-pvc\nPostgreSQL mounts PGDATA on its own PVC and renders persistence.storageClassName when nonempty. Correct chart key is storageClassName; source/values/postgres.yaml uses storageClass, so changing that unmatched field would not select an encrypted class.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/templates/statefulset.yaml\n\n[sol-storage-seaweed-chart] sol-storage-seaweed-chart\nCurrent chart runs weed server -s3 -s3.config=... without data-encryption flags; a mounted dataPVC supplies its persistence. Its PVC template currently has no storageClassName field, so add optional class binding or deliberately choose the cluster default.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/seaweedfs/templates/deployment.yaml\n\n[sol-storage-pg-official] sol-storage-pg-official\nPostgreSQL18 explicitly supports filesystem/block/full-disk encryption as a data-partition protection layer. Mounted filesystem access still returns plaintext; this does not defeat privileged server access or replace authorization/integrity controls.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/encryption-options.html\n\n[sol-storage-core-boundary] sol-storage-core-boundary\nCore expects an existing cluster and defaultStorageClass; it does not provision MPN host disk encryption. Gallatin owns this infrastructure choice and can bind the app/CorePVCs to a selected encrypted storage class.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/getting-started/production/prerequisites.mdx\n\n[sol-storage-seaweed-aead] sol-storage-seaweed-aead\nExact delivered SeaweedFS4.52 cipher code generates32-byte random keys, AES-GCM encryption/nonces and authenticated gcm.Open decryption. This is an existing object-storage mechanism, not new API cryptography.\nhttps://github.com/seaweedfs/seaweedfs/blob/4.52/weed/util/cipher.go\n\n[sol-storage-seaweed-flags] sol-storage-seaweed-flags\nExact4.52 server exposes filer.encryptVolumeData and s3.encryptVolumeData flags, both defaultfalse. Enabling these existing paths provides volume-data encryption for filer and S3 uploads; it does not encrypt all filer metadata automatically.\nhttps://github.com/seaweedfs/seaweedfs/blob/4.52/weed/command/server.go",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Human review on 2026-11-10 selected provisional Likely covered through inherited MPN/platform storage controls under the instruction to apply the same recommendation to similar storage-protection requirements. PR reviewer must confirm or reject platform ownership and the mapping for storage confidentiality and integrity as required by the exact rule. Actual platform protection and the authoritative responsibility boundary remain unverified; this is not an accepted compliance result or an N/A determination. Gallatin retains application data requirements, use of approved protected storage and any application/configuration residuals. This supersedes any earlier assumption that Gallatin must provision a new storage layer.\n\nNext action: PR reviewer must confirm or reject MPN/platform ownership and inherited coverage for storage confidentiality and integrity as required by the exact rule. In the post-classification verification pass, obtain the responsibility agreement and evidence for the actual protection, map all data/components covered by this exact rule, and verify Horizon uses the protected boundary correctly. Identify any Gallatin application/configuration residual separately and revise the recommendation if inheritance or protection is unsupported. Do not prescribe new storage infrastructure before that verification."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204814",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204814",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000435",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204814",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000435",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204814r961620_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204814r961620",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000435-AS-000069",
          "rule_title": "The application server, when a MAC I system, must be in a high-availability (HA) cluster.",
          "fix_text": "If the application server is not a MAC I system, this requirement is NA.\n\nConfigure the application server to be part of an HA cluster.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If the application server is not a MAC I system, this requirement is NA.\n\nReview the application server documentation and configuration to determine if the application server is part of an HA cluster.\n\nIf the application server is not part of an HA cluster, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "A MAC I system is a system that handles data vital to the organization's operational readiness or effectiveness of deployed or contingency forces.  A MAC I system must maintain the highest level of integrity and availability.  By HA clustering the application server, the hosted application and data are given a platform that is load-balanced and provided high-availability.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71807",
            "V-57531"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002385"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "d76bcc7e-ff84-5f61-9cfa-f9fe2a480098",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Unclear\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Applicability still depends on the mission declaring MAC I; code cannot select that designation. The implementation is now defined: a MAC I profile needs multiple failure domains, at least two web/API replicas with disruption/placement controls, and HA stateful, storage and identity dependencies. Current source describes single-node PostgreSQL and does not declare a completed MAC I HA cluster. If MAC I applies, the specified profile needs implementation; otherwise the exact check permits N/A.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE / Gallatin-operated MPN Core platform; mission authority only for mission applicability and CA issuance approval\n\nNext action: Record the single MAC I applicability decision. If true, implement the concrete HA profile; if false, record accepted N/A. Gallatin operates Core and owns the configuration.\n\nEvidence:\n[solution-topology-deploy] solution-topology-deploy\nCommon web/API/worker Deployment supports replicas/HPA, readiness/liveness, affinity and topologySpreadConstraints, with customenv+volume mounts for concrete profile configurations.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-service/templates/deployment.yaml\n\n[solution-topology-ha-values] solution-topology-ha-values\nCommon chart defaults replicaCount1,autoscalingdisabled,pdbdisabled but supportsreplicaCount,HPAminimums,pdb.minAvailable,affinity and topologySpreadConstraints. Built-in redundancy capability is distinct from a currentlyconfiguredHAdeployment.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-service/values.yaml\n\n[solution-topology-pdb] solution-topology-pdb\nPDB template supports minimumAvailable or maximumUnavailable for selected applicationpods. Budgetonlylimitsvoluntarydisruption; itdoesnotmake a singlepodhighavailable.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-service/templates/pdb.yaml\n\n[solution-topology-postgres] solution-topology-postgres\nSelected source explicitly describes single-nodein-clusterPostgreSQL; adding statelessreplicas alone cannot establish statefuldatafailover or MACIclusteravailability.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/values/postgres.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Unclear. Applicability still depends on the mission declaring MAC I; code cannot select that designation. The implementation is now defined: a MAC I profile needs multiple failure domains, at least two web/API replicas with disruption/placement controls, and HA stateful, storage and identity dependencies. Current source describes single-node PostgreSQL and does not declare a completed MAC I HA cluster. If MAC I applies, the specified profile needs implementation; otherwise the exact check permits N/A.\n\nNext action: Record the single MAC I applicability decision. If true, implement the concrete HA profile; if false, record accepted N/A. Gallatin operates Core and owns the configuration."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204815",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204815",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000435",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204815",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000435",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204815r961620_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204815r961620",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000435-AS-000163",
          "rule_title": "The application server must protect against or limit the effects of all types of Denial of Service (DoS) attacks by employing organization-defined security safeguards.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to protect against or limit the effects of all types of Denial of Service (DoS) attacks by employing defined security safeguards.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation and configuration to determine if the application server can protect against or limit the effects of all types of Denial of Service (DoS) attacks by employing defined security safeguards.\n\nIf the application server cannot be configured to protect against or limit the effects of all types of DoS, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "DoS is a condition when a resource is not available for legitimate users.  When this occurs, the organization either cannot accomplish its mission or must operate at degraded capacity.  To reduce the possibility or effect of a DoS, the application server must employ defined security safeguards.  These safeguards will be determined by the placement of the application server and the type of applications being hosted within the application server framework.\n\nThere are many examples of technologies that exist to limit or, in some cases, eliminate the effects of DoS attacks (e.g., limiting processes or restricting the number of sessions the application opens at one time). Employing increased capacity and bandwidth, combined with service redundancy or clustering, may reduce the susceptibility to some DoS attacks.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57529",
            "SV-71805"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002385"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "26a2b265-691d-518d-a8c0-8ff1ee8eefba",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS isolates namespaces/services, denies unapproved host/privileged access and ships resource-constrained app containers plus Falco monitoring. These are explicit DoS-limiting mechanisms; missing init-container resource settings are separately tracked.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[app-pods] app-pods\nCandidate service chart applies non-root/read-only/no-escalation contexts, Secret references, probes and resource settings. Rendered app pods corroborate those settings; some init containers lack explicit resources.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-service/templates/deployment.yaml\n\n[core-falco] core-falco\nFalco loads shipped rule sets, detects runtime behavior and ships Sidekick events to Loki. Alertmanager delivery is commented out; detection is not automatic access revocation.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS isolates namespaces/services, denies unapproved host/privileged access and ships resource-constrained app containers plus Falco monitoring. These are explicit DoS-limiting mechanisms; missing init-container resource settings are separately tracked.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204816",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204816",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000439",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204816",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000439",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204816r961632_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204816r961632",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000439-AS-000155",
          "rule_title": "The application server must protect the confidentiality and integrity of transmitted information through the use of an approved TLS version.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to use a FIPS-2 approved TLS version to maintain the confidentiality and integrity of transmitted information and to disable all non-FIPS-approved SSL versions.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and deployed configuration to determine which version of TLS is being used.\n\nIf the application server is not using TLS to maintain the confidentiality and integrity of transmitted information or non-FIPS-approved SSL versions are enabled, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Preventing the disclosure of transmitted information requires that the application server take measures to employ some form of cryptographic mechanism in order to protect the information during transmission.  This is usually achieved through the use of Transport Layer Security (TLS).\n\nTransmission of data can take place between the application server and a large number of devices/applications external to the application server.  Examples are a web client used by a user, a backend database, a log server, or other application servers in an application server cluster.\n\nIf data is transmitted unencrypted, the data then becomes vulnerable to disclosure.  The disclosure may reveal user identifier/password combinations, website code revealing business logic, or other user personal information.\n\nTLS must be enabled and non-FIPS-approved SSL versions must be disabled.  NIST SP 800-52 specifies the preferred configurations for government systems.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57533",
            "SV-71809"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002418"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "b766650d-8ec9-5de9-b3e9-fb46478ef202",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS gateway enforces HTTPS/TLS1.3-or-1.2 and root STRICT mTLS protects the enrolled workload hops; Horizon opts into ambient mesh. This supports source inference of transit protection, with approved-crypto module claims assessed separately.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[core-gateway] core-gateway\nGateway redirects HTTP to HTTPS and requires TLS 1.3 by default, optionally TLS 1.2; credentialName references Kubernetes TLS secrets.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/charts/uds-istio-config/templates/gateway.yaml\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS gateway enforces HTTPS/TLS1.3-or-1.2 and root STRICT mTLS protects the enrolled workload hops; Horizon opts into ambient mesh. This supports source inference of transit protection, with approved-crypto module claims assessed separately.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204817",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204817",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000439",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204817",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000439",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204817r961632_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204817r961632",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000439-AS-000274",
          "rule_title": "The application server must remove all export ciphers to protect the confidentiality and integrity of transmitted information.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to have export ciphers removed.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and deployed configuration to determine if export ciphers are removed.\n\nIf the application server does not have the export ciphers removed, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "During the initial setup of a Transport Layer Security (TLS) connection to the application server, the client sends a list of supported cipher suites in order of preference.  The application server will reply with the cipher suite it will use for communication from the client list.  If an attacker can intercept the submission of cipher suites to the application server and place, as the preferred cipher suite, a weak export suite, the encryption used for the session becomes easy for the attacker to break, often within minutes to hours.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
