          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Privileged role grant/revocation already records individual actor, targetsubject, role/action and time in the append-only audit store; UDS IdP admin records and platformnode/containerlogs cover their own management boundary. This satisfies the record generation/individual-identity mechanism without requiring a duplicate application serverconsole recorder. Failedprivilege eventcoverage is separately flagged under204719/824/825.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-roleaudit] re-appserver-roleaudit\nRole grant/revocation writes an actor+subject+tenant+action+role audit record, transactionally coupled with mutation. Failed preconditions throw before insert; failed transaction paths do not retain successful audit rows.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/users/user-roles.service.ts\n\n[re-appserver-auditfields] re-appserver-auditfields\nAppendonly permission audit table records actor_user_id, subject_user_id, tenant_id, action, role and timestamp. This is valid audit evidence even though it is a database record rather than Pino JSON.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-logging] re-appserver-logging\nVector collects pod/node records with namespace/app/container/host identifiers, acknowledged diskbuffers and Loki sinks, exports internal metrics and is monitored. It collects process/container identity independent of Horizonrequest actors.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Privileged role grant/revocation already records individual actor, targetsubject, role/action and time in the append-only audit store; UDS IdP admin records and platformnode/containerlogs cover their own management boundary. This satisfies the record generation/individual-identity mechanism without requiring a duplicate application serverconsole recorder. Failedprivilege eventcoverage is separately flagged under204719/824/825.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204828",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204828",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000505",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204828",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000505",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204828r961830_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204828r961830",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000505-AS-000230",
          "rule_title": "The application must generate log records showing starting and ending times for user access to the application server management interface.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to generate log records showing starting and ending times of user access to the management interface.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and the system configuration to determine if the application server generates log records showing starting and ending times for user access to the management interface.\n\nIf log records are not generated showing starting and ending times of user access to the management interface, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Determining when a user has accessed the management interface is important to determine the timeline of events when a security incident occurs.  Generating these events, especially if the management interface is accessed via a stateless protocol like HTTP, the log events will be generated when the user performs a logon (start) and when the user performs a logoff (end).  Without these events, the user and later investigators cannot determine the sequence of events and therefore cannot determine what may have happened and by whom it may have been done.\n\nThe generation of start and end times within log events allow the user to perform their due diligence in the event of a security breach.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71757",
            "V-57481"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000172"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "4dcd686e-cd1d-5539-bcd6-07d4f47df55d",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual UDS identity emits login/logout and other user/admin events with original time, session and source-address fields through its JSON listener. The common Vector/Loki collector retains records across sessions and workstations, supporting start/end and concurrent-logon correlation. The previously cited bundled event-store forwarding gap does not exist in the delivered UDS identity path.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-idlistener] re-appserver-idlistener\nActual UDS identity listener handles both UserEvent and AdminEvent, serializes original event fields plus event type and UTC timestamp, and prints JSON to stdout. Vector can collect the actual provider records directly; no bundled identity database forwarder is needed.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[re-appserver-logging] re-appserver-logging\nVector collects pod/node records with namespace/app/container/host identifiers, acknowledged diskbuffers and Loki sinks, exports internal metrics and is monitored. It collects process/container identity independent of Horizonrequest actors.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Actual UDS identity emits login/logout and other user/admin events with original time, session and source-address fields through its JSON listener. The common Vector/Loki collector retains records across sessions and workstations, supporting start/end and concurrent-logon correlation. The previously cited bundled event-store forwarding gap does not exist in the delivered UDS identity path.\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204829",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204829",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000506",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204829",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000506",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204829r961833_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204829r961833",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000506-AS-000231",
          "rule_title": "The application server must generate log records when concurrent logons from different workstations occur to the application server management interface.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to generate log records showing concurrent logons from different workstations to the management interface.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and the system configuration to determine if the application server generates log records showing concurrent logons from different workstations to the management interface.\n\nIf concurrent logons from different workstations are not logged, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Being able to work on a system through multiple views into the application allows a user to work more efficiently and more accurately.  Before environments with windowing capabilities or multiple desktops, a user would log onto the application from different workstations or terminals.  With today's workstations, this is no longer necessary and may signal a compromised session or user account.\n\nWhen concurrent logons are made from different workstations to the management interface, a log record needs to be generated.  This allows the system administrator to investigate the incident and to be aware of the incident.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71759",
            "V-57483"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000172"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "2c3856bb-ea42-5c8d-a26d-d3b79871cc5f",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual UDS identity emits login/logout and other user/admin events with original time, session and source-address fields through its JSON listener. The common Vector/Loki collector retains records across sessions and workstations, supporting start/end and concurrent-logon correlation. The previously cited bundled event-store forwarding gap does not exist in the delivered UDS identity path.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-idlistener] re-appserver-idlistener\nActual UDS identity listener handles both UserEvent and AdminEvent, serializes original event fields plus event type and UTC timestamp, and prints JSON to stdout. Vector can collect the actual provider records directly; no bundled identity database forwarder is needed.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[re-appserver-logging] re-appserver-logging\nVector collects pod/node records with namespace/app/container/host identifiers, acknowledged diskbuffers and Loki sinks, exports internal metrics and is monitored. It collects process/container identity independent of Horizonrequest actors.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Actual UDS identity emits login/logout and other user/admin events with original time, session and source-address fields through its JSON listener. The common Vector/Loki collector retains records across sessions and workstations, supporting start/end and concurrent-logon correlation. The previously cited bundled event-store forwarding gap does not exist in the delivered UDS identity path.\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204830",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204830",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000509",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204830",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000509",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204830r961842_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204830r961842",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000509-AS-000234",
          "rule_title": "The application server must generate log records for all account creations, modifications, disabling, and termination events.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to generate log records when accounts are created, modified, disabled, or terminated.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and the system configuration to determine if the application server generates log records when accounts are created, modified, disabled, or terminated.\n\nIf the application server does not generate log records for account creation, modification, disabling, and termination, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The maintenance of user accounts is a key activity within the system to determine access and privileges.  Through changes to accounts, an attacker can create an account for persistent access, modify an account to elevate privileges or terminate/disable an account(s) to cause a DoS for user(s).  To be able to track and investigate these actions, log records must be generated for any account modification functions.\n\nApplication servers either provide a local user store, or they can integrate with enterprise user stores like LDAP.  As such, the application server must be able to generate log records on account creation, modification, disabling, and termination.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71761",
            "V-57485"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000172"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "8092e65d-c4e4-57e7-8ddb-751b048e49c4",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The delivered release uses UDS identity. Its realm enables user/admin events, and the JSON listener emits original event type, actor/session/source details and UTC time to stdout, which the existing Vector/Loki path collects. API request/process events and platform lifecycle records provide their respective access/startup/shutdown sources. There is no bundled-Keycloak event-store forwarding dependency in this package.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-idlistener] re-appserver-idlistener\nActual UDS identity listener handles both UserEvent and AdminEvent, serializes original event fields plus event type and UTC timestamp, and prints JSON to stdout. Vector can collect the actual provider records directly; no bundled identity database forwarder is needed.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[re-appserver-logging] re-appserver-logging\nVector collects pod/node records with namespace/app/container/host identifiers, acknowledged diskbuffers and Loki sinks, exports internal metrics and is monitored. It collects process/container identity independent of Horizonrequest actors.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-logger] re-appserver-logger\nPino records stable serviceidentity, trace/requestIDs, requestmethod/URL and outcome, with secretredaction. Its commonbindings do not attach authenticated userId; not every per-domain failure can be assigned to a user from that record alone.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/observability/logging.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The delivered release uses UDS identity. Its realm enables user/admin events, and the JSON listener emits original event type, actor/session/source details and UTC time to stdout, which the existing Vector/Loki path collects. API request/process events and platform lifecycle records provide their respective access/startup/shutdown sources. There is no bundled-Keycloak event-store forwarding dependency in this package.\n\nNext action: Retain the actual UDS identity and delivered-release component mapping in the checklist; no bundled identity remediation is recommended."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204831",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204831",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000514",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204831",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000514",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204831r1137582_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204831r1137582",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000514-AS-000136",
          "rule_title": "Application servers must use NIST-approved or NSA-approved key management technology and processes.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to utilize NIST-approved or NSA-approved key management technology when the application server produces, controls, and distributes symmetric and asymmetric cryptographic keys.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server configuration and the NIST FIPS certificate to validate the application server uses NIST-approved or NSA-approved key management technology and processes when producing, controlling or distributing symmetric and asymmetric keys.\n\nIf the application server does not use this NIST-approved or NSA-approved key management technology and processes, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "An asymmetric encryption key must be protected during transmission. The public portion of an asymmetric key pair can be freely distributed without fear of compromise, and the private portion of the key must be protected. The application server will provide software libraries that applications can programmatically utilize to encrypt and decrypt information. These application server libraries must use NIST-approved or NSA-approved key management technology and processes when producing, controlling, or distributing symmetric and asymmetric keys.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-57543",
            "SV-71819"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002450"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "9a265cea-1ab6-5c70-bfdc-9515675d8656",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual authentication and workload crypto are delegated to UDS strict-FIPS identity and mesh certificate/Secret lifecycle; application crypto uses exact validated FIPS runtime modules. Key management belongs to those service/PKI and protected Secret boundaries, rather than an unimplemented API-local key-management service. This is a platform key-management mapping, not a claim that every vendored crypto primitive is validated; the actual NATS pure-JavaScript authentication signature exception is separately tracked under V204758.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the platform PKI/Secret/key lifecycle mapping and approved trust anchors; assess organizational key-custody policy separately.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-images-release] re-images-release\nSuccessful Nov5 trusted release2026.45.4-uds.1 binds Horizonb84b9af/build19710/run42109769948, package d528b7f, Core1.14.0, actual identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. Twelve delivered image platform digests are explicit; no bundled Keycloak is shipped. Published package digest sha256:486f55939f94ab081d2a12ecade5439512f8f7c4b3119c40b027c711d386adc9. Read build-info/platform-contract/publication metadata artifact13464284834, not just example chart defaults.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-images-core-identity] re-images-core-identity\nCore1.14 always starts Keycloak --features=fips --fips-mode=strict. identity-config0.32 stages bc-fips/bctls/bcutil jars via keycloak-crypto-fips1402 and syncs these providers; supported hardened flavors exist. Actual contract selects this shared identity, so web/API session and authentication crypto belongs here, not missing bundled Keycloak settings. Actual Horizon contract does not select the Core distribution flavor.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/statefulset.yaml\n\n[re-images-identity-libs] re-images-identity-libs\nIdentity-config stages required BouncyCastle FIPS provider jars from its version-matched Keycloak FIPS dependency; sync.sh copies these into Keycloak provider directory when FIPS_ENABLED.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/Dockerfile\n\n[re-images-api-sbom] re-images-api-sbom\nActual built API SBOM contains nodejs24.22.1-r2, NIST-CMVP-5132, openssl-provider-fips-3.4.0 version3.4.0-r5, openssl-config-fipshardened4.1.0-r0. NIST-CMVP-5523-optin is also present but is not the default provider.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/actions/runs/42109769948/artifacts/13453332302\n\n[re-images-nist5132] re-images-nist5132\nNIST lists Chainguard FIPS Provider for OpenSSL, FIPS140-3 certificate5132, Active, sunset2031-01-13, approved-mode caveat. Exact API/web/Postgres SBOM markers map to this certificate; no certificate number guessed from generic FIPS naming.\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-module-validation-program/certificate/5132",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Actual authentication and workload crypto are delegated to UDS strict-FIPS identity and mesh certificate/Secret lifecycle; application crypto uses exact validated FIPS runtime modules. Key management belongs to those service/PKI and protected Secret boundaries, rather than an unimplemented API-local key-management service. This is a platform key-management mapping, not a claim that every vendored crypto primitive is validated; the actual NATS pure-JavaScript authentication signature exception is separately tracked under V204758.\n\nNext action: Retain the platform PKI/Secret/key lifecycle mapping and approved trust anchors; assess organizational key-custody policy separately."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204832",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204832",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000514",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204832",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000514",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204832r1265188_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204832r1265188",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000514-AS-000137",
          "rule_title": "The application server must use DoW or CNSS-approved PKI Class 3 or Class 4 certificates.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to use DoW or CNSS-approved Class 3 or Class 4 PKI certificates.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server configuration to determine if the application server utilizes approved PKI Class 3 or Class 4 certificates.\n\nIf the application server is not configured to use approved DoW or CNS certificates, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Class 3 PKI certificates are used for servers and software signing rather than for identifying individuals. Class 4 certificates are used for business-to-business transactions. Utilizing unapproved certificates not issued or approved by DoW or CNS creates an integrity risk. The application server must utilize approved DoW or CNS Class 3 or Class 4 certificates for software signing and business-to-business transactions.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71821",
            "V-57545"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002450"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "333fd8de-88ae-554d-a293-58960b88e891",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS supports operator-provided certificates at its gateways, but the saved evidence does not establish the actual MPN certificates' issuer, policy/assurance profile or approval. A configured Secret reference alone establishes neither compliance nor failure of an unseen certificate. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work with verification first: confirm the required approved profile, deployed certificate details and ownership/approval evidence before assigning replacement work. Reuse existing gateway termination rather than introduce duplicate application TLS.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE / Gallatin-operated MPN Core platform; mission authority only for mission applicability and CA issuance approval\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, confirm certificate issuance/deployment ownership and the applicable DoW/CNSS Class 3/Class 4 or explicitly accepted profile. Inspect public certificate chains, issuer/policy identifiers, SANs, validity and approval records for applicable gateway/application-server uses. Credit supported existing certificates; arrange issuance or replacements only for confirmed gaps through the authorized mission process. Retain public fingerprints, approval and rotation-owner references without exporting private keys.\n\nEvidence:\n[solution-topology-tls-doc] solution-topology-tls-doc\nCore1.14supports operator-provisioned PEMfullchain+key via deploytimeTLSvariables or existing gateway-namespace KubernetesSecret selected by tls.credentialName. Both tenant/admin gateways can be configured by Gallatin; no DefenseUnicorns-managedcluster or certificate service is required.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/networking/configure-tls-certificates.mdx\n\n[solution-topology-tls-template] solution-topology-tls-template\nGateway HTTPS443 references explicit tls.credentialName and supportsSIMPLE/MUTUAL/OPTIONAL_MUTUAL TLS modes with minimumTLS1.3. Per-servercredentialName also supported. Secret reference is not a claim about the CA/class of its unseen leaf certificate.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/charts/uds-istio-config/templates/gateway.yaml\n\n[solution-topology-istio-secret] solution-topology-istio-secret\nOfficial GatewayAPI supports serverTLS Secret keys tls.crt,tls.key and trustedclientCA/cacert plus CRL; credentialName selects namespace-local certificate material. TLSterminationcanstayatthegateway instead of duplicating in everyAPIcontainer.\nhttps://istio.io/latest/docs/reference/config/networking/gateway/\n\n[solution-topology-dod-ca] solution-topology-dod-ca\nDoD publishes approvedexternalPKI assurance profiles; approvalmustmaptoactual issuer/policy rather than assume any publicDVcertificate or FIPSimage is an approved servercertificate.\nhttps://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/pki-pke/pdf/unclass-dod_approved_external_pkis_master_document.pdf\n\n[solution-topology-dod-commercial] solution-topology-dod-commercial\nOfficialDoD guidance permits selectedcommercialPKI forpublicfacingservers subjecttoDoDapprovedexternalPKI policy; commercialissuername alone neither proves nor disproves approval. Productionissuance/assurance mustbe recorded.\nhttps://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/pki-pke/pdf/unclass-ss_using_commercial_pki_certificates.pdf\n\n[solution-topology-dod-npe] solution-topology-dod-npe\nDoD PKI/PKE provides Non-PersonEntity device/servicecertificate issuance guidance. Gallatin can prepare keys/CSRs and route issuance through the missionauthorizedRA/NPEprocess; no requestforDUclusterattestation is needed.\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/pki-pke",
          "finding_details": "UDS supports operator-provided certificates at its gateways, but the saved evidence does not establish the actual MPN certificates' issuer, policy/assurance profile or approval. A configured Secret reference alone establishes neither compliance nor failure of an unseen certificate. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work with verification first: confirm the required approved profile, deployed certificate details and ownership/approval evidence before assigning replacement work. Reuse existing gateway termination rather than introduce duplicate application TLS.\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, confirm certificate issuance/deployment ownership and the applicable DoW/CNSS Class 3/Class 4 or explicitly accepted profile. Inspect public certificate chains, issuer/policy identifiers, SANs, validity and approval records for applicable gateway/application-server uses. Credit supported existing certificates; arrange issuance or replacements only for confirmed gaps through the authorized mission process. Retain public fingerprints, approval and rotation-owner references without exporting private keys."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204833",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204833",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000515",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204833",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000515",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204833r1265166_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204833r1265166",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000515-AS-000203",
          "rule_title": "The application server must, at a minimum, transfer the logs of interconnected systems in real time, and transfer the logs of standalone systems weekly.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to off-load interconnected systems in real time and standalone systems weekly.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the log records are being off-loaded, at a minimum of real time for interconnected systems and weekly for standalone systems.\n\nIf the application server is not meeting these requirements, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Information stored in one location is vulnerable to accidental or incidental deletion or alteration. Protecting log data is important during a forensic investigation to ensure investigators can track and understand what may have occurred. Off-loading should be set up as a scheduled task but can be configured to be run manually, if other processes during the off-loading are manual.\n\nOff-loading is a common process in information systems with limited log storage capacity.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71697",
            "V-57425"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001851"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "f058c8ae-839b-5828-bdec-263bf9a1130d",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: nginx/Istio emit access records to stdout, current API structured logging records event/method/path/source/status with timestamps, and Vector continuously aggregates pod/node records in Loki. This supports the specified timing/location/outcome/collection mechanics, not complete security-event or actor coverage.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-web] app-web\nnginx serves a static SPA at /usr/share/nginx/html on port 3000, with try_files fallback, stdout/stderr logs and no configured proxy_pass, CGI or WebDAV locations.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/web/nginx.conf\n\n[core-accesslog] core-accesslog\nIstio access logs go to stdout; path normalization merges slashes, proxies are held until ready, and forwarded certificate details are sanitized.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/values/base-istiod.yaml\n\n[logging] API logging\nCurrent HTTP logger includes service, request/trace IDs, event, remote address and response status, with secret redaction. Its common bindings do not add authenticated actor identity; other records/correlation must be checked.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/core/observability/logging.ts\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. nginx/Istio emit access records to stdout, current API structured logging records event/method/path/source/status with timestamps, and Vector continuously aggregates pod/node records in Loki. This supports the specified timing/location/outcome/collection mechanics, not complete security-event or actor coverage.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204834",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204834",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000516",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204834",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000516",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204834r1265191_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204834r1265191",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000516-AS-000237",
          "rule_title": "The application server must be configured in accordance with the security configuration settings based on DoW security configuration or implementation guidance, including STIGs, NSA configuration guides, CTOs, and DTMs.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to be in accordance with the security configuration settings based on DoW security configuration or implementation guidance, including STIGs, NSA configuration guides, CTOs, and DTMs.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the application server documentation and configuration to determine if the application server is configured in accordance with the security configuration settings based on DoW security configuration or implementation guidance, including STIGs, NSA configuration guides, CTOs, and DTMs.\n\nIf the application server is not configured in accordance with security configuration settings, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Configuring the application to implement organization-wide security implementation guides and security checklists ensures compliance with federal standards and establishes a common security baseline across DoW that reflects the most restrictive security posture consistent with operational requirements.\n\nConfiguration settings are the set of parameters that can be changed that affect the security posture and/or functionality of the system. Security-related parameters are those parameters impacting the security state of the application, including the parameters required to satisfy other security control requirements.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71775",
            "V-57499"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000366"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "47e5c086-4abf-5cef-852a-3968f89a8650",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The deliveredCore/workload design already implements the applicable hardeningmechanisms through restrictedPepradmission, isolatedmanagedlogging and centralizedSSO/PKI. Exact identified exceptions are separately tracked under their ownrules. Counting this general configuration-guidance rule as another undifferentiated implementationgap duplicates those findings; retain the componentbaseline/exceptionmapping as documentation.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-podsecurity] re-appserver-podsecurity\nAdmission restricts privileges/non-root/escalation/capabilities with explicitexemptions. This supplies secureapplication server operation and a preventionmechanism, but does not itself integrate Falcointrusionevents into accesscontrol.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[re-appserver-logging] re-appserver-logging\nVector collects pod/node records with namespace/app/container/host identifiers, acknowledged diskbuffers and Loki sinks, exports internal metrics and is monitored. It collects process/container identity independent of Horizonrequest actors.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-coreidentity] re-appserver-coreidentity\nCore 1.14 default enables OTP and X509, sets concurrent per-user limit0(unlimited), leaves inactivity workflow opt-in, includes explicit truststorepaths and optional alert enablement.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-auth] re-appserver-auth\nEvery protected request validates OIDC claims and single tenantgroup, resolves stable sub->localuser/tenant, and sets trusted userId/tenantId CLS context. Pending/no-role users are denied.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The deliveredCore/workload design already implements the applicable hardeningmechanisms through restrictedPepradmission, isolatedmanagedlogging and centralizedSSO/PKI. Exact identified exceptions are separately tracked under their ownrules. Counting this general configuration-guidance rule as another undifferentiated implementationgap duplicates those findings; retain the componentbaseline/exceptionmapping as documentation.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-240925",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-240925",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000416",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-240925",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000416",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-240925r1117186_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-240925r1117186",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000416-AS-000140",
          "rule_title": "The application server must implement NSA-approved cryptography to protect classified information in accordance with applicable federal laws, Executive Orders, directives, policies, regulations, and standards.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to utilize NSA-approved cryptography to protect classified information.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review application server documentation to verify that the application server is using NSA-approved cryptography to protect classified data and applications resident on the device.\n\nIf the application server is not using NSA-approved cryptography for classified data and applications, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Cryptography is only as strong as the encryption modules/algorithms employed to encrypt the data. Use of weak or untested encryption algorithms undermines the purposes of utilizing encryption to protect data.\n\nNSA has developed Type 1 algorithms for protecting classified information. The Committee on National Security Systems (CNSS) National Information Assurance Glossary (CNSS Instruction No. 4009) defines Type 1 products as:\n\n\"Cryptographic equipment, assembly or component classified or certified by NSA for encrypting and decrypting classified and sensitive national security information when appropriately keyed. Developed using established NSA business processes and containing NSA-approved algorithms \nare used to protect systems requiring the most stringent protection mechanisms.\" \n\nNSA-approved cryptography is required to be used for classified information system processing.\n\nThe application server must utilize NSA-approved encryption modules when protecting classified data. This means using AES and other approved encryption modules.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-71817",
            "V-57541"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002450"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "fd3c475b-3dbd-567b-bc29-e724810b73a7",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The deployment is classified, so this rule applies. Delivered application FIPS modules are identified, but the configured storage and transport encryption still need to meet the NSA-approved requirement. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work pending verification for this and similar classified-cryptography requirements. Confirm mission data scope and the actual inherited MPN storage/transport cryptographic boundary before assigning new implementation. Preserve this rule's exact cryptographic requirements; do not infer blanket classified approval from FIPS naming or duplicate platform protection inside Horizon.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Mission data/security authority and MPN cryptographic-boundary owner; Gallatin for application mapping/configuration residuals\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, confirm the mission data classification and protection requirements and identify the MPN/Gallatin responsibility boundary. Inspect actual module/solution/configuration evidence for the cryptographic tasks and data covered by this exact rule, credit supported inherited protection, and identify only uncovered application/configuration responsibilities. Check the encryption protecting application-server data against the NSA-approved requirement and configure any missing protection. Record the approved implementation and settings. Retain a controlled scope/protection mapping and revise the recommendation where coverage is supported.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-mission-nsa-system-solutions] re-mission-nsa-system-solutions\nNSA describes approved classified solutions as properly configured layered system designs, and cautions that partner/non-US implementations follow the applicable national authority. Ordinary product FIPS images are not themselves a registered CSfC system or evidence of an approved classified boundary.\nhttps://www.nsa.gov/Resources/Commercial-Solutions-for-Classified-Program/faq/\n\n[re-mission-nsa-registration] re-mission-nsa-registration\nNSA solution registration requires system diagrams, selected components, CONOPS and the relevant approved solution package/authority artifacts. This supports one boundary-level evidence package rather than an invented requirement to add independent classified encryption inside each application service.\nhttps://www.nsa.gov/Resources/Commercial-Solutions-for-Classified-Program/solution-registration/\n\n[re-mission-nsa-cp] re-mission-nsa-cp\nNSA publishes distinct classified data-in-transit and data-at-rest capability packages with architecture/component/configuration requirements; an approved storage or network boundary must be mapped to its own use case, not assumed from a TLS version or FIPS image label.\nhttps://www.nsa.gov/Resources/Commercial-Solutions-for-Classified-Program/Capability-Packages/\n\n[classified-crypto-definition] classified-crypto-definition\nNSA-approved cryptography includes an approved algorithm, an implementation approved for the relevant environment and supporting key management. A FIPS validation certificate by itself does not establish that full approval.\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/NSA_approved_cryptography",
          "finding_details": "The deployment is classified, so this rule applies. Delivered application FIPS modules are identified, but the configured storage and transport encryption still need to meet the NSA-approved requirement. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work pending verification for this and similar classified-cryptography requirements. Confirm mission data scope and the actual inherited MPN storage/transport cryptographic boundary before assigning new implementation. Preserve this rule's exact cryptographic requirements; do not infer blanket classified approval from FIPS naming or duplicate platform protection inside Horizon.\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, confirm the mission data classification and protection requirements and identify the MPN/Gallatin responsibility boundary. Inspect actual module/solution/configuration evidence for the cryptographic tasks and data covered by this exact rule, credit supported inherited protection, and identify only uncovered application/configuration responsibilities. Check the encryption protecting application-server data against the NSA-approved requirement and configure any missing protection. Record the approved implementation and settings. Retain a controlled scope/protection mapping and revise the recommendation where coverage is supported."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-263549",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-263549",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000705",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-263549",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000705",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-263549r981699_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-263549r981699",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000705-AS-000110",
          "rule_title": "The application server must disable accounts when the accounts are no longer associated to a user.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to disable accounts when the accounts are no longer associated to a user.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the application server disables accounts when the accounts are no longer associated to a user.\n\nIf the application server does not disable accounts when the accounts are no longer associated to a user, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Disabling expired, inactive, or otherwise anomalous accounts supports the concepts of least privilege and least functionality, which reduce the attack surface of the system.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-003628"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "69a6035f-8ecf-5c15-8a73-7f8cbb7cbdef",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual UDS provider centralizes accounts and exposes administration, but no authoritative departure/deassociation feed or enabled account cleanup workflow is selected in deliveredprofile. Existing userprovisioning creates/updates identities onlogin and APIofflineJWTverification does not itself establish disablement when directoryassociationends. Configure this in the provider/directory lifecycle integration. The selected human lifecycle policy and Gallatin offboarding execution remain; the provider mechanisms are already available.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Gallatin records its authoritative user-deassociation/offboarding source and uses the existing UDS user enabled-state/group-entitlement controls for the required disablement. Add an integration only if the chosen policy requires automation; do not duplicate account management in Horizon or await DU operations.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-auth] re-appserver-auth\nEvery protected request validates OIDC claims and single tenantgroup, resolves stable sub->localuser/tenant, and sets trusted userId/tenantId CLS context. Pending/no-role users are denied.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[re-appserver-coreidentity] re-appserver-coreidentity\nCore 1.14 default enables OTP and X509, sets concurrent per-user limit0(unlimited), leaves inactivity workflow opt-in, includes explicit truststorepaths and optional alert enablement.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-jwt] re-appserver-jwt\nProtectedAPI verifies JWTsignature/issuer/audience/expiry offline. There is no per-request session revocation check; immediate bearer credential invalidation is a residual beyond ending the IdP/browser interactive session.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.oidc-jwt.service.ts\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Actual UDS provider centralizes accounts and exposes administration, but no authoritative departure/deassociation feed or enabled account cleanup workflow is selected in deliveredprofile. Existing userprovisioning creates/updates identities onlogin and APIofflineJWTverification does not itself establish disablement when directoryassociationends. Configure this in the provider/directory lifecycle integration. The selected human lifecycle policy and Gallatin offboarding execution remain; the provider mechanisms are already available.\n\nNext action: Gallatin records its authoritative user-deassociation/offboarding source and uses the existing UDS user enabled-state/group-entitlement controls for the required disablement. Add an integration only if the chosen policy requires automation; do not duplicate account management in Horizon or await DU operations."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-263550",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-263550",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000795",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-263550",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000795",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-263550r981702_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-263550r981702",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000795-AS-000130",
          "rule_title": "The application server must alert organization-defined personnel or roles upon detection of unauthorized access, modification, or deletion of audit information.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to alert organization-defined personnel or roles upon detection of unauthorized access, modification, or deletion of audit information.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the application server alerts organization-defined personnel or roles upon detection of unauthorized access, modification, or deletion of audit information.\n\nIf the application server does not alert organization-defined personnel or roles upon detection of unauthorized access, modification, or deletion of audit information, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Audit information includes all information needed to successfully audit system activity, such as audit records, audit log settings, audit reports, and personally identifiable information. Audit logging tools are those programs and devices used to conduct system audit and logging activities. Protection of audit information focuses on technical protection and limits the ability to access and execute audit logging tools to authorized individuals. Physical protection of audit information is addressed by both media protection controls and physical and environmental protection controls.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-003831"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "28422128-40ed-52c7-8c59-42b30b821655",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. The dedicated UDS logging/monitoring service is the correct implementationboundary and supplies Vectorinternal metrics, storage/Prometheus alerts, Falco and optional identity rules. Baseline configuration nevertheless does not establish all required processingfailure/tampering events, the 75 percent audit capacity threshold or SA/ISSO receiver. This is sharedmonitoring configuration/routing, not a new API alert engine. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Identify detection sources for unauthorized access, modification and deletion of audit information. Configure missing detection/alert rules and route notifications to organization-defined personnel or roles. Test each required event type and recipient delivery; general uptime or account-change rules do not establish tamper coverage. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-logging] re-appserver-logging\nVector collects pod/node records with namespace/app/container/host identifiers, acknowledged diskbuffers and Loki sinks, exports internal metrics and is monitored. It collects process/container identity independent of Horizonrequest actors.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-alerts] re-appserver-alerts\nCore enables storage/workload/Prometheus defaultalerts and Alertmanager. This baseline does not configure a dedicated 75 percent audit storage threshold or actual SA/ISSO receiver.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/prometheus-stack/values/kube-prometheus-stack-values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-identityalerts] re-appserver-identityalerts\nIdentity realm/user/admin alert definitions exist behind detailedObservability.alerts.enabled; they do not automatically enable alerts or define operational receivers.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/keycloak-logging-alerting-rules.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-falco] re-appserver-falco\nFalco/Sidekick detects runtime behavior and sends events toLoki, but Alertmanager routing is commented and no direct automated permissionrevocation/responsetrigger is configured.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-core-falco-notify] mpn-core-falco-notify\nFalco sends structured events to Loki by default. falcosidekick.config.alertmanager.hostport/endpoint are documented but commented; Gallatin can enable the route to monitoring Alertmanager and configure receivers itself.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. The dedicated UDS logging/monitoring service is the correct implementationboundary and supplies Vectorinternal metrics, storage/Prometheus alerts, Falco and optional identity rules. Baseline configuration nevertheless does not establish all required processingfailure/tampering events, the 75 percent audit capacity threshold or SA/ISSO receiver. This is sharedmonitoring configuration/routing, not a new API alert engine. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nNext action: Identify detection sources for unauthorized access, modification and deletion of audit information. Configure missing detection/alert rules and route notifications to organization-defined personnel or roles. Test each required event type and recipient delivery; general uptime or account-change rules do not establish tamper coverage. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-263551",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-263551",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000820",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-263551",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000820",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-263551r981705_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-263551r981705",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000820-AS-000170",
          "rule_title": "The application server must implement multifactor authentication for local; network; and/or remote access to privileged accounts; and/or nonprivileged accounts such that one of the factors is provided by a device separate from the system gaining access.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to implement multifactor authentication for local; network; and/or remote access to privileged accounts; and/or nonprivileged accounts such that one of the factors is provided by a device separate from the system gaining access.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the application server implements multifactor authentication for local; network; and/or remote access to privileged accounts; and/or nonprivileged accounts such that one of the factors is provided by a device separate from the system gaining access.\n\nIf the application server does not implement multifactor authentication for local; network; and/or remote access to privileged accounts; and/or nonprivileged accounts such that one of the factors is provided by a device separate from the system gaining access, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The purpose of requiring a device separate from the system to which the user is attempting to gain access for one of the factors during multifactor authentication is to reduce the likelihood of compromising authenticators or credentials stored on the system. Adversaries may be able to compromise such authenticators or credentials and subsequently impersonate authorized users. Implementing one of the factors on a separate device (e.g., a hardware token), provides a greater strength of mechanism and an increased level of assurance in the authentication process.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004046"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "fc7866a2-50ae-53ce-811d-7a6179c17cd4",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Horizondelegates authentication to actual UDS provider. Core chart renders OTP true into REQUIRED MFA/OTP flow, and provides X509/CAC path through the separateadmin/SSOgateway. A separate device OTP/CACpath is implemented at the identity layer; Horizon API does not need to repeat MFA after trusted OIDC authentication. Role/device policy exceptions remain platformconfiguration conditions.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-coreidentity] re-appserver-coreidentity\nCore 1.14 default enables OTP and X509, sets concurrent per-user limit0(unlimited), leaves inactivity workflow opt-in, includes explicit truststorepaths and optional alert enablement.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-flowrender] re-appserver-flowrender\nChart maps OTP_ENABLED=true to OTP_FLOW_ENABLED=REQUIRED and MFA_FLOW_ENABLED=REQUIRED, and maps X509 auth to enabled alternate flow. Raw realm interpolation fallbacks alone are not the final rendered flow.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/secret-kc-realm.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-realm] re-appserver-realm\nRealm provides X509UPN/custom-attribute mapping, OCSP checking defaulttrue/failclosed, localCRLfalse, OTP browser flow, user/admin events, ten-minute idle. Consent required action defaultsdisabled; inactivity workflow disabled unless configured; sessionsunlimited default.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-appserver-identitygateway] re-appserver-identitygateway\nUDS routes privateKeycloak paths away from public tenantgateway, exposes admin access separately, strips user-supplied clientcertificate header and injects downstreamcert for delegated X509 handling.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Horizondelegates authentication to actual UDS provider. Core chart renders OTP true into REQUIRED MFA/OTP flow, and provides X509/CAC path through the separateadmin/SSOgateway. A separate device OTP/CACpath is implemented at the identity layer; Horizon API does not need to repeat MFA after trusted OIDC authentication. Role/device policy exceptions remain platformconfiguration conditions.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
        },
        {
