          "group_id": "V-263554",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000910",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-263554r981714_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-263554r981714",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000910-AS-000300",
          "rule_title": "The application server must include only approved trust anchors in trust stores or certificate stores managed by the organization.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to include only approved trust anchors in trust stores or certificate stores managed by the organization.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the application server includes only approved trust anchors in trust stores or certificate stores managed by the organization.\n\nIf the application server does not include only approved trust anchors in trust stores or certificate stores managed by the organization, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Public key infrastructure (PKI) certificates are certificates with visibility external to organizational systems and certificates related to the internal operations of systems, such as application-specific time services. In cryptographic systems with a hierarchical structure, a trust anchor is an authoritative source (i.e., a certificate authority) for which trust is assumed and not derived. A root certificate for a PKI system is an example of a trust anchor. A trust store or certificate store maintains a list of trusted root certificates.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004909"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "e626b34e-4300-5f32-86f1-6b9a7f689d98",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS identity config builds its certificate bundle from the official SHA-verified DoD-approvedexternalPKIchains, skips expiredcertificates and explicitlysyncs them into Keycloaktruststores. Core additional paths are declared UDSbundle and KubernetesserviceaccountCA for their respective platformpurposes. Source defines a controlled trust-anchor provisioning path. Organization-specific extraanchors/overrides remain checklistconditions.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-trust] re-appserver-trust\nImage trust builder defaults officialDoD approved externalPKI trust chains ZIP, requires SHA256 verification, converts .cer files, excludes configured certificate categories, and includes non-expired certificates. This is actual source provisioning, not a generic claim that trust is unknowable.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/truststore/ca-to-jks.sh\n\n[re-appserver-trustsync] re-appserver-trustsync\nIdentityconfig sync copies generated certs into Keycloak conf/truststores and installs realm/plugin/theme configuration. FIPS libraries are copied when configured.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/sync.sh\n\n[re-appserver-coreidentity] re-appserver-coreidentity\nCore 1.14 default enables OTP and X509, sets concurrent per-user limit0(unlimited), leaves inactivity workflow opt-in, includes explicit truststorepaths and optional alert enablement.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS identity config builds its certificate bundle from the official SHA-verified DoD-approvedexternalPKIchains, skips expiredcertificates and explicitlysyncs them into Keycloaktruststores. Core additional paths are declared UDSbundle and KubernetesserviceaccountCA for their respective platformpurposes. Source defines a controlled trust-anchor provisioning path. Organization-specific extraanchors/overrides remain checklistconditions.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
        },
