          "uuid": "d6631c1d-25e0-5817-8fab-f8b9b2b51c23",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Gateway/mesh private keys are supplied through restricted Kubernetes Secret/identity paths, not baked into the static application image; runtime containers are non-root/read-only and platform access is separated. If an HSM is organizationally required, that additional safeguard needs an explicit requirement.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-gateway] core-gateway\nGateway redirects HTTP to HTTPS and requires TLS 1.3 by default, optionally TLS 1.2; credentialName references Kubernetes TLS secrets.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/charts/uds-istio-config/templates/gateway.yaml\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[app-pods] app-pods\nCandidate service chart applies non-root/read-only/no-escalation contexts, Secret references, probes and resource settings. Rendered app pods corroborate those settings; some init containers lack explicit resources.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-service/templates/deployment.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Gateway/mesh private keys are supplied through restricted Kubernetes Secret/identity paths, not baked into the static application image; runtime containers are non-root/read-only and platform access is separated. If an HSM is organizationally required, that additional safeguard needs an explicit requirement.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-263556",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-263556",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000920",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-263556",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000920",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-263556r981720_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-263556r981720",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000920-AS-000320",
          "rule_title": "The application server must synchronize system clocks within and between systems or system components.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to synchronize system clocks within and between systems or system components.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the application server synchronizes system clocks within and between systems or system components.\n\nIf the application server does not synchronize system clocks within and between systems or system components, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Time synchronization of system clocks is essential for the correct execution of many system services, including identification and authentication processes that involve certificates and time-of-day restrictions as part of access control. Denial of service or failure to deny expired credentials may result without properly synchronized clocks within and between systems and system components. Time is commonly expressed in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC), a modern continuation of Greenwich Mean Time (GMT), or local time with an offset from UTC. The granularity of time measurements refers to the degree of synchronization between system clocks and reference clocks, such as clocks synchronizing within hundreds of milliseconds or tens of milliseconds. Organizations may define different time granularities for system components. Time service can be critical to other security capabilities such as access control and identification and authentication depending on the nature of the mechanisms used to support the capabilities.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004922"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "dc33b24d-e618-5fdc-9d52-fec05db22a47",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Horizon containers and UDS identity/logging containers use their nodes' clocks; the identity listener timestamps events in UTC with subsecond precision. Time synchronization belongs to the MPN node operating-system boundary, so no NTP daemon is required inside Horizon. Retain host synchronization inheritance with the component checklist.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the MPN node time-synchronization inheritance mapping; no application NTP implementation is recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-idlistener] re-appserver-idlistener\nActual UDS identity listener handles both UserEvent and AdminEvent, serializes original event fields plus event type and UTC timestamp, and prints JSON to stdout. Vector can collect the actual provider records directly; no bundled identity database forwarder is needed.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[re-appserver-logging] re-appserver-logging\nVector collects pod/node records with namespace/app/container/host identifiers, acknowledged diskbuffers and Loki sinks, exports internal metrics and is monitored. It collects process/container identity independent of Horizonrequest actors.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Horizon containers and UDS identity/logging containers use their nodes' clocks; the identity listener timestamps events in UTC with subsecond precision. Time synchronization belongs to the MPN node operating-system boundary, so no NTP daemon is required inside Horizon. Retain host synchronization inheritance with the component checklist.\n\nNext action: Retain the MPN node time-synchronization inheritance mapping; no application NTP implementation is recommended."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-278959",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-278959",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000009",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-278959",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000009",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-278959r1137588_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-278959r1137588",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000009-AS-000321",
          "rule_title": "The application server must dynamically associate security attributes with organization-defined subjects in accordance with organization-defined security policies as information is created and combined.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to dynamically associate security attributes with organization-defined subjects in accordance with organization-defined security policies as information is created and combined.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the application server is configured to dynamically associate security attributes with organization-defined subjects in accordance with organization-defined security policies as information is created and combined.\n\nIf the application server does not dynamically associate security attributes with organization-defined subjects in accordance with organization-defined security policies as information is created and combined, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If the application does not dynamically reconfigure the data security attributes as data is created and combined, there is the possibility that the security attributes will not correctly reflect the data with which they are associated.\n\nSecurity attributes are abstractions representing the basic properties or characteristics of an entity (e.g., subjects and objects) with respect to safeguarding information. These attributes are typically associated with internal data structures (e.g., data records, buffers, files) within the application and are used to enable the implementation of access control and flow control policies, reflect special dissemination, handling or distribution instructions, or support other aspects of the information security policy. Organizations define the security attributes of their data (e.g., classified, CUI).\n\nWhen data is created and/or combined, data security attributes defined by organizational policy must be dynamically created and/or updated to reflect the potential change in data sensitivity and characteristics. Dynamic association of security attributes is appropriate whenever the security characteristics of information changes over time. Security attributes may change, for example, due to information aggregation issues (i.e., the security characteristics of individual information elements are different from the combined elements), changes in individual access authorizations (i.e., privileges), and changes in the security category of information.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001424",
            "CCI-000366"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "0f088855-bc72-5627-ba2c-0aa2c9e7c733",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Current source has concrete security attributes: authenticatedOIDC sub+tenant groups map to trusted userId/tenantId, request context carries them during processing, OpenFGAderives object/relation/contextual tuples and database objects automatically attach tenant_id with tenant-consistent keys/RLS. These are dynamic subject/object attribute associations in process/transit/storage. The generic rule does not require inventing a classifiedlabel taxonomy when the product's selected securitypolicy is tenancy/roleisolation.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-auth] re-appserver-auth\nEvery protected request validates OIDC claims and single tenantgroup, resolves stable sub->localuser/tenant, and sets trusted userId/tenantId CLS context. Pending/no-role users are denied.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[re-appserver-authorization] re-appserver-authorization\nAuthorization derives user, objecttype/relation/objectId and contextual tuples, passes the pinned model to OpenFGA and uses a per-request cache keyed by user+object+relation+tuples.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/authorization/authorization.service.ts\n\n[re-appserver-tenantresolver] re-appserver-tenantresolver\nTenant-scoped authorization targets derive from authenticated tenantId CLS rather than caller-selected arbitrary tenancy.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/authorization/tenant-permission-context.resolver.ts\n\n[re-appserver-schemaattributes] re-appserver-schemaattributes\nBranch records automatically derive tenant_id from trusted app.tenant_id context; composite ancestry/tenantkeys and user audit fields preserve objectsecurity context. Application tenancy is an actual security attribute, not merely an arbitrary label.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/branches.pg.hcl",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Current source has concrete security attributes: authenticatedOIDC sub+tenant groups map to trusted userId/tenantId, request context carries them during processing, OpenFGAderives object/relation/contextual tuples and database objects automatically attach tenant_id with tenant-consistent keys/RLS. These are dynamic subject/object attribute associations in process/transit/storage. The generic rule does not require inventing a classifiedlabel taxonomy when the product's selected securitypolicy is tenancy/roleisolation.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
        },
