          "group_id": "V-278960",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000334",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-278960r1137591_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-278960r1137591",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000334-AS-000322",
          "rule_title": "The application server must uniquely identify and authenticate source by organization, system, application, and/or individual for information transfer.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to uniquely identify and authenticate source by organization, system, application, and/or individual for information transfer.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the application server is configured to uniquely identify and authenticate source by organization, system, application, and/or individual for information transfer.\n\nIf the application server does not uniquely identify and authenticate source by organization, system, application, and/or individual for information transfer, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Attribution is a critical component of a security concept of operations. The ability to identify source and destination points for information flowing in information systems, allows the forensic reconstruction of events when required, and encourages policy compliance by attributing policy violations to specific organizations/individuals. Successful domain authentication requires that information system labels distinguish among systems, organizations, and individuals involved in preparing, sending, receiving, or disseminating information.\n\nThis requirement applies to applications that transfer information between different security domains (e.g., multilevel security).\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002205",
            "CCI-000366"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "9f012d75-9aa6-5158-a36b-f7c8a2e3d0fc",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: API validates JWT signature/issuer/audience/expiry and establishes tenant/user context; NATS identities and UDS mesh service identities cover process-level sources. This supports identification of data-transfer callers in the existing application path.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[authz] Application access control\nAPI guard validates identity and sets tenant/user context; permission guard invokes a permission enforcer when route metadata is present. This is source support, not complete route/tenant coverage.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. API validates JWT signature/issuer/audience/expiry and establishes tenant/user context; NATS identities and UDS mesh service identities cover process-level sources. This supports identification of data-transfer callers in the existing application path.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-278961",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-278961",
              "title": "SRG-APP-001040",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-278961",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-001040",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-278961r1227257_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-278961r1227257",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-001040-AS-000324",
          "rule_title": "The application server must enforce attribute-based control access over defined subjects and objects based upon organization-defined attributes to assume access permissions.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to enforce attribute-based control access over defined subjects and objects based upon organization-defined attributes to assume access permissions.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the application server is configured enforce attribute-based control access over defined subjects and objects based upon organization-defined attributes to assume access permissions.\n\nIf the application server does not enforce attribute-based control access over defined subjects and objects based upon organization-defined attributes to assume access permissions, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Attribute-based access control is an access control policy that restricts system access to authorized users based on specified organizational attributes (e.g., job function, identity), action attributes (e.g., read, write, delete), environmental attributes (e.g., time of day, location), and resource attributes (e.g., classification of a document). Organizations can create rules based on attributes and the authorizations (i.e., privileges) to perform needed operations on the systems associated with organization-defined attributes and rules. When users are assigned to attributes defined in attribute-based access control policies or rules, they can be provisioned to a system with the appropriate privileges or dynamically granted access to a protected resource.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-003652",
            "CCI-000366"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "61d27077-d534-5359-9411-5610e18214ee",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Authorization actually evaluates subject user, relation/permission, target object and authenticatedtenant/contextual tuples using pinned OpenFGA model; tenant RLS adds a databaseboundary. This is attribute/context-based subject-object enforcement even though the implementation uses relationshiptuples and roles rather than a component namedABAC. Do not count absence of that label as missingauthorization.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-auth] re-appserver-auth\nEvery protected request validates OIDC claims and single tenantgroup, resolves stable sub->localuser/tenant, and sets trusted userId/tenantId CLS context. Pending/no-role users are denied.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[re-appserver-authorization] re-appserver-authorization\nAuthorization derives user, objecttype/relation/objectId and contextual tuples, passes the pinned model to OpenFGA and uses a per-request cache keyed by user+object+relation+tuples.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/authorization/authorization.service.ts\n\n[re-appserver-tenantresolver] re-appserver-tenantresolver\nTenant-scoped authorization targets derive from authenticated tenantId CLS rather than caller-selected arbitrary tenancy.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/authorization/tenant-permission-context.resolver.ts\n\n[re-appserver-schemaattributes] re-appserver-schemaattributes\nBranch records automatically derive tenant_id from trusted app.tenant_id context; composite ancestry/tenantkeys and user audit fields preserve objectsecurity context. Application tenancy is an actual security attribute, not merely an arbitrary label.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/branches.pg.hcl",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Authorization actually evaluates subject user, relation/permission, target object and authenticatedtenant/contextual tuples using pinned OpenFGA model; tenant RLS adds a databaseboundary. This is attribute/context-based subject-object enforcement even though the implementation uses relationshiptuples and roles rather than a component namedABAC. Do not count absence of that label as missingauthorization.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
        },
