            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-278962",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-001045",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-278962r1227258_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-278962r1227258",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-001045-AS-000325",
          "rule_title": "The application server must attach data tags containing organization-defined authorized processing to organization-defined elements of personally identifiable information.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to attach data tags containing organization-defined authorized processing to organization-defined elements of personally identifiable information.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the application server is configured to attach data tags containing organization-defined authorized processing to organization-defined elements of personally identifiable information.\n\nIf the application server does not attach data tags containing organization-defined authorized processing to organization-defined elements of personally identifiable information, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Data tags support the tracking and enforcement of authorized processing by conveying the types of processing that are authorized along with the relevant elements of personally identifiable information throughout the system. Data tags may also support the use of automated tools.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004544",
            "CCI-000366"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "cd523cec-aaec-57bd-a1fa-4f6c8c82379a",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The candidate PII elements are identifiable in the delivered identity schema, and file/chunk tag propagation already exists. The missing authorized processing association is a bounded policy/configuration integration, not a reason to invent a new general tagging subsystem. Tenant_id and security classification do not encode this semantic purpose by themselves.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin privacy/security lead + mission data owner; API/ingestion maintainers\n\nNext action: Have the privacy/mission data owner approve the PII element/purpose matrix below. Reuse files.tags and propagated chunk metadata for document-level tags such as privacy.profile, privacy.authorized_processing and privacy.processing_purpose. Attach a versioned schema-level privacy profile to account identity fields in the API/schema data dictionary; implement and validate only the missing association/propagation needed by the approved scope. If field-level tags are required, add narrow schema metadata or a field-to-policy mapping, not a wholesale per-row schema rewrite. Verify the exact data-tag interpretation with the assessor before treating a standalone document as an attached tag.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-mission-user-fields] re-mission-user-fields\nIdentity schemas expose email, display/given/family names and preferred username with tenant identity and role context. These are concrete candidate PII elements; the schema lacks defined authorized-processing/purpose metadata.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/users/users.schema.ts\n\n[re-mission-upload-tags] re-mission-upload-tags\nUploadMetadataSchema accepts classification and arbitrary string-key/string-value tags; file PUT/PATCH/output schemas preserve tags. Existing object metadata can carry approved privacy-purpose labels without introducing a parallel per-object metadata table.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/files/files.schema.ts\n\n[re-mission-ingestion-tags] re-mission-ingestion-tags\nIngestion copies caller tags into resolved_tags and passes classification/tags to persisted jobs and downstream chunk preparation. This supports propagation of approved metadata but does not itself define legal processing purposes or field-level PII scope.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/workers/artifact-ingestion/artifact_ingestion/processor.py\n\n[re-docs-classification-policy] re-docs-classification-policy\nCompany data-classification guidance exists; contract-specific applicable CUI categories and approved systems by tier remain explicit decision inputs. This is distinct from source-level classification enums or enforcement mechanisms and does not justify claiming no taxonomy exists.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/gallatin-company-policies/blob/e5b6ec5a2862cc3880c2ccc011d57698f14611e9/POL-CUI-data-classification.md",
          "finding_details": "The candidate PII elements are identifiable in the delivered identity schema, and file/chunk tag propagation already exists. The missing authorized processing association is a bounded policy/configuration integration, not a reason to invent a new general tagging subsystem. Tenant_id and security classification do not encode this semantic purpose by themselves.\n\nNext action: Have the privacy/mission data owner approve the PII element/purpose matrix below. Reuse files.tags and propagated chunk metadata for document-level tags such as privacy.profile, privacy.authorized_processing and privacy.processing_purpose. Attach a versioned schema-level privacy profile to account identity fields in the API/schema data dictionary; implement and validate only the missing association/propagation needed by the approved scope. If field-level tags are required, add narrow schema metadata or a field-to-policy mapping, not a wholesale per-row schema rewrite. Verify the exact data-tag interpretation with the assessor before treating a standalone document as an attached tag."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-278963",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-278963",
