          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "25448068-708a-5860-bdaf-7922604aa90b",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Wholeproduct review credits existing Pepr prevention/networkpolicies and Falco detection, but code routes Falco events toLoki only and does not connect detectedintrusions to automatic access-control response. Independent prevention and detection are not the specified automated integration. Resolve through platform responder/integration or accepted policytailoring, not an API-specificIDSrewrite.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Configure an accepted Falco/security event-to-access-control response or record approvedtailoring for the exactautomated integration.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-falco] re-appserver-falco\nFalco/Sidekick detects runtime behavior and sends events toLoki, but Alertmanager routing is commented and no direct automated permissionrevocation/responsetrigger is configured.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-appserver-podsecurity] re-appserver-podsecurity\nAdmission restricts privileges/non-root/escalation/capabilities with explicitexemptions. This supplies secureapplication server operation and a preventionmechanism, but does not itself integrate Falcointrusionevents into accesscontrol.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts",
          "finding_details": "Wholeproduct review credits existing Pepr prevention/networkpolicies and Falco detection, but code routes Falco events toLoki only and does not connect detectedintrusions to automatic access-control response. Independent prevention and detection are not the specified automated integration. Resolve through platform responder/integration or accepted policytailoring, not an API-specificIDSrewrite.\n\nNext action: Configure an accepted Falco/security event-to-access-control response or record approvedtailoring for the exactautomated integration."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-278965",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-278965",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000298",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-278965",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000298",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-278965r1137606_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-278965r1137606",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000298-AS-000190",
          "rule_title": "The application server must dynamically associate security attributes with organization-defined objects in accordance with organization-defined security policies as information is created and combined.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to dynamically associate security attributes with organization-defined objects in accordance with organization-defined security policies as information is created and combined.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the application server is configured to dynamically associate security attributes with organization-defined objects in accordance with organization-defined security policies as information is created and combined.\n\nIf the application server does not dynamically associate security attributes with organization-defined objects in accordance with organization-defined security policies as information is created and combined, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If the application does not dynamically reconfigure the data security attributes as data is created and combined, there is the possibility that the security attributes will not correctly reflect the data with which they are associated.\n\nSecurity attributes are abstractions representing the basic properties or characteristics of an entity (e.g., subjects and objects) with respect to safeguarding information. These attributes are typically associated with internal data structures (e.g., data records, buffers, and files) within the application and are used to enable the implementation of access control and flow control policies, reflect special dissemination, handling or distribution instructions, or support other aspects of the information security policy. Organizations define the security attributes of their data (e.g., classified and CUI).\n\nWhen data is created and/or combined, data security attributes defined by organizational policy must be dynamically created and/or updated to reflect the potential change in data sensitivity and characteristics. Dynamic association of security attributes is appropriate whenever the security characteristics of information changes over time. Security attributes may change due to information aggregation issues (i.e., the security characteristics of individual information elements are different from the combined elements); changes in individual access authorizations (i.e., privileges); or changes in the security category of information.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002272",
            "CCI-000366"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "188c07b3-c762-5083-beed-9dcd54aba18e",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Current source has concrete security attributes: authenticatedOIDC sub+tenant groups map to trusted userId/tenantId, request context carries them during processing, OpenFGAderives object/relation/contextual tuples and database objects automatically attach tenant_id with tenant-consistent keys/RLS. These are dynamic subject/object attribute associations in process/transit/storage. The generic rule does not require inventing a classifiedlabel taxonomy when the product's selected securitypolicy is tenancy/roleisolation.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-appserver-release] re-appserver-release\nThe successful UDS push packaged2026.45.4-uds.1 records trusted Horizonb84b9af/build19710, packagecommitd528b7f, UDS Core 1.14.0 and platformContract identity.mode=uds, issuerhttps://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. The actual delivered profile omits bundled Keycloak. Source-default bundled realm is not the delivered identity boundary.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-appserver-auth] re-appserver-auth\nEvery protected request validates OIDC claims and single tenantgroup, resolves stable sub->localuser/tenant, and sets trusted userId/tenantId CLS context. Pending/no-role users are denied.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[re-appserver-authorization] re-appserver-authorization\nAuthorization derives user, objecttype/relation/objectId and contextual tuples, passes the pinned model to OpenFGA and uses a per-request cache keyed by user+object+relation+tuples.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/authorization/authorization.service.ts\n\n[re-appserver-tenantresolver] re-appserver-tenantresolver\nTenant-scoped authorization targets derive from authenticated tenantId CLS rather than caller-selected arbitrary tenancy.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/authorization/tenant-permission-context.resolver.ts\n\n[re-appserver-schemaattributes] re-appserver-schemaattributes\nBranch records automatically derive tenant_id from trusted app.tenant_id context; composite ancestry/tenantkeys and user audit fields preserve objectsecurity context. Application tenancy is an actual security attribute, not merely an arbitrary label.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/branches.pg.hcl",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Current source has concrete security attributes: authenticatedOIDC sub+tenant groups map to trusted userId/tenantId, request context carries them during processing, OpenFGAderives object/relation/contextual tuples and database objects automatically attach tenant_id with tenant-consistent keys/RLS. These are dynamic subject/object attribute associations in process/transit/storage. The generic rule does not require inventing a classifiedlabel taxonomy when the product's selected securitypolicy is tenancy/roleisolation.\n\nNext action: Retain the delivered-release source mapping and component boundary in the checklist; no new application implementation is recommended for this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-278966",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-278966",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000329",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-278966",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000329",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-278966r1137609_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-278966r1137609",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000329-AS-000200",
          "rule_title": "The application server must enforce a role-based access control (RBAC) policy over defined subjects and objects.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the application server to enforce an RBAC policy over defined subjects and objects.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the application server is configured to enforce an RBAC policy over defined subjects and objects.\n\nIf the application server does not enforce an RBAC policy over defined subjects and objects, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Application_Server_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "RBAC is an access control policy that restricts information system access to authorized users. Without these security policies, access control and enforcement mechanisms will not prevent unauthorized access.\n\nOrganizations can create specific roles based on job functions and the authorizations (i.e., privileges) to perform needed operations on organizational information systems associated with the organization-defined roles. When users are assigned to the organizational roles, they inherit the authorizations or privileges defined for those roles. RBAC simplifies privilege administration for organizations because privileges are not assigned directly to every user (which can be a significant number of individuals for mid- to large-size organizations) but are instead acquired through role assignments. RBAC can be implemented either as a mandatory or discretionary form of access control.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002169",
            "CCI-000366"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2900",
          "uuid": "e80d2d75-9cbf-53c8-86a1-52adeb016af9",
          "stig_uuid": "3817652a-d733-5ebb-b7dc-03a0b4466071",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Current global authentication plus per-route permission enforcement consults the authorization model and fails closed if user/target/authorization resolution fails. Schema/route coverage checks constrain omitted metadata; preserve negative role/tenant tests for the release.\n\nComponents: Delivered Horizon API, workers, chat, ingestion and storage; delegated UDS identity/gateway/mesh\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[authz] Application access control\nAPI guard validates identity and sets tenant/user context; permission guard invokes a permission enforcer when route metadata is present. This is source support, not complete route/tenant coverage.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[app-enforcer] app-enforcer\nPermission enforcement resolves user/target and fails closed on missing authorization or unexpected resolution errors. Individual route metadata remains part of coverage.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/core/authorization/permission-enforcer.service.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Current global authentication plus per-route permission enforcement consults the authorization model and fails closed if user/target/authorization resolution fails. Schema/route coverage checks constrain omitted metadata; preserve negative role/tenant tests for the release.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-278967",
          "group_tree": [
